LM.SEARCH_INDEX = {"version":1,"records":[{"id":"page:a1-grammar-alphabet-pronunciation","group":"pages","title":"Alphabet, pronunciation and stress","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Spanish is read the way it is written. Learn the few letters that differ from English, where the stress falls, and ¿ ¡.","text":"Spanish spelling is regular: once you know the letters, you can read any word aloud and usually write a word you hear. Compare English though, through and tough. Spanish has five clear vowels, a few letters that differ from English, and simple stress rules. How it works Vowels. There are five, and each has one short, clear sound. They never change into \"uh\" as in English banana. Letter Sounds like Example a a in father casa (house) e e in bed mesa (table) i ee in see, but short sí (yes) o o in go, but short, without the w glide at the end ocho (eight) u oo in food, short uno (one) Letters that differ from English. Letter How to say it Example h silent hola, hotel j, and g before e / i a breathy sound from the throat, like a strong h José, gente ñ ny as in canyon España, mañana ll, y y as in yes (in some regions closer to j in jam; in Argentina and Uruguay like sh) calle, yo r between vowels one quick tap, like tt in American better pero (but) rr, and r at the start of a word or after n, l, s a rolled r perro (dog), rojo, Enrique b, v the same sound vaso, bar c before e / i, z th in think in most of Spain; s in Latin America, the Canary Islands and parts of southern Spain cine, zona c before a / o / u, g before a / o / u k; g in go casa, gracias qu k (the u is silent) queso Stress. Every word has one strong syllable. Three rules find it: A word ending in a vowel, n or s is stressed on the second-to-last syllable: CA-sa, HA-blan, LU-nes. A word ending in any other consonant is stressed on the last syllable: ho-TEL, ciu-DAD. A written accent breaks the rules and shows the stressed syllable: ca-FÉ, te-LÉ-fo-no, Jo-SÉ. Questions and exclamations have a mark at the start, upside down: ¿Cómo te llamas? ¡Hola! When to use it Reading a new word. Say each letter, then put the stress where the rules say. Spelling your name. People will ask ¿Cómo se escribe? (How do you spell it?). The letter names: a, be, ce, de, e, efe, ge, hache, i, jota, ka, ele, eme, ene, eñe, o, pe, cu, erre, ese, te, u, uve (Spain) / ve (Latin America), uve doble (Spain) / doble ve or doble u (Latin America), equis, ye, zeta. Writing. A missing accent or ñ can change the meaning: papa (potato, Latin America) and papá (dad); ano (anus) and año (year). Examples Spanish English hola hello (the h is silent) España Spain la calle the street el perro the dog (rolled rr) pero but (one tap) el teléfono the telephone (te-LÉ-fo-no) ¿Cómo te llamas? What's your name? Good to know ch sounds as in church: chocolate. Common mistakes ✗ Como te llamas? → ✓ ¿Cómo te llamas? — a question needs ¿ at the start, and question words have an accent. ✗ Espana → ✓ España — n and ñ are two different letters. ✗ pero (for \"dog\") → ✓ perro — one r is a tap; rr is a roll, and it is a different word. ✗ Jose → ✓ José — the name is stressed on the last syllable, and the accent shows it. Practice Where is the stress? Write the stressed syllable. mesa hotel café hablan Write the word correctly. Espana (Spain) el pero (the dog) Como te llamas? (What's your name?) ola (hello) Answers ME-sa (ends in a vowel) ho-TEL (ends in l) ca-FÉ (written accent) HA-blan (ends in n) España el perro ¿Cómo te llamas? hola","level":"A1","category":"Grammar","route":"#/a1/grammar-alphabet-pronunciation"},{"id":"page:a1-functions-greetings-introductions","group":"pages","title":"Greet people, say goodbye and introduce yourself","headings":["Key phrases","Dialogue","Culture note","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Say hello at any time of day, give your name, ask someone's name, react politely and say goodbye.","text":"These are the first sentences you will use, and you can learn them as fixed phrases before you know the grammar inside them. One difference from English matters from day one: Spanish has two ways to say \"you\". Use tú with friends, family, children and people your age; use usted with older people, strangers in formal situations, and customers or senior people at work. Many phrases below come in both versions. Key phrases Saying hello Spanish English Hola. Hi. / Hello. (any time, any person) Buenos días. Good morning. (until noon in Latin America; until lunch, about 2 p.m., in Spain) Buenas tardes. Good afternoon. / Good evening. (until it gets dark) Buenas noches. Good evening. / Good night. (after dark, also to say goodbye) ¿Qué tal? How's it going? (very common, friendly) ¿Cómo estás? / ¿Cómo está usted? How are you? (informal / formal) Bien, gracias. ¿Y tú? / ¿Y usted? Fine, thanks. And you? Introducing yourself and others Spanish English Me llamo Ana. My name is Ana. Soy Ana. I'm Ana. ¿Cómo te llamas? What's your name? (informal) ¿Cómo se llama usted? What's your name? (formal) Este es Pedro. / Esta es Lucía. This is Pedro. / This is Lucía. Mucho gusto. Nice to meet you. Encantado. / Encantada. Pleased to meet you. (a man says encantado, a woman encantada) Igualmente. Likewise. Saying goodbye Spanish English Adiós. Goodbye. Hasta luego. See you later. (also for \"bye\" in a shop) Hasta mañana. See you tomorrow. Nos vemos. See you. (informal) Chao. Bye. (informal, common in Latin America) Dialogue At a language exchange, two people meet. Marta: ¡Hola! Me llamo Marta. ¿Y tú? (Hi! My name is Marta. And you?) Tom: Hola, Marta. Soy Tom. (Hi, Marta. I'm Tom.) Marta: Mucho gusto, Tom. (Nice to meet you, Tom.) Tom: Igualmente. ¿Qué tal? (Likewise. How's it going?) Marta: Muy bien, gracias. Mira, esta es Elena. (Very well, thanks. Look, this is Elena.) Elena: Hola, Tom. Encantada. (Hi, Tom. Pleased to meet you.) Tom: Encantado, Elena. (Pleased to meet you, Elena.) Marta: Bueno, hasta luego. (OK, see you later.) Tom and Elena: ¡Hasta luego! (See you later!) Culture note In Spain friends and new acquaintances often greet with two kisses, one on each cheek; in much of Latin America one kiss is usual. Men usually shake hands with each other. Buenas on its own is a friendly short form of buenos días or buenas tardes. When in doubt, start with usted. People will tell you if they prefer tú: Puedes tutearme (\"you can say tú to me\"). Common mistakes ✗ Buenos tardes. → ✓ Buenas tardes. — tarde and noche are feminine words, so it is buenas; only días takes buenos. ✗ Me llamo es Ana. → ✓ Me llamo Ana. — me llamo already means \"my name is\"; there is no extra verb. ✗ Encantado (said by a woman) → ✓ Encantada — the word agrees with the person speaking. ✗ ¿Cómo te llamas? (to an older stranger at an office) → ✓ ¿Cómo se llama usted? — use the formal version with people you would call \"sir\" or \"madam\". ✗ Bien, y tú? → ✓ Bien, ¿y tú? — a question inside a sentence still opens with ¿. Practice Choose the right greeting. It is 9 a.m. You walk into a bakery: (Buenos días / Buenas noches) It is 4 p.m.: (Buenas tardes / Buenos tardes) Complete the dialogue. —Hola, me Carlos. —Mucho gusto. Yo soy Rosa. —Encantada. —. (Likewise.) —¿Qué tal? —Bien, gracias. ¿Y ? (to a friend) Translate into Spanish. What's your name? (to your teacher's grandmother) See you tomorrow! Pleased to meet you. (said by a woman) Answers Buenos días Buenas tardes llamo Igualmente tú ¿Cómo se llama usted? / ¿Cómo se llama? ¡Hasta mañana! Encantada. / Mucho gusto.","level":"A1","category":"Communication","route":"#/a1/functions-greetings-introductions"},{"id":"page:a1-grammar-subject-pronouns","group":"pages","title":"Subject pronouns; tú vs usted","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"I, you, he, she, we, they in Spanish; four words for \"you\"; and why Spanish often leaves the pronoun out.","text":"Subject pronouns are the words for \"I\", \"you\", \"she\" and so on. Spanish has more of them than English, for two reasons. First, \"you\" has a friendly form and a polite form, and a singular and a plural. Second, \"we\" and \"they\" show whether the group is all female. But Spanish uses them much less, because the verb ending already shows who is meant. How it works English Spanish Notes I yo no capital letter you (one friend) tú with an accent; tu without it means \"your\" he / she él / ella él with an accent; el without it means \"the\" you (one person, formal) usted often written Ud. we nosotros / nosotras nosotras: only women you (a group of friends) (Spain) vosotros / vosotras vosotras: only women they ellos / ellas ellas: only women you (a group) ustedes formal in Spain; all groups in Latin America. Often Uds. A mixed group takes the masculine form: Ana and Pedro are ellos; Ana, Rosa and Pedro are still ellos. Usted and ustedes mean \"you\", but they take the same verb form as él and ellos: ¿Cómo está usted? is \"How are you?\" There is no word for \"it\" as a subject: Es grande. (It is big.) When to use it Choose tú or usted. Tú for friends, family, children and people your age in relaxed settings. Usted for older people, customers, officials and anyone you would call \"sir\" or \"madam\". To a friend: ¿Y tú? To your new boss: ¿Y usted? Talk to a group. In Spain, vosotros for friends and ustedes for formal groups. In Latin America, ustedes for every group. Both are correct; use the one people use where you are. Leave the pronoun out. In a normal sentence the verb is enough: Me llamo Ana (not Yo me llamo Ana every time). Use the pronoun for contrast or emphasis. Yo me llamo Ana y él se llama Pedro. (I'm Ana, and he is Pedro.) —¿Quién es Marta? —Yo. (Me.) Examples Spanish English —Bien, gracias. ¿Y tú? Fine, thanks. And you? —Muy bien. ¿Y usted, señora? Very well. And you, madam? Él se llama Luis y ella se llama Rosa. He's called Luis and she's called Rosa. ¿Cómo se llama usted? What's your name? (formal) —¿Y vosotros? —Nosotros, bien. (Spain) \"And you guys?\" \"We're fine.\" —¿Y ustedes? —Nosotras, muy bien. \"And you?\" \"We're very well.\" (a group of women) Mucho gusto. Yo soy Pablo. Nice to meet you. I'm Pablo. Good to know In Argentina, Uruguay, Paraguay and much of Central America (and in parts of other countries) people say vos instead of tú with different verb forms (vos sos, vos tenés). You will understand it from context; this course uses tú. Common mistakes ✗ Yo me llamo Ana. Yo soy de Lima. Yo soy profesora. → ✓ Me llamo Ana. Soy de Lima. Soy profesora. — repeating yo in every sentence sounds unnatural. ✗ Usted te llamas Pedro. → ✓ Usted se llama Pedro. — usted takes the forms of él, not of tú. ✗ Ellos (for Ana and María) → ✓ Ellas — a group of only women is ellas, and nosotras for \"we\". ✗ Él es grande. (about a table) → ✓ Es grande. — there is no subject pronoun for \"it\". ✗ tu (for \"you\") → ✓ tú — without the accent, tu means \"your\". Practice Which pronoun? Write it in Spanish. you, to your best friend you, to an old man you meet in a museum they (Elena and Sofía) we (you and your brother; you are a woman) Translate into Spanish. And you? (to a group of friends in Mexico) And you? (to a group of friends in Madrid) He's called Pablo. What's your name? (to a hotel receptionist) Answers tú usted ellas nosotros ¿Y ustedes? ¿Y vosotros? / ¿Y vosotras? (¿Y ustedes? is also understood) Se llama Pablo. / Él se llama Pablo. ¿Cómo se llama usted? / ¿Cómo se llama?","level":"A1","category":"Grammar","route":"#/a1/grammar-subject-pronouns"},{"id":"page:a1-verbs-ser","group":"pages","title":"ser: identity, origin and profession","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"The first \"to be\": say who you are, where you are from, what you do and what someone is like.","text":"English has one verb \"to be\". Spanish has two, ser and estar, and they are not interchangeable. This page is about ser, the verb for what something is: a name, a nationality, a job, a lasting quality. You will meet estar (where something is, how someone feels) in unit 3. Ser is irregular, so learn its six forms by heart. How it works Person ser English yo soy I am tú eres you are él, ella, usted es he / she is, you are (formal) nosotros, nosotras somos we are vosotros, vosotras (Spain) sois you are (plural) ellos, ellas, ustedes son they are, you are (plural) Negative: put no before the verb: No soy de aquí. (I'm not from here.) Question: the same words, with ¿ ? and rising intonation: ¿Eres de México? (Are you from Mexico?) Origin: ser + de + place: Soy de Chile. Jobs: no un / una before the job: Es médico. (He is a doctor.) When to use it Name and identity. Soy Marta. (I'm Marta.) Es mi amigo. (He is my friend.) Origin and nationality. Somos de Colombia. (We're from Colombia.) Es español. (He is Spanish.) Nationalities are written with a small letter. Job. Soy profesor. (I'm a teacher; a woman says profesora.) Son estudiantes. (They are students.) What someone or something is like (a lasting quality). Es inteligente. (She is smart.) Eres muy amable. (You are very kind.) Day and date. Hoy es lunes. (Today is Monday.) Examples Spanish English Soy Daniel. Soy de Canadá. I'm Daniel. I'm from Canada. ¿Eres estudiante? Are you a student? Mi madre es médica. My mother is a doctor. No somos de aquí. We're not from here. ¿De dónde es usted? Where are you from? (formal) Estos son Ana y Luis. Son de Madrid. This is Ana and Luis. They're from Madrid. Mi profesora es muy simpática. My teacher is very nice. ¿Sois hermanos? (Spain) Are you brothers? / Are you brother and sister? Good to know Es can mean \"he is\", \"she is\", \"it is\" and \"you are\" (formal). The context tells you which; add él, ella or usted only if it is not clear. With a job plus a description, the article comes back: Es un médico muy bueno. (He is a very good doctor.) For age Spanish uses tener, not ser: Tengo 25 años. See . Common mistakes ✗ Soy un profesor. → ✓ Soy profesor. — no un / una before a job on its own. ✗ Soy 20 años. → ✓ Tengo 20 años. — age is \"having years\" in Spanish. ✗ Yo es de Chile. → ✓ Yo soy de Chile. — each person has its own form: yo soy, él es. ✗ Es en Madrid. (where a person is) → ✓ Está en Madrid. — location of people and things takes estar; ser de is for origin (events are different; you will see them later). Practice Complete with the right form of ser. Yo de Perú. ¿Tú profesora? Nosotros amigos. Ellas de Italia. Usted muy amable. Translate into Spanish. I'm not from here. She is a nurse. Where are you from? (to a friend) Answers soy eres somos son es No soy de aquí. Es enfermera. / Ella es enfermera. ¿De dónde eres? / ¿De dónde eres tú?","level":"A1","category":"Verbs: tenses and moods","route":"#/a1/verbs-ser"},{"id":"page:a1-vocabulary-numbers-days-months","group":"pages","title":"Numbers 0–100, days of the week, months","headings":["Words","Word partners","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Count to one hundred, name the days and months, and say \"on Monday\" and \"in July\" the Spanish way.","text":"Numbers, days and months are everywhere: prices, phone numbers, plans. Two things surprise English speakers. Days and months are written with a small letter in Spanish. And you say \"on Monday\" without any word for \"on\": just el lunes. Words Numbers 0–20 Spanish English cero, uno, dos, tres, cuatro, cinco 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 seis, siete, ocho, nueve, diez 6, 7, 8, 9, 10 once, doce, trece, catorce, quince 11, 12, 13, 14, 15 dieciséis, diecisiete, dieciocho, diecinueve, veinte 16, 17, 18, 19, 20 Numbers 21–100 Spanish English veintiuno, veintidós, veintitrés 21, 22, 23 veinticuatro … veintinueve 24 … 29 treinta, treinta y uno, treinta y dos 30, 31, 32 cuarenta, cincuenta, sesenta 40, 50, 60 setenta, ochenta, noventa 70, 80, 90 noventa y nueve 99 cien 100 16–29 are one word: diecisiete, veintiocho. From 31 they are three words with y: cuarenta y cinco. Watch the accents: dieciséis, veintidós, veintitrés, veintiséis. Uno becomes un before a masculine noun and una before a feminine one: un euro, una persona, veintiún años. Days of the week (the week starts on Monday) Spanish English el lunes Monday el martes Tuesday el miércoles Wednesday el jueves Thursday el viernes Friday el sábado Saturday el domingo Sunday el fin de semana the weekend Months Spanish English enero, febrero, marzo January, February, March abril, mayo, junio April, May, June julio, agosto, septiembre July, August, September octubre, noviembre, diciembre October, November, December Word partners Spanish English Hoy es martes. Today is Tuesday. el lunes on Monday (this coming Monday) los lunes on Mondays (every Monday) de lunes a viernes from Monday to Friday el fin de semana on the weekend en julio in July en verano in summer el número de teléfono the phone number Mi número es seis, cinco, cero… My number is six, five, zero… ¿Cuánto es? —Son veinte euros. How much is it? —It's twenty euros. Good to know Phone numbers are read digit by digit or in pairs: 61 20 34 → sesenta y uno, veinte, treinta y cuatro. Septiembre is also spelled setiembre in some countries. Days ending in -s do not change in the plural: el lunes, los lunes. Sábado and domingo add -s: los sábados. Common mistakes ✗ Hoy es Lunes. → ✓ Hoy es lunes. — days and months take a small letter. ✗ en el lunes → ✓ el lunes — Spanish has no word for \"on\" with days. ✗ veinte y dos → ✓ veintidós — today 21–29 are written as one word. ✗ en Julio → ✓ en julio — months also take a small letter. Practice Write the number in words. 15 22 48 100 Translate into Spanish. on Saturday on Fridays in October Today is Wednesday. Answers quince veintidós cuarenta y ocho cien el sábado los viernes en octubre Hoy es miércoles.","level":"A1","category":"Vocabulary","route":"#/a1/vocabulary-numbers-days-months"},{"id":"page:a1-grammar-gender-number","group":"pages","title":"Gender and number of nouns","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Every Spanish noun is masculine or feminine. Learn the endings that tell you which, and how to make plurals.","text":"In English a table is just \"it\". In Spanish every noun is masculine or feminine, even a table or an idea: el libro (the book) is masculine, la mesa (the table) is feminine. The gender has nothing to do with the thing itself, but it matters because the words around the noun change with it. Learn every new noun together with el or la. How it works Gender: most nouns show it in the ending. Ending Usually Examples -o masculine el libro, el vaso, el perro -a feminine la mesa, la casa, la playa -ción, -sión, -dad, -tad feminine la canción, la televisión, la ciudad, la libertad -or, -aje masculine el color, el viaje -e or a consonant (other than -or, -aje) either: learn it el coche, la noche; el hotel, la piel Common exceptions: el día, el mapa, el problema, el idioma, el sofá; la mano, la foto, la moto, la radio (el radio for the device in Mexico and parts of Latin America), la flor. People: many nouns change the ending (el amigo, la amiga; el profesor, la profesora). Nouns in -ista and -e often keep one form: el / la estudiante, el / la periodista. Number: making the plural. Singular ends in Add Examples a vowel -s libro → libros, coche → coches a consonant -es hotel → hoteles, ciudad → ciudades -z z → -ces lápiz → lápices, vez → veces el → los, la → las: los libros, las mesas. When a word stressed on the last syllable ends in -n or -s and adds -es, the written accent disappears: canción → canciones, inglés → ingleses. A stressed final vowel keeps it: sofá → sofás. Words in unstressed -s do not change: el lunes → los lunes. A group of men and women takes the masculine plural: los alumnos can be all boys or boys and girls. When to use it Every time you learn a noun. Learn la ciudad, not just ciudad. The article is your note of the gender. When you meet a new noun without an article. Look at the ending and guess with the table; you will be right most of the time. Talking about more than one. Dos hoteles, tres canciones, veinte días. Talking about mixed groups. Mis hermanos (mis = my) can mean \"my brothers\" or \"my brothers and sisters\". Examples Spanish English el día y la noche the day and the night la mano the hand el problema the problem las ciudades the cities los lápices the pencils dos canciones two songs los estudiantes the students (all men, or men and women) las estudiantes the students (all women) Good to know Some nouns change meaning with the gender: el capital (money) and la capital (the capital city). Most countries and cities are used without an article: Soy de Perú. Son de Madrid. Spanish has no neuter \"it\" nouns: everything is el or la. Common mistakes ✗ la problema → ✓ el problema — problema ends in -a but is masculine. ✗ el mano → ✓ la mano — mano ends in -o but is feminine. ✗ las ciudads → ✓ las ciudades — after a consonant the plural adds -es. ✗ los lápizs → ✓ los lápices — a final z becomes c before -es. Practice El or la? libro canción día foto Write the plural with the article. el hotel la ciudad el lápiz la canción Answers el la el la los hoteles las ciudades los lápices las canciones","level":"A1","category":"Grammar","route":"#/a1/grammar-gender-number"},{"id":"page:a1-grammar-articles","group":"pages","title":"Definite and indefinite articles; al and del","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"el, la, los, las and un, una, unos, unas; when Spanish uses an article where English does not, and a + el = al, de + el = del.","text":"Spanish articles work like English \"the\" and \"a\", with two differences. They change with the gender and number of the noun, so there are four of each. And Spanish uses \"the\" more often than English: for things in general (la vida, life), with titles (el señor García) and with days (el lunes). If your language has no articles at all, this is the topic to practice most. How it works masculine feminine the (singular) el libro la mesa the (plural) los libros las mesas a / an un libro una mesa some unos libros unas mesas Two short forms. A (to) and de (of, from) join with el: a + el = al: el cine → Voy al cine. (I'm going to the movies.) de + el = del: el profesor → el libro del profesor (the teacher's book) Only with el: a la, de la, a los, de las do not change. A feminine noun that starts with a stressed a- takes el and un in the singular, to avoid two a sounds: el agua (water), un aula (a classroom). It is still feminine: el agua fría, las aguas. When to use it Something specific, or already mentioned: el, la, los, las. Es la casa de Ana. (It's Ana's house.) Something new, one of many: un, una. Es un problema. (It's a problem.) Things in general take el / la / los / las, where English uses no article: El café es caro aquí. (Coffee is expensive here.) Los niños son curiosos. (Children are curious.) Talking about a person with a title: el señor García, la doctora Ruiz. But not when you speak to the person: Buenos días, señor García. Days of the week: el lunes (on Monday), los lunes (on Mondays). No article with a job after ser (Es médico), with most countries (Soy de Perú), and for an unspecified amount of something: Hay pan y leche. (There's bread and milk.) Examples Spanish English Es un libro. Es el libro de Marta. It's a book. It's Marta's book. Las mesas son nuevas. The tables are new. El café es caro aquí. Coffee is expensive here. El señor García es de Chile. Mr. García is from Chile. Buenos días, señora Ruiz. Good morning, Mrs. Ruiz. Voy al cine el sábado. I'm going to the movies on Saturday. Es el coche del profesor. It's the teacher's car. Son unos amigos de México. They're friends from Mexico. Good to know Voy (I'm going) is a form of ir; you will learn it later. Here it only shows al. De + person shows possession, where English has 's: la casa de Ana (Ana's house). Common mistakes ✗ Coche es rojo. → ✓ El coche es rojo. — a singular noun as the subject needs an article. ✗ Voy a el cine. → ✓ Voy al cine. — a + el always joins into al. ✗ el libro de el profesor → ✓ el libro del profesor — de + el joins into del. ✗ El señor García, ¿cómo está? (speaking to him) → ✓ Señor García, ¿cómo está? — no article when you speak directly to someone. ✗ la agua → ✓ el agua — a feminine noun with a stressed a- takes el in the singular. Practice Write el, la, los, las, un or una. Es casa muy bonita. (a) niños son simpáticos. (the) ciudad es grande. (the) Hay problema. (a) Write al, del or de la. Voy cine. (el cine) Es el perro vecino. (el vecino) Es la casa profesora. (la profesora) Translate: Good afternoon, Mr. López. Answers una Los La un al del de la Buenas tardes, señor López.","level":"A1","category":"Grammar","route":"#/a1/grammar-articles"},{"id":"page:a1-grammar-adjective-agreement","group":"pages","title":"Adjective agreement and position","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Spanish adjectives agree with the noun in gender and number, and they usually come after it: una casa blanca.","text":"An English adjective never changes: a white house, two white cars. A Spanish adjective follows its noun in two ways. It agrees: it takes the same gender and number as the noun. And it usually comes after the noun: una casa blanca is word for word \"a house white\". How it works Agreement. Adjectives have up to four forms. Adjective ends in masculine feminine masculine plural feminine plural -o alto alta altos altas -e grande grande grandes grandes a consonant azul azul azules azules nationality in a consonant español española españoles españolas -e and most consonant adjectives have one form for both genders: un coche grande, una casa grande; un libro fácil, una pregunta fácil. Nationalities in a consonant add -a for the feminine: inglés → inglesa, alemán → alemana. The accent disappears when a syllable is added. A mixed group takes the masculine plural: Juan y Ana son altos. Position. Most adjectives go after the noun: un coche rojo, una ciudad bonita, unos chicos simpáticos. These go before the noun: numbers and quantities: dos libros, mucho tiempo, pocas personas; bueno and malo often do, and they lose the -o before a masculine singular noun: un buen amigo, un mal día; grande before a singular noun becomes gran and means \"great, important\": un gran hombre (a great man), un hombre grande (a big man). When to use it Describing things. Es un coche rojo. (It's a red car.) Describing people with ser. Mi hermana es alta. Mis amigos son simpáticos. Saying where someone is from. Es mexicana. (She is Mexican.) Son españoles. (They are Spanish.) Nationalities take a small letter. Giving an opinion with muy. La casa es muy bonita. (The house is very pretty.) Muy never changes. Examples Spanish English una casa blanca a white house unos chicos simpáticos some nice boys (or boys and girls) Las calles son muy estrechas. The streets are very narrow. Mi profesora es colombiana. My teacher is Colombian. Es un libro fácil. It's an easy book. Los zapatos son azules. The shoes are blue. Es un buen restaurante. It's a good restaurant. Es un gran hombre. He's a great man. Good to know An adjective can also stand after ser, far from the noun, and it still agrees: La sopa es deliciosa. Los tomates son caros. Colors that are also nouns, like naranja (orange) and rosa (pink), usually do not change: unas flores naranja. Both flores naranja and flores naranjas are heard. Common mistakes ✗ la casa blanco → ✓ la casa blanca — the adjective takes the gender of the noun. ✗ un rojo coche → ✓ un coche rojo — colors and most adjectives come after the noun. ✗ las chicas simpáticos → ✓ las chicas simpáticas — gender and number both agree. ✗ Ella es inglés. → ✓ Ella es inglesa. — nationalities in a consonant have a feminine form. ✗ un bueno amigo → ✓ un buen amigo — bueno loses the -o before a masculine singular noun. Practice Write the right form of the adjective. una ciudad (bonito) unos coches (rojo) unas mesas (grande) María es (español) Put the words in order. coche / un / azul amigo / un / buen simpáticas / las / chicas / son Translate: It's a white house. Answers una ciudad bonita unos coches rojos unas mesas grandes María es española. un coche azul un buen amigo Las chicas son simpáticas. Es una casa blanca.","level":"A1","category":"Grammar","route":"#/a1/grammar-adjective-agreement"},{"id":"page:a1-verbs-tener","group":"pages","title":"tener: possession, age and common expressions","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Tener means \"to have\". Spanish also uses it for age and for hunger, thirst, heat, cold and fear.","text":"Tener means \"to have\": Tengo un perro. (I have a dog.) Spanish uses it in more places than English does. Your age is something you \"have\": Tengo 30 años. And many feelings of the body are things you \"have\" too: Tengo hambre (I'm hungry, word for word \"I have hunger\"). Where English says \"I am…\", Spanish often says tengo. How it works Person tener English yo tengo I have tú tienes you have él, ella, usted tiene he / she has, you have (formal) nosotros, nosotras tenemos we have vosotros, vosotras (Spain) tenéis you have (plural) ellos, ellas, ustedes tienen they have, you have (plural) The yo form ends in -go: tengo. The stem changes to tie- in tienes, tiene and tienen, but not in tengo, tenemos and tenéis. Negative: No tengo hermanos. After no, a single countable thing often has no article: No tengo coche. (more natural than No tengo un coche) Question: ¿Tienes hermanos? (Do you have any brothers or sisters?) Expressions with tener (the noun after tener does not change): Spanish English tener hambre to be hungry tener sed to be thirsty tener calor / frío to be hot / cold (a person) tener sueño to be sleepy tener miedo to be afraid tener prisa to be in a hurry tener razón to be right To say \"very\", use mucho or mucha with the gender of the noun: mucho frío, mucho calor, but mucha hambre, mucha sed, mucha prisa. When to use it Possession. Tengo un coche nuevo. (I have a new car.) Family and friends. Tenemos dos hijos. (We have two children.) ¿Tienes novio? (Do you have a boyfriend?) Age. ¿Cuántos años tienes? (How old are you?) Tengo veintiún años. (I'm twenty-one.) How your body feels. Tengo frío. (I'm cold.) ¿Tienes hambre? (Are you hungry?) Agreeing with someone. Tienes razón. (You're right.) Examples Spanish English Tengo dos hermanos y una hermana. I have two brothers and a sister. ¿Cuántos años tiene usted? How old are you? (formal) Mi abuela tiene ochenta años. My grandmother is eighty. No tenemos coche. We don't have a car. ¿Tenéis hambre? (Spain) Are you (all) hungry? Los niños tienen sueño. The children are sleepy. Tengo mucho calor. I'm very hot. Tienes razón. You're right. Good to know Tengo calor is about how you feel. For the weather, Spanish says Hace calor (it's hot); you will learn it later. Estar caliente about a person can have a sexual meaning. Say tengo calor. Tener que + infinitive means \"to have to\": Tengo que trabajar. It comes in A2. Common mistakes ✗ Soy 25 años. → ✓ Tengo 25 años. — age takes tener, not ser. ✗ Estoy caliente. (I feel hot) → ✓ Tengo calor. — the body feeling is tener calor. ✗ Tengo mucho hambre. → ✓ Tengo mucha hambre. — hambre is feminine (el hambre, like el agua). ✗ Estoy hambre. → ✓ Tengo hambre. — hambre is a noun (hunger), so you \"have\" it. Practice Complete with the right form of tener. Yo un gato. ¿Cuántos años (tú)? Nosotros dos hijas. Ellos no coche. Translate into Spanish. I'm hungry. She is twenty years old. Are you cold? (to a friend) You're right. (formal) Answers tengo tienes tenemos tienen Tengo hambre. Tiene veinte años. / Ella tiene veinte años. ¿Tienes frío? Usted tiene razón. / Tiene razón.","level":"A1","category":"Verbs: tenses and moods","route":"#/a1/verbs-tener"},{"id":"page:a1-functions-personal-details","group":"pages","title":"Give and ask for personal details","headings":["Key phrases","Dialogue","Culture note","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Ask and answer where you are from, how old you are, what you do, your phone number and e-mail, in both tú and usted.","text":"Forms, language courses, hotels and new friends all ask the same few questions. With ser and tener you can already answer most of them. Two things are different from English: \"What is your name / number?\" is ¿Cuál es…?, not ¿Qué es…?, and \"How old are you?\" uses tener: ¿Cuántos años tienes? Learn each question in two versions: with tú for people your age and with usted at an office or a reception desk. Key phrases Asking (tú / usted) Spanish English ¿Cómo te llamas? / ¿Cómo se llama? What's your name? ¿De dónde eres? / ¿De dónde es? Where are you from? ¿Cuántos años tienes? / ¿Cuántos años tiene? How old are you? ¿A qué te dedicas? / ¿A qué se dedica? What do you do (for a living)? ¿Cuál es tu número de teléfono? / ¿Cuál es su número? What's your phone number? ¿Cuál es tu correo electrónico? / ¿Cuál es su correo? What's your e-mail address? ¿Cómo se escribe? How do you spell it? Answering Spanish English Me llamo Ana García. My name is Ana García. Soy de Canadá. Soy canadiense. I'm from Canada. I'm Canadian. Tengo veintiocho años. I'm twenty-eight. Soy enfermera. / Soy estudiante. I'm a nurse. / I'm a student. Mi número es el 6 47 31 25 08. My number is 6 47 31 25 08. Es ana punto garcía arroba correo punto com. It's ana.garcia@correo.com. Con tilde / sin tilde. With / without an accent. Checking Spanish English ¿Perdón? ¿Puedes repetir? / ¿Puede repetir? Sorry? Can you repeat that? ¿Y tú? / ¿Y usted? And you? Dialogue At the reception desk of a language school. Receptionist: Buenos días. ¿Cómo se llama? (Good morning. What's your name?) Daniel: Daniel Wright. (Daniel Wright.) Receptionist: ¿Cómo se escribe Wright? (How do you spell Wright?) Daniel: Uve doble, erre, i, ge, hache, te. (W, R, I, G, H, T.) Receptionist: Gracias. ¿De dónde es? (Thank you. Where are you from?) Daniel: Soy de Toronto. Soy canadiense. (I'm from Toronto. I'm Canadian.) Receptionist: ¿Y cuántos años tiene? (And how old are you?) Daniel: Treinta y cuatro. (Thirty-four.) Receptionist: ¿Cuál es su número de teléfono? (What's your phone number?) Daniel: Es el seis, cuarenta y siete, treinta y uno, veinticinco, cero ocho. (It's 6 47 31 25 08.) Culture note In Spain phone numbers are often read in pairs or threes; in many Latin American countries digit by digit is more common. Both are understood. Spanish speakers have two family names, the father's and the mother's: Ana García López. On forms, apellidos means \"surnames\". @ is arroba, . is punto, - is guion and is guion bajo. W is uve doble (Spain), doble ve or doble u (Latin America). Common mistakes ✗ ¿Qué es tu número? → ✓ ¿Cuál es tu número? — \"what is\" + a piece of information is ¿cuál es…? ✗ ¿Cuántos años eres? → ✓ ¿Cuántos años tienes? — age takes tener. ✗ Soy Argentino. → ✓ Soy argentino. — nationalities take a small letter. ✗ Soy un estudiante. → ✓ Soy estudiante. — no article before a job or role on its own. Practice Write the question for each answer (use tú). —Me llamo Sofía. —Soy de Chile. —Tengo diecinueve años. —Soy camarero. Write the formal (usted) version. ¿De dónde eres? ¿Cuántos años tienes? ¿Cuál es tu número de teléfono? Translate: I'm a student and I'm twenty years old. Answers ¿Cómo te llamas? ¿De dónde eres? ¿Cuántos años tienes? ¿A qué te dedicas? ¿De dónde es usted? / ¿De dónde es? ¿Cuántos años tiene usted? / ¿Cuántos años tiene? ¿Cuál es su número de teléfono? Soy estudiante y tengo veinte años.","level":"A1","category":"Communication","route":"#/a1/functions-personal-details"},{"id":"page:a1-vocabulary-family-people","group":"pages","title":"Family, describing people, nationalities, jobs","headings":["Words","Word partners","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Words for your family, how people look and what they are like, where they are from and what they do.","text":"With ser and tener you can now talk about the people in your life: Tengo una hermana. Es alta y muy simpática. Es médica. Remember three rules from earlier pages: adjectives and many nouns change for women (el médico, la médica), nationalities take a small letter, and there is no un / una before a job on its own. Words Family Spanish English el padre / la madre father / mother los padres parents (or fathers) el hermano / la hermana brother / sister los hermanos brothers, or brothers and sisters el hijo / la hija son / daughter los hijos children (sons and daughters) el abuelo / la abuela grandfather / grandmother el tío / la tía uncle / aunt el primo / la prima cousin (male / female) el marido / la mujer husband / wife el novio / la novia boyfriend / girlfriend Describing people Spanish English alto / bajo tall / short joven / mayor young / older, elderly rubio / moreno blond / dark-haired delgado slim simpático / antipático nice / unfriendly inteligente smart divertido fun tímido shy Nationalities (small letter) Spanish English español / española Spanish mexicano / mexicana Mexican colombiano / colombiana Colombian argentino / argentina Argentinian estadounidense American (from the US) canadiense Canadian inglés / inglesa English alemán / alemana German Jobs Spanish English el médico / la médica doctor el enfermero / la enfermera nurse el profesor / la profesora teacher el / la estudiante student el camarero / la camarera waiter / waitress (Spain; mesero in Mexico, mozo in Argentina) el ingeniero / la ingeniera engineer el / la policía police officer (la policía also means \"the police\") el abogado / la abogada lawyer Word partners Spanish English Tengo dos hermanos. I have two brothers (or a brother and a sister). Es hijo único. He's an only child. Mi hermana es alta y morena. My sister is tall and dark-haired. Mis padres son muy simpáticos. My parents are very nice. Mi abuelo es mayor y tiene noventa años. My grandfather is old; he's ninety. Es de Colombia, es colombiana. She's from Colombia; she's Colombian. Mi madre es profesora. My mother is a teacher. ¿Tienes novio? Do you have a boyfriend? Es muy divertido. He's a lot of fun. Good to know El marido / la mujer are used everywhere; el esposo / la esposa are also common, especially in Latin America. Mayor is the polite word for an old person. Viejo can sound rude about people. Americano can mean anyone from the Americas; estadounidense is only \"from the US\". Common mistakes ✗ mis parientes (meaning \"my parents\") → ✓ mis padres — parientes means \"relatives\". ✗ Es Mexicana. → ✓ Es mexicana. — nationalities take a small letter. ✗ Mi hermana es alto. → ✓ Mi hermana es alta. — the adjective agrees with hermana. ✗ Ella es inglés. → ✓ Ella es inglesa. — the feminine adds -a. Practice Write the female form. el abuelo el profesor Es español. Es alemán. Translate into Spanish. my parents I have a sister. He's tall and nice. She's a nurse. Answers la abuela la profesora Es española. Es alemana. mis padres Tengo una hermana. Es alto y simpático. Es enfermera.","level":"A1","category":"Vocabulary","route":"#/a1/vocabulary-family-people"},{"id":"page:a1-verbs-present-regular","group":"pages","title":"Present tense of regular -ar, -er, -ir verbs","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Drop -ar, -er or -ir and add one ending per person. One pattern gives you hundreds of verbs.","text":"Every Spanish verb ends in -ar, -er or -ir in the dictionary: hablar (to speak), comer (to eat), vivir (to live). In the present tense you take off that ending and add a new one that shows who does the action. English adds only -s (I speak, she speaks); Spanish has six endings, one per person. The good news: once you know the pattern, it works for hundreds of regular verbs. How it works Person hablar (to speak) comer (to eat) vivir (to live) yo hablo como vivo tú hablas comes vives él, ella, usted habla come vive nosotros, nosotras hablamos comemos vivimos vosotros, vosotras (Spain) habláis coméis vivís ellos, ellas, ustedes hablan comen viven The yo form always ends in -o. -er and -ir verbs share every ending except nosotros and vosotros: comemos / vivimos, coméis / vivís. The pronoun is usually left out, because the ending shows the person: Hablo inglés. (I speak English.) Negative: no before the verb: No como carne. (I don't eat meat.) Question: the same verb with ¿ ?: ¿Hablas español? (Do you speak Spanish?) Spanish has no \"do / does\". Common regular verbs: -ar: hablar, trabajar (to work), estudiar (to study), escuchar (to listen), llegar (to arrive), comprar (to buy), tomar (to take, to drink). -er: comer, beber (to drink), leer (to read), aprender (to learn), comprender (to understand). -ir: vivir, escribir (to write), abrir (to open), recibir (to receive). When to use it Things that are true now. Vivo en Bogotá. (I live in Bogotá.) Habits and routines. Trabajo de lunes a viernes. (I work from Monday to Friday.) Actions happening right now. ¿Qué lees? (What are you reading?) English uses \"-ing\" here; Spanish can use the simple present. Near plans with a time word. Mañana trabajo hasta las seis. (Tomorrow I'm working until six.) Examples Spanish English Hablo inglés y un poco de español. I speak English and a little Spanish. ¿Dónde vives? Where do you live? Mi hermano trabaja en un banco. My brother works in a bank. No comemos carne. We don't eat meat. Los niños aprenden muy rápido. Children learn very fast. ¿Escribís en español? (Spain) Do you (all) write in Spanish? Mis padres viven en el centro. My parents live downtown. ¿Lees el periódico todos los días? Do you read the newspaper every day? Good to know Many verbs change their stem or have an irregular yo form (quiero, tengo, hago). They come in unit 4; see . Tomar is used for drinks and food in many places: Tomo un café. (I'm having a coffee.) The vosotros forms carry a written accent: habláis, coméis, vivís. Common mistakes ✗ Yo hablar español. → ✓ Hablo español. — the verb needs a personal ending; hablar is only the dictionary form. ✗ Él comes pescado. → ✓ Él come pescado. — -es is the tú ending; él takes come. ✗ Nosotros vivemos en Lima. → ✓ Nosotros vivimos en Lima. — -ir verbs end in -imos. ✗ Él come no carne. → ✓ Él no come carne. — no goes before the verb. Practice Complete with the right form. Yo (trabajar) en una tienda. ¿Tú (comer) pescado? Ana (vivir) en México. Nosotros (aprender) español. Ellos no (beber) café. Translate into Spanish. Do you speak English? (to a friend) We live downtown. I don't read the newspaper. Answers trabajo comes vive aprendemos beben ¿Hablas inglés? Vivimos en el centro. No leo el periódico.","level":"A1","category":"Verbs: tenses and moods","route":"#/a1/verbs-present-regular"},{"id":"page:a1-grammar-questions-negation","group":"pages","title":"Question words and negation","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Ask with qué, quién, dónde, cuándo, cómo, cuánto, por qué; say no with no before the verb, and nunca, nada, nadie.","text":"Spanish questions are simpler than English ones: there is no \"do\" or \"does\". You keep the normal verb and add ¿ ? and a rising voice, or start with a question word. Question words carry a written accent when they ask (or exclaim). Negation is simple too: put no before the verb. One surprise for English speakers: Spanish uses a double negative, No como nada (\"I don't eat nothing\"), and it is correct. How it works Yes / no questions: the same words as a statement, with ¿ ? Often the subject goes after the verb. Hablas español. → ¿Hablas español? (Do you speak Spanish?) ¿Trabaja Ana aquí? / ¿Ana trabaja aquí? (Does Ana work here?) Both orders are correct. Question words (always with an accent): Spanish English Example ¿qué? what? ¿Qué comes? ¿quién? / ¿quiénes? who? ¿Quién es? ¿dónde? where? ¿Dónde vives? ¿de dónde? where … from? ¿De dónde eres? ¿cuándo? when? ¿Cuándo trabajas? ¿cómo? how? / what … like? ¿Cómo es tu casa? ¿cuánto? / ¿cuánta? / ¿cuántos? / ¿cuántas? how much? / how many? ¿Cuántos años tienes? ¿cuál? / ¿cuáles? which? / what? ¿Cuál es tu número? ¿por qué? why? ¿Por qué estudias español? The question word comes first; the verb comes right after it: ¿Dónde trabaja tu madre? A preposition goes before the question word: ¿De dónde eres? ¿Con quién vives? Cuánto agrees with the noun: ¿Cuántas hermanas tienes? The answer to ¿por qué? starts with porque (one word, no accent): Porque me gusta. (Because I like it.) Negation: no + verb: No hablo francés. (I don't speak French.) Short answers: —¿Eres de aquí? —No, no soy de aquí. The first no answers, the second one negates the verb. Negative words need no before the verb when they come after it: No como nunca carne. (I never eat meat.) No tengo nada. (I have nothing.) No hay nadie. (There is nobody.) Before the verb, they work alone: Nunca como carne. When to use it Asking for information. ¿Dónde está el baño? (Where is the bathroom?) Checking something. ¿Trabajas mañana? (Are you working tomorrow?) Saying what you don't do or have. No tengo perro. (I don't have a dog.) Saying \"never\", \"nothing\", \"nobody\". No trabajo nunca los domingos. (I never work on Sundays.) Examples Spanish English ¿Qué estudias? What do you study? ¿Quién es la chica de la foto? Who is the girl in the photo? ¿Cuándo llega el tren? When does the train arrive? ¿Cuántos hermanos tienes? How many brothers and sisters do you have? ¿Por qué no comes? —Porque no tengo hambre. Why aren't you eating? —Because I'm not hungry. No vivo aquí. I don't live here. No como nunca pescado. I never eat fish. No hay nadie en casa. There's nobody at home. Good to know ¿Qué? or ¿Cómo? can mean \"Sorry? What did you say?\". ¿Cómo? is more polite. ¿Qué es…? asks for a definition (¿Qué es una tortilla?); ¿Cuál es…? asks for one item of information (¿Cuál es tu dirección?). In exclamations the accent stays: ¡Qué bonito! (How pretty!) Common mistakes ✗ ¿Donde vives? → ✓ ¿Dónde vives? — question words carry a written accent. ✗ No veo algo. → ✓ No veo nada. — after no, use the negative word nada. ✗ Hablo no inglés. → ✓ No hablo inglés. — no goes right before the verb. ✗ ¿Por qué? —Por qué no tengo tiempo. → ✓ —Porque no tengo tiempo. — the answer uses porque, one word. Practice Choose the question word. ¿ vives? —En Lima. (Dónde / Cuándo) ¿ años tienes? —Veinte. (Cuántos / Cuántas) ¿ es tu profesor? —Es simpático. (Cómo / Qué) ¿ estudias español? —Porque me gusta. (Por qué / Porque) Make the sentences negative. Hablo alemán. Tengo algo. (→ nada) Hay alguien en casa. (→ nadie) Translate: I never work on Sundays. Answers Dónde Cuántos Cómo Por qué No hablo alemán. No tengo nada. No hay nadie en casa. Nunca trabajo los domingos. / No trabajo nunca los domingos.","level":"A1","category":"Grammar","route":"#/a1/grammar-questions-negation"},{"id":"page:a1-verbs-estar-hay","group":"pages","title":"estar for location and states; hay vs está","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Estar says where something is and how someone feels. Hay says that something exists; está says where a known thing is.","text":"Estar is the second Spanish verb for \"to be\". You use it for where a person or thing is (Estoy en casa) and for how someone or something is at the moment (Estoy cansado). Next to it stands hay, \"there is / there are\", which says that something exists somewhere: Hay un banco en la plaza. English uses \"to be\" for all of this, so these three are worth learning together. How it works Person estar English yo estoy I am tú estás you are él, ella, usted está he / she is, you are (formal) nosotros, nosotras estamos we are vosotros, vosotras (Spain) estáis you are (plural) ellos, ellas, ustedes están they are, you are (plural) Watch the accents: estás, está, estáis, están. Esta without an accent means \"this\". Location: estar + en / aquí / allí …: Estamos en el hotel. States and feelings: estar + adjective, which agrees: Ana está cansada. Los niños están contentos. Hay (from the verb haber) has one form for singular and plural: Hay + a / an, numbers, or no article: Hay un parque. Hay dos bancos. Hay pan. Está / están + the, a name or my: El parque está allí. Mis llaves están en la mesa. Ask with ¿Hay…? when you don't know if something exists, and with ¿Dónde está…? when you know it does: ¿Hay un banco por aquí? (Is there a bank around here?) ¿Dónde está el banco? (Where is the bank?) When to use it Where someone or something is. ¿Dónde estás? —Estoy en el trabajo. (I'm at work.) How someone feels now. Estoy cansado. (I'm tired.) ¿Estás bien? (Are you OK?) The state of something. La puerta está abierta. (The door is open.) El café está frío. (The coffee is cold.) Asking and saying how someone is. ¿Cómo estás? —Muy bien, gracias. Saying what there is in a place: hay. En mi calle hay dos restaurantes. (There are two restaurants on my street.) Examples Spanish English Estoy en casa. I'm at home. ¿Dónde están las llaves? Where are the keys? Marta está enferma hoy. Marta is sick today. Estamos muy contentos. We're very happy. La tienda está cerrada. The store is closed. Hay un supermercado en la esquina. There's a supermarket on the corner. ¿Hay wifi en el hotel? Is there wifi in the hotel? No hay leche. There's no milk. Good to know Hay has no plural form: Hay una farmacia. Hay tres farmacias. Never hayn. Estar with bien / mal: Estoy bien. (I'm fine.) Bien never changes. The difference between ser and estar with adjectives (es aburrido / está aburrido) comes in . Common mistakes ✗ Soy en casa. → ✓ Estoy en casa. — where you are takes estar. ✗ Hay el banco en la plaza. → ✓ El banco está en la plaza. — with el, la use está; hay is for something new (hay un banco). ✗ Están dos farmacias en mi calle. → ✓ Hay dos farmacias en mi calle. — \"there are\" is hay. ✗ Ella esta cansada. → ✓ Ella está cansada. — esta without the accent means \"this\". ✗ Estoy cansado (said by a woman) → ✓ Estoy cansada. — the adjective agrees with the speaker. Practice Complete with the right form of estar. Yo en la oficina. ¿Dónde (tú)? Los niños cansados. Hay or está / están? un restaurante muy bueno cerca. El restaurante en la calle Mayor. ¿Dónde mis llaves? Translate into Spanish. Is there a pharmacy around here? We're at home. Answers estoy estás están Hay está están ¿Hay una farmacia por aquí? Estamos en casa.","level":"A1","category":"Verbs: tenses and moods","route":"#/a1/verbs-estar-hay"},{"id":"page:a1-verbs-ser-vs-estar-basics","group":"pages","title":"ser vs estar: the basics","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Ser for what something is; estar for where it is and how it is now. Some adjectives change meaning with the verb.","text":"You now know both verbs for \"to be\". This page puts them side by side, because choosing between them is one of the first real challenges in Spanish. The short version: ser says what someone or something is (identity, origin, job, character), and estar says where it is and how it is at the moment. The same adjective can go with both verbs and change the meaning: Es aburrido (he is boring), Está aburrido (he is bored). How it works ser estar identity, name: Es Ana. location: Ana está en Madrid. origin: Es de Chile. feelings now: Está contenta. job: Es médica. states now: Está enferma. character, looks: Es simpática. Es alta. how it looks or tastes now: La sopa está rica. time and dates: Son las tres. Hoy es lunes. results of a change: La puerta está abierta. where an event takes place: La fiesta es en mi casa. bien / mal: Estoy bien. Some adjectives change meaning: with ser with estar Es aburrido. He is boring. Está aburrido. He is bored. Es listo. He is clever. Está listo. He is ready. Es rico. He is rich. Está rico. (food) It tastes good. Es malo. He is bad. Está malo. He is sick (mainly Spain; everywhere: está enfermo). / (food) It tastes bad. When to use it Ask \"what is it like in general?\" → ser. Mi ciudad es tranquila. (My city is quiet.) Ask \"how is it now?\" or \"where is it?\" → estar. Hoy la ciudad está tranquila. (The city is quiet today.) La ciudad está en el norte. Describing a person: ser for who they are, estar for how they feel. Pedro es tímido, pero hoy está contento. (Pedro is shy, but today he's happy.) Talking about food: ser for what it is (La paella es un plato español), estar for how it tastes now (La paella está muy rica). Examples Spanish English Soy de Colombia, pero estoy en Madrid. I'm from Colombia, but I'm in Madrid. Laura es médica. Hoy está cansada. Laura is a doctor. Today she's tired. La casa es grande y está en el centro. The house is big and it's downtown. Mi hermano es muy alegre. My brother is a very cheerful person. ¿Estás bien? —No, estoy un poco mal. Are you OK? —No, I'm a bit unwell. La película es aburrida. The movie is boring. Los niños están aburridos. The children are bored. ¿Estás listo? Son las ocho. Are you ready? It's eight o'clock. Good to know Estar + muerto (dead) is the normal form, although death is permanent: El pez está muerto. Some words take only one verb: ser + nouns (Es profesor), estar + de acuerdo (Estoy de acuerdo, I agree). A quick check: if you can add \"right now\" without changing the sense, estar is usually the answer. Common mistakes ✗ Soy cansado. → ✓ Estoy cansado. — feeling tired is a state now: estar. ✗ Está médico. → ✓ Es médico. — a job is what someone is: ser. ✗ Madrid es en España. → ✓ Madrid está en España. — location of places takes estar. ✗ La fiesta está en mi casa. → ✓ La fiesta es en mi casa. — where an event takes place uses ser. ✗ Estoy aburrido (meaning \"I'm a boring person\") → ✓ Soy aburrido. — with estar it means \"I'm bored\". Practice Ser or estar? Write the right form. Mi madre profesora. Hoy (yo) muy cansado. Lima en Perú. La fiesta en el bar de Juan. Carlos de Argentina. ¿Dónde los niños? Translate into Spanish. The children are bored. Are you ready? (to a friend; you are talking to a woman) Answers es estoy está es es están Los niños están aburridos. ¿Estás lista?","level":"A1","category":"Verbs: tenses and moods","route":"#/a1/verbs-ser-vs-estar-basics"},{"id":"page:a1-vocabulary-home-city","group":"pages","title":"Home, furniture and places in town","headings":["Words","Word partners","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Rooms, furniture and the places you need in a town, with hay and estar to say what there is and where it is.","text":"With hay and estar you can now describe where you live: Mi casa tiene dos dormitorios. Hay un supermercado en la esquina. La farmacia está al lado del banco. Learn every noun with its article. Words for \"apartment\" differ by country, so both are given; you will be understood with either. Words The home Spanish English la casa the house, the home el piso (Spain) / el departamento (Mexico, Argentina, Chile) / el apartamento (understood everywhere) the apartment la habitación / el cuarto the room, the bedroom el dormitorio the bedroom el salón (Spain) / la sala (Latin America) the living room la cocina the kitchen el baño the bathroom el jardín the garden, the yard la ventana the window la puerta the door Furniture Spanish English la cama the bed la mesa the table la silla the chair el sofá the sofa el armario the closet, the wardrobe la estantería the bookcase, the shelves la lámpara the lamp la nevera (Spain) / el refrigerador (Latin America) the fridge Places in town Spanish English la calle the street la plaza the square el banco the bank la farmacia the pharmacy el supermercado the supermarket la tienda the store el restaurante the restaurant el hospital the hospital la estación (de tren) the (train) station la parada de autobús the bus stop el parque the park el museo the museum la biblioteca the library Where? Spanish English cerca (de) / lejos (de) near / far (from) al lado de next to enfrente de opposite entre between en la esquina on the corner Word partners Spanish English Vivo en un piso pequeño. (Spain) I live in a small apartment. La cocina es muy grande. The kitchen is very big. En mi habitación hay una cama y una mesa. In my room there's a bed and a table. ¿Hay un banco por aquí? Is there a bank around here? La farmacia está al lado del banco. The pharmacy is next to the bank. El museo está enfrente del parque. The museum is opposite the park. La estación está lejos del centro. The station is far from downtown. La parada de autobús está en la esquina. The bus stop is on the corner. Mi casa está cerca de la playa. My house is near the beach. Good to know De + el always becomes del: al lado del banco, cerca del parque (but cerca de la plaza). Librería is a bookstore; a library is la biblioteca. El baño is the bathroom at home; in a restaurant people often ask ¿Dónde está el baño? or, in Spain, ¿Dónde están los servicios? Common mistakes ✗ Hay la farmacia en la plaza. → ✓ La farmacia está en la plaza. — a known place (la) takes está. ✗ la librería (meaning \"the library\") → ✓ la biblioteca — librería means \"bookstore\". ✗ al lado de el banco → ✓ al lado del banco — de + el becomes del. ✗ El hospital está cerca la estación. → ✓ El hospital está cerca de la estación. — cerca needs de before a place. Practice Write the word with el or la. kitchen bed pharmacy library Translate into Spanish. Is there a supermarket near here? The bank is next to the park. My apartment has two bedrooms. (Spain) The station is far from downtown. Answers la cocina la cama la farmacia la biblioteca ¿Hay un supermercado cerca? / ¿Hay un supermercado cerca de aquí? / ¿Hay un supermercado por aquí? El banco está al lado del parque. Mi piso tiene dos dormitorios. / Mi piso tiene dos habitaciones. La estación está lejos del centro.","level":"A1","category":"Vocabulary","route":"#/a1/vocabulary-home-city"},{"id":"page:a1-verbs-gustar","group":"pages","title":"gustar and similar verbs","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Say what you like and don't like. In Spanish the thing you like is the subject, not you.","text":"In English you say \"I like coffee\", and I is the subject. Spanish turns the sentence around: Me gusta el café is, word for word, \"Coffee pleases me\". The thing you like is the subject; you are the person it pleases. That is why you only need two forms of the verb: gusta and gustan. How it works (a + person) + pronoun + gusta / gustan + what you like Who likes it Pronoun One thing, or an action Several things (a mí) me gusta gustan (a ti) te gusta gustan (a él, a ella, a usted) le gusta gustan (a nosotros, a nosotras) nos gusta gustan (a vosotros, a vosotras) (Spain) os gusta gustan (a ellos, a ellas, a ustedes) les gusta gustan gusta + singular noun with its article: Me gusta el café. gusta + infinitive, also with two or more infinitives: Me gusta bailar. Me gusta cantar y bailar. gustan + plural noun: Me gustan los gatos. Negative: no goes before the pronoun: No me gusta el té. Questions keep the same word order: ¿Te gusta el fútbol? When to use it Say what you like or don't like. Me gusta la música. (I like music.) No me gustan los lunes. (I don't like Mondays.) Ask someone. To a friend: ¿Te gusta cocinar? Formal: ¿Le gusta la habitación? Say how much. Me gusta mucho el cine. (I like movies a lot.) No me gusta nada el frío. (I don't like the cold at all.) Me encanta means \"I love\": Me encanta esta ciudad. Make clear who. Le can mean he, she or you (formal), so add a + the person: A María le gusta el cine. A mí no. Agree or disagree. —Me gusta el café. —A mí también. (Me too.) —No me gusta el té. —A mí tampoco. (Me neither.) Examples Spanish English Me gusta el chocolate. I like chocolate. ¿Te gustan los perros? Do you like dogs? No nos gusta trabajar los sábados. We don't like working on Saturdays. A Carlos le gusta mucho viajar. Carlos really likes traveling. A mis padres les gustan las películas antiguas. My parents like old movies. Me encanta esta ciudad. I love this city. ¿Os gusta la comida mexicana? (Spain) Do you all like Mexican food? —No me gusta el frío. —A mí tampoco. \"I don't like the cold.\" \"Me neither.\" Good to know The same pattern works for encantar (to love), interesar (to interest) and doler (to hurt): Me interesan los idiomas. Me duele la cabeza. Me encanta is already strong. Do not add mucho. About people, Me gustas means \"I am attracted to you\". To say you like someone as a person, use Me cae bien: Tu hermano me cae muy bien. Common mistakes ✗ Yo gusto el café. → ✓ Me gusta el café. — you are not the subject; the coffee is. ✗ Me gusta los gatos. → ✓ Me gustan los gatos. — the verb agrees with the thing you like. ✗ Me gusta café. → ✓ Me gusta el café. — things you like in general need the article. ✗ María le gusta bailar. → ✓ A María le gusta bailar. — the person needs a. ✗ Me gusto bailar. → ✓ Me gusta bailar. — me gusto means \"I like myself\". Practice Complete with the pronoun and gusta or gustan. (a mí) la paella. (a ti) ¿ los deportes? A Ana leer. (a nosotros) No las verduras. A mis amigos cantar y bailar. Translate into Spanish. I don't like coffee. Do you like the hotel? (formal) \"I love dogs.\" \"Me too.\" Answers Me gusta Te gustan le gusta nos gustan les gusta No me gusta el café. ¿Le gusta el hotel? —Me encantan los perros. —A mí también.","level":"A1","category":"Verbs: tenses and moods","route":"#/a1/verbs-gustar"},{"id":"page:a1-verbs-present-irregular","group":"pages","title":"Common irregular and stem-changing verbs in the present","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"The verbs you use most are irregular: ir, hacer, querer, poder, salir, jugar. Learn the yo forms and the three stem changes.","text":"The most useful Spanish verbs are irregular, as in English (be, have, go), but they follow a few patterns. Some have an odd yo form (hago, salgo). Others change a vowel in the stem: querer → quiero, poder → puedo, pedir → pido. And a few, like ir (to go), you simply learn by heart. How it works 1. Stem changes (in every form except nosotros and vosotros): e → ie: querer (to want) o → ue: poder (can) e → i: pedir (to ask for) quiero puedo pido quieres puedes pides quiere puede pide queremos podemos pedimos queréis podéis pedís quieren pueden piden More: e → ie: empezar (to start), pensar (to think), preferir (to prefer), cerrar (to close). o → ue: dormir (to sleep), volver (to come back), costar (to cost), encontrar (to find). e → i: repetir (to repeat), servir (to serve). u → ue: only jugar (to play): juego, juegas, juega, jugamos, jugáis, juegan. 2. Irregular yo form, the rest regular: Verb yo Example hacer (to do, to make) hago Hago la cena. salir (to go out, to leave) salgo Salgo a las ocho. poner (to put) pongo Pongo la mesa. traer (to bring) traigo Traigo vino. conocer (to know a person or place) conozco Conozco Madrid. saber (to know a fact) sé No sé. ver (to see) veo Veo la tele. 3. Fully irregular: ir (to go): voy, vas, va, vamos, vais, van. Usually with a (remember a + el = al): Voy a la playa. Vamos al cine. When to use it Saying what you want and can do. Quiero un café. ¿Puedes venir mañana? (Can you come tomorrow?) Talking about your week. Los sábados juego al fútbol y salgo con amigos. Saying where you go. Voy al trabajo en autobús. (I go to work by bus.) Asking for things. Pido una pizza. (I order a pizza.) Examples Spanish English ¿Quieres un té? Would you like a cup of tea? No puedo, trabajo mañana. I can't, I'm working tomorrow. ¿A qué hora empieza la clase? What time does the class start? Los domingos duermo mucho. On Sundays I sleep a lot. ¿Cuánto cuesta? How much is it? Mis hijos juegan al tenis. My kids play tennis. Hago deporte dos veces a la semana. I exercise twice a week. Vamos a la playa en agosto. We go to the beach in August. Good to know Jugar a + game: jugar al fútbol, jugar a las cartas. In parts of Latin America people also say jugar fútbol. Saber is for facts and skills (Sé nadar, I can swim); conocer is for people and places (Conozco a Ana). Before a person, conocer takes a. Pedir is \"to ask for\" something; preguntar is \"to ask\" a question. Common mistakes ✗ Yo quero un café. → ✓ Yo quiero un café. — querer changes e to ie in the stem. ✗ Nosotros quieremos. → ✓ Nosotros queremos. — no stem change in nosotros and vosotros. ✗ Yo hace la cena. → ✓ Yo hago la cena. — the yo form of hacer is hago. ✗ Yo sabo. → ✓ Yo sé. — the yo form of saber is sé. ✗ Conozco que es tarde. → ✓ Sé que es tarde. — facts take saber; conocer is for people and places. Practice Complete with the right form. Yo no (poder) venir hoy. ¿Tú (querer) un café? Nosotros (dormir) ocho horas. Yo (hacer) la cena. Ellos (ir) a la playa. Translate into Spanish. I'm going out with my friends. (salir) What time does the movie start? (empezar) I don't know. Answers puedo quieres dormimos hago van Salgo con mis amigos. ¿A qué hora empieza la película? No sé.","level":"A1","category":"Verbs: tenses and moods","route":"#/a1/verbs-present-irregular"},{"id":"page:a1-verbs-reflexive-routine","group":"pages","title":"Reflexive verbs for the daily routine","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Me levanto, me ducho, me acuesto. Reflexive verbs carry a small pronoun that changes with the person.","text":"Many everyday actions are reflexive in Spanish: you do them to yourself. English often says \"I get up\", \"I shower\", \"I go to bed\" with no extra word; Spanish adds a small pronoun: me levanto, me ducho, me acuesto. You already know one reflexive verb: llamarse (me llamo Ana). In the dictionary these verbs end in -se: levantarse, ducharse. How it works The pronoun (me, te, se, nos, os, se) goes before the conjugated verb. Person levantarse (to get up) ducharse (to shower) yo me levanto me ducho tú te levantas te duchas él, ella, usted se levanta se ducha nosotros, nosotras nos levantamos nos duchamos vosotros, vosotras (Spain) os levantáis os ducháis ellos, ellas, ustedes se levantan se duchan Negative: no before the pronoun: No me levanto temprano. Question: ¿A qué hora te levantas? (What time do you get up?) Some are also stem-changing: despertarse (e → ie): me despierto; acostarse (o → ue): me acuesto; vestirse (e → i): me visto. Routine verbs: despertarse (to wake up), levantarse (to get up), ducharse (to shower), lavarse (to wash; lavarse los dientes = to brush your teeth, also cepillarse los dientes), vestirse (to get dressed), peinarse (to comb your hair), acostarse (to go to bed), dormirse (to fall asleep). Not reflexive, but part of the day: desayunar (to have breakfast), comer (to eat; in Spain and Mexico also \"to have lunch\"; many Latin American countries say almorzar), cenar (to have dinner), trabajar, salir de casa, volver a casa. When to use it Describing your day. Me levanto a las siete y me ducho. (I get up at seven and shower.) Asking about someone's routine. ¿A qué hora te acuestas? (What time do you go to bed?) Saying what you do to your body. Me lavo las manos. (I wash my hands.) Spanish uses las, not mis: the pronoun already shows whose hands. Examples Spanish English Me despierto a las seis y media. I wake up at six thirty. ¿Te duchas por la mañana o por la noche? Do you shower in the morning or at night? Mi hijo se viste solo. My son gets dressed on his own. Nos acostamos tarde los sábados. We go to bed late on Saturdays. Desayuno café con pan tostado. I have coffee and toast for breakfast. Mis padres se levantan muy temprano. My parents get up very early. No me peino nunca. I never comb my hair. ¿A qué hora os levantáis? (Spain) What time do you (all) get up? Good to know With an infinitive, the pronoun can go at the end and joins the verb: Quiero ducharme. = Me quiero duchar. (I want to take a shower.) Lunch is the main meal in Spain and much of Latin America; in Spain it is often around 2 p.m. More on times in . Common mistakes ✗ Levanto a las siete. → ✓ Me levanto a las siete. — \"get up\" needs the reflexive pronoun. ✗ Me lavo mis dientes. → ✓ Me lavo los dientes. — with body parts Spanish uses los / las, not mis. ✗ Ella me ducha. (meaning \"she showers\") → ✓ Ella se ducha. — the pronoun matches the person: se for ella. ✗ Me acosto a las once. → ✓ Me acuesto a las once. — acostarse changes o to ue. Practice Complete with the pronoun and the verb. Yo (levantarse) a las siete. ¿Tú (ducharse) por la mañana? Ana (acostarse) tarde. Nosotros (despertarse) temprano. Translate into Spanish. I get dressed. What time do you go to bed? (to a friend) My parents get up at six. I brush my teeth. Answers me levanto te duchas se acuesta nos despertamos Me visto. ¿A qué hora te acuestas? Mis padres se levantan a las seis. Me lavo los dientes.","level":"A1","category":"Verbs: tenses and moods","route":"#/a1/verbs-reflexive-routine"},{"id":"page:a1-functions-time-dates","group":"pages","title":"Tell the time, give dates and talk about schedules","headings":["Key phrases","Dialogue","Culture note","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Ask and say the time, give the date, and ask when shops open and things start.","text":"To tell the time Spanish uses ser and the article la / las: Es la una. Son las tres. \"At\" a time is a la / a las: a las ocho. Dates are simple: el + number + de + month. Two things differ from English: there is no word for \"o'clock\" (en punto only stresses \"exactly\"), and in Spain times after the half hour are usually said as \"to\" the next hour (las cinco menos cuarto). In daily life in Latin America, many people say the minutes after the hour instead (las cuatro y cuarenta y cinco) or un cuarto para las cinco (a quarter to five). Key phrases Asking and telling the time Spanish English ¿Qué hora es? What time is it? Es la una. It's one o'clock. Son las dos. / Son las diez. It's two. / It's ten. Son las tres y cuarto. It's a quarter past three. Son las tres y media. It's half past three. Son las cuatro menos cuarto. It's a quarter to four. Son las cinco y diez. / Son las seis menos veinte. It's ten past five. / It's twenty to six. Es mediodía. / Es medianoche. It's noon. / It's midnight. de la mañana / de la tarde / de la noche a.m. / p.m. (afternoon) / p.m. (night) When? Schedules Spanish English ¿A qué hora abre la farmacia? What time does the pharmacy open? Abre a las nueve y cierra a las ocho. It opens at nine and closes at eight. ¿A qué hora empieza la película? What time does the movie start? A las diez en punto. At ten o'clock sharp. de nueve a cinco from nine to five por la mañana / por la tarde / por la noche in the morning / in the afternoon / at night (Latin America also: en la mañana, en la noche) Dates Spanish English ¿Qué día es hoy? What day is it today? Hoy es martes, 5 de mayo. Today is Tuesday, May 5. ¿Cuándo es tu cumpleaños? When is your birthday? Es el 12 de octubre. It's October 12. el uno de enero / el primero de enero January 1 Dialogue Two friends plan to see a movie. Lucía: ¿Qué hora es? (What time is it?) Javier: Son las seis y media. (It's half past six.) Lucía: ¿A qué hora empieza la película? (What time does the movie start?) Javier: A las ocho menos cuarto. (At a quarter to eight.) Lucía: Perfecto. ¿Y qué día es el concierto de Ana? (Perfect. And what day is Ana's concert?) Javier: El sábado, 14 de junio, a las nueve de la noche. (On Saturday, June 14, at nine p.m.) Lucía: ¡Genial! (Great!) Culture note Timetables, trains and shops use the 24-hour clock: El tren sale a las 18:30 (a las dieciocho treinta). In Spain lunch is often at 2 p.m. and dinner at 9 or 10 p.m.; la tarde lasts until it gets dark. Common mistakes ✗ Son la una. → ✓ Es la una. — one o'clock is singular: es la una. ✗ Es las tres. → ✓ Son las tres. — from two o'clock on, the verb is son. ✗ en las ocho → ✓ a las ocho — \"at\" a time is a. ✗ mayo 5 → ✓ el 5 de mayo — the day comes first, then de and the month. Practice Write the time in words. 1:00 3:15 7:30 9:45 Translate into Spanish. What time is it? The bank opens at nine. My birthday is (on) March 3. at night Answers Es la una. Son las tres y cuarto. Son las siete y media. Son las diez menos cuarto. / Son las nueve y cuarenta y cinco. ¿Qué hora es? El banco abre a las nueve. Mi cumpleaños es el 3 de marzo. por la noche / en la noche / de noche","level":"A1","category":"Communication","route":"#/a1/functions-time-dates"},{"id":"page:a1-vocabulary-food-shopping","group":"pages","title":"Food, drink, shops and prices","headings":["Words","Word partners","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Everyday food and drink, the shops that sell them, quantities and how to ask what something costs.","text":"Food is the easiest place to practice: you buy it every day. Learn each noun with its article, and notice two habits. Spanish talks about food in general with the article (Me gusta el pan), but asks for an amount without it (Quiero pan). And prices use costar (o → ue): ¿Cuánto cuesta? for one thing, ¿Cuánto cuestan? for several. Words Food Spanish English el pan bread el arroz rice la pasta pasta la carne meat el pollo chicken el pescado fish (as food) el huevo egg el queso cheese el jamón ham la fruta fruit la manzana apple el plátano (Spain, Mexico) / la banana, el banano (other parts of Latin America) banana la naranja orange el tomate tomato la patata (Spain) / la papa (Latin America) potato la verdura / las verduras vegetables la ensalada salad Drinks Spanish English el agua (f.) water el café / el café con leche coffee / coffee with milk el té tea la leche milk el zumo (Spain) / el jugo (Latin America) juice el vino / la cerveza wine / beer Shops and amounts Spanish English el supermercado supermarket el mercado market la panadería bakery la frutería fruit store un kilo de a kilo of medio kilo de half a kilo of una botella de a bottle of un paquete de a package of una barra de pan a loaf of bread (a baguette) el euro / el peso / el dólar euro / peso / dollar Word partners Spanish English ¿Cuánto cuesta el queso? How much is the cheese? ¿Cuánto cuestan las manzanas? How much are the apples? Cuesta tres euros con cincuenta. It costs three euros fifty. Un kilo de tomates, por favor. A kilo of tomatoes, please. Quiero una botella de agua. I'd like a bottle of water. No como carne. I don't eat meat. Tomo el café con leche y sin azúcar. I take my coffee with milk and no sugar. ¿Algo más? —No, nada más, gracias. Anything else? —No, nothing else, thanks. Me gusta mucho la fruta. I really like fruit. Good to know El pescado is fish as food; a live fish is el pez. Many prices are said with con: 2,50 € is dos euros con cincuenta (or dos cincuenta). Spain and much of Latin America write a decimal comma: 2,50; Mexico and some other countries use a decimal point: 2.50. Papa (potato) is Latin American; in Spain it is patata. Jugo is the word for juice in Latin America; zumo is the word in Spain. Common mistakes ✗ ¿Cuánto cuesta los tomates? → ✓ ¿Cuánto cuestan los tomates? — the verb agrees with the plural noun. ✗ Me gusta café. → ✓ Me gusta el café. — food in general takes the article after gustar. ✗ un kilo tomates → ✓ un kilo de tomates — amounts need de. ✗ la agua → ✓ el agua — agua is feminine but takes el in the singular. Practice Write the word with the article. bread cheese water chicken Translate into Spanish. How much are the oranges? A kilo of apples, please. I don't eat fish. A bottle of milk. Answers el pan el queso el agua el pollo ¿Cuánto cuestan las naranjas? Un kilo de manzanas, por favor. No como pescado. Una botella de leche.","level":"A1","category":"Vocabulary","route":"#/a1/vocabulary-food-shopping"},{"id":"page:a1-grammar-possessives-demonstratives","group":"pages","title":"Possessives and demonstratives","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"My, your, his, our (mi, tu, su, nuestro) and this, that, that over there (este, ese, aquel).","text":"You have already used mi and mis in sentences like Mi hermana es alta. This page shows the whole set: mi, tu, su, nuestro, vuestro. They agree with the thing owned, not with the owner: mis libros (my books), nuestra casa (our house). It also shows the words for \"this\" and \"that\". Spanish has three distances instead of two: este (this, near me), ese (that, near you) and aquel (that, far from both of us). How it works Possessives (before the noun): Owner one thing several things yo mi casa mis libros tú tu casa tus libros él, ella, usted su casa sus libros nosotros, nosotras nuestro coche / nuestra casa nuestros amigos / nuestras amigas vosotros, vosotras (Spain) vuestro coche / vuestra casa vuestros amigos / vuestras amigas ellos, ellas, ustedes su casa sus libros Mi, tu, su change only for the plural (mis, tus, sus). Nuestro and vuestro also change for the feminine. Tu (your) has no accent; tú (you) has one. Su can mean his, her, its, your (formal) or their. If it is unclear, use de + the person: la casa de Ana, el coche de ustedes. Demonstratives (they agree with the noun): near me near you far from both masculine este libro ese libro aquel libro feminine esta mesa esa mesa aquella mesa masculine plural estos libros esos libros aquellos libros feminine plural estas mesas esas mesas aquellas mesas Without a noun they mean \"this one\", \"that one\": Quiero este. (I want this one.) Esto, eso (and aquello, far away) refer to something unknown or an idea: ¿Qué es esto? (What is this?) Eso es verdad. (That's true.) Aquí (here), ahí (there, near you) and allí (over there) match the three distances. When to use it Talking about your things and people. Mi casa es pequeña. Mis padres viven en Lima. Asking about someone else's. ¿Cómo se llama tu perro? ¿Dónde está su hotel, señora? Pointing in a shop or a café. ¿Cuánto cuesta esta camisa? Quiero ese pastel. Something far away. ¿Ves aquella casa? Es de mis abuelos. (Do you see that house? It's my grandparents'.) An unknown thing or an idea. ¿Qué es eso? (What's that?) Examples Spanish English Mi madre es profesora y mis hermanos son estudiantes. My mother is a teacher and my brothers and sisters are students. ¿Es tu coche? Is it your car? Nuestra casa está cerca del centro. Our house is near downtown. Señor López, ¿es su maleta? Mr. López, is it your suitcase? Este café está muy bueno. This coffee is very good. ¿Te gustan esas fotos? Do you like those photos (near you)? Aquellos edificios son nuevos. Those buildings (over there) are new. ¿Qué es esto? What's this? Good to know With body parts and clothes, Spanish often uses el / la instead of mi: Me lavo las manos. Texts written before 2010 often have an accent on éste, ése; today they are written without it. In Latin America aquel is used less in speech; ese often covers both distances. Common mistakes ✗ mi libros → ✓ mis libros — the possessive agrees with the things, which are plural. ✗ nuestro casa → ✓ nuestra casa — nuestro agrees with the feminine casa. ✗ Esto libro es bueno. → ✓ Este libro es bueno. — before a noun, use este, not esto. ✗ la mi casa → ✓ mi casa — no article before mi, tu, su. Practice Write the possessive. (yo) amigos (nosotros) casa (tú) hermana (ellos) padres Write the right form of este or ese. (this) mesa (these) libros (that, near you) chaqueta Translate: What's that? Answers mis amigos nuestra casa tu hermana sus padres esta mesa estos libros esa chaqueta ¿Qué es eso?","level":"A1","category":"Grammar","route":"#/a1/grammar-possessives-demonstratives"},{"id":"page:a1-grammar-prepositions-place-time","group":"pages","title":"Basic prepositions of place and time","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"en, a, de, con, entre, desde, hasta, para; with places, times, days, months and seasons.","text":"A few short words do most of the work: en, a, de, con, and they do not match English one to one. En covers \"in\", \"on\" and \"at\" for places (en casa, en la mesa), but \"at\" a clock time is a (a las tres). And with days Spanish uses no preposition at all: el lunes means \"on Monday\". Learn them in phrases, not as single words. How it works Place Spanish English Example en in, on, at (a place) en casa, en la mesa, en el trabajo a to (movement) Voy a Madrid. Voy al banco. de from, of Soy de Perú. el libro de Ana desde … hasta from … to desde Lima hasta Cusco entre between entre el banco y la farmacia con / sin with / without café con leche, sin azúcar para for, to (a purpose, a person) un regalo para ti (a present for you) Location phrases with de: cerca de, lejos de, al lado de, enfrente de (opposite), delante de (in front of), detrás de (behind), encima de (on top of), debajo de (under). Remember de + el = del. Time Spanish English a las tres at three por la mañana / tarde / noche (Latin America also: en la mañana) in the morning / afternoon / at night el lunes / los lunes on Monday / on Mondays en julio, en verano, en 2026 in July, in summer, in 2026 de nueve a cinco from nine to five desde las ocho / hasta las ocho from (since) eight / until eight antes de / después de comer before / after eating After a preposition (but not entre: entre tú y yo), the pronouns yo and tú become mí and ti: para mí, para ti. With con: conmigo, contigo. When to use it Where something is: en. Las llaves están en la mesa. (The keys are on the table.) Where you go: a. Vamos a la playa. (We're going to the beach.) Where something comes from: de. El tren de Sevilla llega a las diez. When: a las, por la, el, en. Trabajo por la mañana. En agosto no trabajo. Who with or for: con, para. ¿Vienes conmigo? Este regalo es para ti. Examples Spanish English Estoy en el trabajo. I'm at work. Voy a la oficina en autobús. I go to the office by bus. La clase es de diez a doce. The class is from ten to twelve. Nos vemos el sábado por la tarde. See you on Saturday afternoon. En invierno hace frío. It's cold in winter. El gato está debajo de la cama. The cat is under the bed. ¿Quieres venir conmigo? Do you want to come with me? Me lavo los dientes después de comer. I brush my teeth after eating. Good to know Means of transport take en: en coche, en tren, en avión. But \"on foot\" is a pie or caminando. Common mistakes ✗ en el lunes → ✓ el lunes — no preposition with days. ✗ en las tres → ✓ a las tres — \"at\" a clock time is a. ✗ Voy en la playa. → ✓ Voy a la playa. — movement to a place takes a. ✗ con yo → ✓ conmigo — \"with me\" is one word. ✗ para tú → ✓ para ti — after a preposition tú becomes ti. Practice Write the preposition or the article. Vivo Madrid. Voy la universidad. La tienda abre las nueve. Trabajo la mañana. Mi cumpleaños es mayo. Nos vemos viernes. Translate into Spanish. with me a coffee with milk and no sugar Answers en a a por en el (no preposition, but the article: nos vemos el viernes) conmigo un café con leche sin azúcar / un café con leche y sin azúcar","level":"A1","category":"Grammar","route":"#/a1/grammar-prepositions-place-time"},{"id":"page:a1-verbs-ir-a-infinitive","group":"pages","title":"ir a + infinitive: plans","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Voy a visitar a mi madre. Talk about plans and intentions with ir a + an infinitive, like English \"going to\".","text":"You already know ir (to go): voy, vas, va, vamos, vais, van. Put a and an infinitive after it and you have the easiest way to talk about the future: Voy a estudiar (I'm going to study). It works just like English \"going to\", so it is the first future form most learners use. Vamos a + infinitive can also mean \"let's\": Vamos a ver. (Let's see.) How it works ir (present) + a + infinitive Person ir a + infinitive English yo voy a comer I'm going to eat tú vas a comer you're going to eat él, ella, usted va a comer he / she is going to eat, you're going to eat nosotros, nosotras vamos a comer we're going to eat vosotros, vosotras (Spain) vais a comer you're going to eat ellos, ellas, ustedes van a comer they're going to eat, you're going to eat Only ir changes; a and the infinitive stay the same. Negative: No voy a salir. (I'm not going to go out.) Question: ¿Qué vas a hacer mañana? (What are you going to do tomorrow?) Reflexive verbs: the pronoun goes before ir or joins the infinitive: Me voy a acostar. = Voy a acostarme. Time words for the future: mañana (tomorrow), pasado mañana (the day after tomorrow), esta tarde (this afternoon), esta noche (tonight), este fin de semana (this weekend), la semana que viene / la próxima semana (next week), el año que viene (next year). When to use it Plans. Este verano voy a viajar a México. (This summer I'm going to travel to Mexico.) Intentions. Voy a estudiar más. (I'm going to study more.) Something you can see is about to happen. ¡Cuidado! Te vas a caer. (Careful! You're going to fall.) Suggestions with vamos a. Vamos a empezar. (Let's start.) Asking about plans. ¿Qué vais a hacer el sábado? (Spain) / ¿Qué van a hacer el sábado? Examples Spanish English Voy a visitar a mi madre. I'm going to visit my mother. ¿Qué vas a hacer esta noche? What are you going to do tonight? Mañana vamos a comer en un restaurante. Tomorrow we're going to eat in a restaurant. Mis amigos van a venir a las ocho. My friends are going to come at eight. No voy a trabajar el lunes. I'm not going to work on Monday. Va a llover. It's going to rain. Vamos a ver. Let's see. ¿Vas a venir a la fiesta? Are you going to come to the party? Good to know When the object of the verb is a person, add a: Voy a visitar a mi madre, llamar a Ana (but visitar Roma). This \"personal a\" has no English equivalent. Voy a la playa (I'm going to the beach) is ir a + a place, not a plan with an infinitive. Both use a. The present can also talk about fixed plans: Mañana trabajo. Ir a puts the intention first. See the card ir a + infinitivo in the Tense & Mood Lab. Common mistakes ✗ Voy comer. → ✓ Voy a comer. — do not leave out a. ✗ Vamos a salimos. → ✓ Vamos a salir. — after a comes the infinitive. ✗ Yo va a estudiar. → ✓ Yo voy a estudiar. — ir changes with the person. ✗ Voy a visitar mi madre. → ✓ Voy a visitar a mi madre. — a person after visitar takes a. Practice Complete with ir a + infinitive. Yo (estudiar) esta tarde. ¿Tú (venir) mañana? Nosotros (comer) en casa. Ellos no (trabajar) el sábado. Translate into Spanish. What are you going to do tomorrow? (to a friend) I'm going to call my sister. It's going to rain. Let's start. Answers voy a estudiar vas a venir vamos a comer van a trabajar ¿Qué vas a hacer mañana? Voy a llamar a mi hermana. Va a llover. Vamos a empezar.","level":"A1","category":"Verbs: tenses and moods","route":"#/a1/verbs-ir-a-infinitive"},{"id":"page:a1-functions-ordering-shopping","group":"pages","title":"Order in a café and buy things","headings":["Key phrases","Dialogue","Culture note","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Order food and drinks, ask for the bill, and buy things in a shop politely.","text":"In a café or a shop a few polite phrases do everything. Spanish speakers often order with a simple verb: Un café, por favor or Ponme un café (Spain, informal). The most polite way for a learner is Quería… (I'd like…), a past form used here as a soft request; you do not need to know the tense to use it. Waiters often use usted, and customers answer with usted too. Key phrases Ordering Spanish English Quería un café con leche, por favor. I'd like a coffee with milk, please. Para mí, un té. / Yo, un agua. For me, a tea. / I'll have a water. ¿Me pone un bocadillo de queso? (Spain) Can I have a cheese sandwich? ¿Me da una empanada? Can I have an empanada? ¿Qué tiene de comer? What do you have to eat? ¿Tiene algo sin carne? Do you have anything without meat? Una botella de agua sin gas / con gas. A bottle of still / sparkling water. The waiter asks Spanish English ¿Qué va a tomar? What will you have? ¿Algo más? Anything else? ¿Para tomar aquí o para llevar? For here or to go? Paying Spanish English La cuenta, por favor. The check, please. ¿Cuánto es? How much is it? ¿Me cobra, por favor? Can I pay, please? ¿Puedo pagar con tarjeta? Can I pay by card? En efectivo. In cash. In a shop (learn these as fixed phrases) Spanish English Estoy buscando una camiseta. I'm looking for a T-shirt. (playera in Mexico, remera in Argentina) ¿Cuánto cuesta esta? How much is this one? ¿La tiene en azul? Do you have it in blue? Me la llevo. I'll take it. Solo estoy mirando, gracias. I'm just looking, thanks. Dialogue In a café in the morning. Waiter: Buenos días. ¿Qué va a tomar? (Good morning. What will you have?) Laura: Quería un café con leche y una tostada, por favor. (I'd like a coffee with milk and a piece of toast, please.) Waiter: ¿Con tomate o con mantequilla? (With tomato or with butter?) Laura: Con tomate. (With tomato.) Waiter: Muy bien. ¿Algo más? (Very well. Anything else?) Laura: No, nada más, gracias. (No, nothing else, thanks.) Waiter: Muy bien. (Very well.) Laura: La cuenta, por favor. ¿Puedo pagar con tarjeta? (The check, please. Can I pay by card?) Waiter: Sí, claro. Son tres euros con cincuenta. (Yes, of course. It's three euros fifty.) Culture note A bocadillo in Spain is a sandwich in a baguette; a sándwich is made with sliced bread. In Mexico a similar sandwich is a torta. In Spain you often pay at the end at the bar, and tips are small; in Mexico about 10–15% is usual. Por favor and gracias are used less often than in English in quick exchanges (especially in Spain); a friendly tone does the same job. Common mistakes ✗ Quiero tener un café. → ✓ Quería un café. / Un café, por favor. — you just name what you want. ✗ Para yo, un café. → ✓ Para mí, un café. — after para, yo becomes mí. ✗ ¿Puedo pagar por tarjeta? → ✓ ¿Puedo pagar con tarjeta? — you pay con a card, en cash. ✗ Una agua, por favor. → ✓ Un agua, por favor. — agua is feminine but takes un / el. Practice Write what you say. You want a tea. (polite) You want the check. You ask if you can pay by card. The waiter says ¿Algo más? You don't want anything else. Complete the dialogue. —¿Qué va a ? —Un café, por favor. —¿Para tomar aquí o para ? —Para tomar aquí. —¿Cuánto ? —Son dos euros. Translate: I'm just looking, thanks. Answers Quería un té, por favor. / Un té, por favor. La cuenta, por favor. ¿Puedo pagar con tarjeta? No, nada más, gracias. tomar llevar es Solo estoy mirando, gracias.","level":"A1","category":"Communication","route":"#/a1/functions-ordering-shopping"},{"id":"page:a1-functions-asking-to-repeat","group":"pages","title":"Survival phrases: ask people to repeat, slow down, explain","headings":["Key phrases","Dialogue","Culture note","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"The phrases that keep a conversation going when you don't understand: repeat, slower, how do you say, what does it mean.","text":"Real Spanish is fast, and you will not understand everything. That is normal, and these phrases are your safety net. They let you keep talking instead of going silent. Most of them use poder (can): ¿Puede repetir? (formal) or ¿Puedes repetir? (informal). Learn both versions, and keep the formal one for strangers, shops and offices. Key phrases When you don't understand Spanish English Perdón, no entiendo. Sorry, I don't understand. ¿Puede repetir, por favor? / ¿Puedes repetir? Can you repeat that, please? Más despacio, por favor. (also más lento, common in Latin America) Slower, please. ¿Puede hablar más despacio? / ¿Puedes hablar más despacio? Can you speak more slowly? ¿Cómo? / ¿Perdón? Sorry? (I didn't hear) No hablo mucho español. I don't speak much Spanish. ¿Habla inglés? / ¿Hablas inglés? Do you speak English? Asking about words Spanish English ¿Cómo se dice \"key\" en español? How do you say \"key\" in Spanish? ¿Qué significa \"llave\"? What does \"llave\" mean? ¿Cómo se escribe? How do you spell it? ¿Cómo se pronuncia? How do you pronounce it? ¿Puede escribirlo, por favor? Can you write it down, please? Checking and thanking Spanish English ¿Así? Like this? ¿Es correcto? Is that right? Ah, vale. (Spain) / Ah, okey. / Ah, ya entiendo. Oh, OK. / Oh, I get it now. Muchas gracias por su paciencia. Thank you very much for your patience. Dialogue At a train station ticket office. Clerk: Buenos días. ¿Adónde va? (Good morning. Where are you going?) Tom: Perdón, no entiendo. ¿Puede repetir, por favor? (Sorry, I don't understand. Can you repeat that, please?) Clerk: Claro. ¿A qué ciudad va? (Of course. Which city are you going to?) Tom: A Toledo. ¿Cómo se dice \"return ticket\" en español? (To Toledo. How do you say \"return ticket\" in Spanish?) Clerk: Ida y vuelta. (Round trip.) Tom: Ah, vale. Un billete de ida y vuelta, por favor. (Oh, OK. A round-trip ticket, please.) Clerk: Son veinte euros con cuarenta. (That's twenty euros forty.) Tom: Más despacio, por favor. (Slower, please.) Clerk: Veinte… euros… con cuarenta. (Twenty… euros… forty.) Tom: Muchas gracias. (Thank you very much.) Culture note Vale is the Spain word for \"OK\"; in much of Latin America people say está bien, bueno, ok or dale (Argentina, Uruguay). A ticket for a train or a bus is un billete in Spain and un boleto in most of Latin America. People are usually happy to repeat. Showing you are trying is more important than speaking perfectly. Common mistakes ✗ No comprendo nada, repite. (to a stranger) → ✓ Perdón, no entiendo. ¿Puede repetir? — too direct with a stranger: use usted and a question. ✗ ¿Qué significa en español \"key\"? → ✓ ¿Cómo se dice \"key\" en español? — significa asks for the meaning of a word you heard. ✗ ¿Puede repetir, por favor. → ✓ ¿Puede repetir, por favor? — a question opens with ¿ and closes with ?. Practice What do you say? You don't understand. Someone speaks too fast. (to a stranger) You want to know how to say \"bread\" in Spanish. You want to know what \"llave\" means. Translate into Spanish. Can you repeat that, please? (formal) How do you spell it? Can you write it down, please? (formal) I don't speak much Spanish. Answers Perdón, no entiendo. / No entiendo. ¿Puede hablar más despacio, por favor? / Más despacio, por favor. ¿Cómo se dice \"bread\" en español? ¿Qué significa \"llave\"? ¿Puede repetir, por favor? ¿Cómo se escribe? ¿Puede escribirlo, por favor? No hablo mucho español.","level":"A1","category":"Communication","route":"#/a1/functions-asking-to-repeat"},{"id":"page:a2-verbs-preterite-regular","group":"pages","title":"Pretérito indefinido: regular verbs","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Hablé, comiste, vivió: the simple past for finished actions, with ayer, anoche and el año pasado.","text":"The preterite (pretérito indefinido) is the Spanish simple past: Ayer hablé con Marta. (Yesterday I talked to Marta.) It tells what happened at a finished moment, like English \"talked\" or \"ate\". English needs \"did\" for questions and negatives; Spanish does not: ¿Hablaste con ella? (Did you talk to her?) Watch the accents: hablo is \"I speak\", habló is \"he / she / you spoke\". How it works Drop -ar, -er or -ir and add the ending; -er and -ir share one set. Person hablar comer vivir yo hablé comí viví tú hablaste comiste viviste él, ella, usted habló comió vivió nosotros, nosotras hablamos comimos vivimos vosotros, vosotras (Spain) hablasteis comisteis vivisteis ellos, ellas, ustedes hablaron comieron vivieron The yo and él / ella / usted forms have a written accent: hablé, habló, comí, comió. Hablamos and vivimos look like the present; the time word tells you which: Ayer hablamos. The tú form has no -s at the end: hablaste. Stem-changing verbs in -ar and -er do not change in the preterite: cerrar → cerré, volver → volví, jugar → jugué. Spelling changes in the yo form: Infinitive yo él, ella, usted buscar, tocar (-car) busqué, toqué buscó, tocó llegar, pagar (-gar) llegué, pagué llegó, pagó empezar (-zar) empecé empezó Leer: leyó, leyeron. Time words: ayer (yesterday), anteayer (the day before yesterday), anoche (last night), el lunes pasado (last Monday), la semana pasada (last week), el año pasado (last year), hace dos años (two years ago). When to use it A finished action. Ayer compré pan. (Yesterday I bought bread.) Actions one after the other. Llegué a casa, cené y me acosté. (I got home, had dinner and went to bed.) A closed period. Viví tres años en Lima. (I lived in Lima for three years.) Questions and negatives, without \"did\". ¿Cenaste en casa? —No, no cené. (Did you have dinner at home? No.) \"Ago\": hace. Terminé la universidad hace cinco años. (I finished college five years ago.) Examples Spanish English Anoche cenamos en un restaurante peruano. Last night we had dinner at a Peruvian restaurant. ¿A qué hora llegaste? What time did you arrive? Mis padres vivieron dos años en Chile. My parents lived in Chile for two years. Ayer no bebí café. I didn't drink coffee yesterday. El concierto empezó a las nueve. The concert started at nine. Busqué las llaves por toda la casa. I looked for the keys all over the house. Good to know In Spain, for things that happened earlier today (hoy, esta mañana), people usually use another past tense: see . In most of Latin America the preterite is normal for today too: Hoy hablé con Marta. With ayer or el año pasado, everyone uses the preterite. Common mistakes ✗ Ella hablo con el profesor. → ✓ Ella habló con el profesor. — without the accent, hablo means \"I speak\". ✗ ¿Comistes en casa? → ✓ ¿Comiste en casa? — the tú form of the preterite has no final -s. ✗ Ayer como con mis amigos. → ✓ Ayer comí con mis amigos. — a finished past action needs the preterite. ✗ Anoche comé pizza. → ✓ Anoche comí pizza. — -er verbs take -í, not the -ar ending -é. ✗ Buscé las llaves. → ✓ Busqué las llaves. — -car becomes -qué in the yo form. Practice Write the preterite. Ayer yo (trabajar) hasta las seis. ¿Tú (comer) en casa anoche? Mi hermano (vivir) dos años en Bogotá. Yo (pagar) la cuenta. La película (empezar) tarde. Translate into Spanish. Did you buy bread? (to a friend) I finished my work two hours ago. Answers trabajé comiste vivió pagué empezó ¿Compraste pan? Terminé mi trabajo hace dos horas. / Hace dos horas terminé mi trabajo.","level":"A2","category":"Verbs: tenses and moods","route":"#/a2/verbs-preterite-regular"},{"id":"page:a2-verbs-preterite-irregular","group":"pages","title":"Pretérito indefinido: common irregular verbs","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Fui, hice, tuve, estuve, dije, pude, vine: the irregular preterites you need every day, with new stems and no written accents.","text":"The most frequent verbs are irregular in the preterite, just as English has \"went\", \"did\" and \"had\". Most of them follow one pattern: a new stem (tener → tuv-) and one set of endings without written accents: tuve, tuvo. Ser and ir share their forms, so fui means both \"I was\" and \"I went\". How it works ser and ir: the same forms Person ser / ir yo fui tú fuiste él, ella, usted fue nosotros, nosotras fuimos vosotros, vosotras (Spain) fuisteis ellos, ellas, ustedes fueron Fui a Lima. (I went to Lima.) Fue un viaje largo. (It was a long trip.) A new stem + -e, -iste, -o, -imos, -isteis, -ieron Infinitive Stem yo él, ella, usted ellos, ellas, ustedes tener tuv- tuve tuvo tuvieron estar estuv- estuve estuvo estuvieron poder pud- pude pudo pudieron poner pus- puse puso pusieron venir vin- vine vino vinieron hacer hic- hice hizo hicieron No written accents: tuve, pudo, hizo. Hacer writes z in hizo (to keep the sound). Stems in -j: the ellos form ends in -eron Infinitive Stem yo él, ella, usted ellos, ellas, ustedes decir dij- dije dijo dijeron traer traj- traje trajo trajeron dar and ver are short and have no accents: di, diste, dio, dimos, disteis, dieron; vi, viste, vio, vimos, visteis, vieron. -ir verbs with a stem change change only in the third person: pedir → pidió, pidieron; dormir → durmió, durmieron; also preferir → prefirió. The other forms are regular: pedí, dormí. When to use it Where you went. El verano pasado fuimos a Cartagena. (Last summer we went to Cartagena.) How something was, as a whole. La fiesta fue muy divertida. (The party was a lot of fun.) Where you were, and for how long. Estuve dos semanas en Perú. (I was in Peru for two weeks.) What you did, had or could do. Hice la cena. No pude venir. (I made dinner. I couldn't come.) What someone said. Ana dijo que sí. (Ana said yes.) Examples Spanish English ¿Qué hiciste el sábado? What did you do on Saturday? Fui al cine con Diego. I went to the movies with Diego. Mis primos vinieron a cenar. My cousins came for dinner. Estuvimos en casa todo el día. We were at home all day. ¿Por qué no pudiste venir? Why couldn't you come? Ayer tuve un examen. I had an exam yesterday. ¿Qué dijo el profesor? What did the teacher say? Sofía durmió nueve horas. Sofía slept for nine hours. Good to know For an event, fue and estuvo are both common: La fiesta fue / estuvo genial. (The party was great.) For where someone was, only estar: Estuve en Madrid. Hay becomes hubo: Hubo un accidente en la autopista. (There was an accident on the highway.) Ser or ir? Fue a + a place is \"went\"; fue + a description is \"was\". Common mistakes ✗ Ayer tení un examen. → ✓ Ayer tuve un examen. — tener has its own stem, tuv-. ✗ Mi padre hizó la cena. → ✓ Mi padre hizo la cena. — irregular preterites have no written accent. ✗ Mis amigos dijieron que no. → ✓ Mis amigos dijeron que no. — after j, the ending is -eron. ✗ Yo fue a la playa. → ✓ Yo fui a la playa. — fue is for él, ella, usted. ✗ Lucía pedió una pizza. → ✓ Lucía pidió una pizza. — pedir changes e → i in él, ella, usted. Practice Write the preterite. El verano pasado nosotros (ir) a Perú. ¿Qué (hacer) tú ayer? Mi madre (estar) dos días en el hospital. Ellos no (poder) venir. Yo (tener) un problema en el trabajo. ¿Qué (decir) los profesores? Translate into Spanish. The concert was very long. My friends came on Sunday. Answers fuimos hiciste estuvo pudieron tuve dijeron El concierto fue muy largo. Mis amigos vinieron el domingo. / El domingo vinieron mis amigos.","level":"A2","category":"Verbs: tenses and moods","route":"#/a2/verbs-preterite-irregular"},{"id":"page:a2-functions-weekend-vacation","group":"pages","title":"Say what you did last weekend or on vacation","headings":["Key phrases","Dialogue","Culture note","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Ask about someone's weekend or trip, tell what you did in order, say how it was and react.","text":"\"How was your weekend?\" is a typical Monday question, and now you have the tense to answer it: El sábado fui a la playa y comí paella. Tell the actions in order with primero, después and al final. \"I had a good time\" is Lo pasé bien. Key phrases Asking Spanish English ¿Qué tal el fin de semana? How was your weekend? ¿Qué hiciste el sábado? What did you do on Saturday? ¿Adónde fuiste de vacaciones? Where did you go on vacation? ¿Con quién fuiste? Who did you go with? ¿Cómo fue el viaje? How was the trip? ¿Cuánto tiempo estuviste allí? How long were you there? Answering Spanish English El sábado fui a la playa. On Saturday I went to the beach. Me quedé en casa y descansé. I stayed at home and rested. Salí con unos amigos. I went out with some friends. Visité a mis abuelos. I visited my grandparents. Fuimos de excursión a la montaña. We went on a trip to the mountains. Lo pasé muy bien. (Latin America also: La pasé muy bien.) I had a great time. Fue genial. / Fue un desastre. It was great. / It was a disaster. Me gustó mucho. I liked it a lot. Telling it in order Spanish English Primero… después… luego… al final… First… then… after that… in the end… Por la mañana… por la tarde… por la noche… (Latin America also: en la…) In the morning… in the afternoon… at night… Reacting Spanish English ¡Qué bien! / ¡Qué suerte! How nice! / Lucky you! ¡Qué pena! What a shame! Dialogue Monday morning at work in Madrid. Elena: ¡Hola, Tom! ¿Qué tal el fin de semana? (Hi, Tom! How was your weekend?) Tom: Muy bien, gracias. El sábado fui a Segovia con mi novia. (Really good, thanks. On Saturday I went to Segovia with my girlfriend.) Elena: ¡Qué bien! ¿Y qué hicisteis? (How nice! And what did you two do?) Tom: Primero visitamos el acueducto y luego comimos en un restaurante del centro. (First we visited the aqueduct and then we had lunch at a restaurant downtown.) Elena: ¿Y qué tal? (And how was it?) Tom: Lo pasamos muy bien. ¿Y tú? ¿Qué hiciste? (We had a great time. And you? What did you do?) Elena: Nada especial. Me quedé en casa porque llovió todo el fin de semana. (Nothing special. I stayed at home because it rained all weekend.) Tom: ¡Qué pena! (What a shame!) Culture note In Spain Elena says hicisteis (vosotros); in Latin America, ¿Y qué hicieron? Un puente is a long weekend: when a holiday falls on a Tuesday or a Thursday, many people in Spain take the Monday or Friday off too. Common mistakes ✗ Tuve un buen tiempo. → ✓ Lo pasé bien. — \"have a good time\" is not translated word for word. ✗ Fui en la playa. → ✓ Fui a la playa. — after ir, the place takes a. ✗ Visité mis abuelos. → ✓ Visité a mis abuelos. — a person after visitar takes a. ✗ ¿Qué hiciste en el sábado? → ✓ ¿Qué hiciste el sábado? — no preposition with days. Practice What do you say? Ask a friend how their weekend was. Say that you stayed at home and rested. Your friend Javier went to Mexico. Ask him: how long were you there? It rained all week on your friend's vacation. React. Complete. —¿Adónde de vacaciones? —A Costa Rica. Primero visitamos el museo y comimos en un restaurante. Translate into Spanish. I had a great time. We went to the mountains with some friends. Answers ¿Qué tal el fin de semana? / ¿Qué tal tu fin de semana? / ¿Cómo fue tu fin de semana? Me quedé en casa y descansé. ¿Cuánto tiempo estuviste allí? / ¿Cuánto tiempo estuviste en México? ¡Qué pena! fuiste después / luego Lo pasé muy bien. / La pasé muy bien. Fuimos a la montaña con unos amigos. / … con algunos amigos.","level":"A2","category":"Communication","route":"#/a2/functions-weekend-vacation"},{"id":"page:a2-vocabulary-travel-transport","group":"pages","title":"Travel, transportation and accommodation","headings":["Words","Word partners","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Trains, buses, planes, tickets and hotel rooms, with the Spain and Latin American words side by side.","text":"With the preterite you can now tell the story of a trip: Tomé el tren de las ocho, pero llegué tarde. Travel words differ between Spain and Latin America, so both are given. One verb needs care: coger (to take a bus) is normal in Spain but rude in parts of Latin America; tomar works everywhere. Words Getting around Spanish English el viaje the trip el tren the train el autobús (Mexico: el camión; Argentina: el colectivo) the bus el metro the subway el avión the plane el barco the boat, the ship el coche (Spain) / el carro (Latin America) / el auto (Argentina, Chile) the car el taxi the taxi la bicicleta the bicycle a pie on foot At the station and the airport Spanish English la estación the station el aeropuerto the airport el andén the platform el billete (Spain) / el boleto (Latin America) the ticket de ida / de ida y vuelta one-way / round-trip el horario the timetable, the schedule la salida / la llegada the departure / the arrival el vuelo the flight la puerta de embarque the gate la tarjeta de embarque (Spain) / el pase de abordar (Mexico, Central America) the boarding pass el pasaporte the passport la maleta (Argentina: la valija) the suitcase el equipaje the luggage el retraso (Latin America also: la demora) the delay Where you stay Spanish English el hotel the hotel el hostal the guesthouse, the hostel la habitación individual (Latin America also: sencilla) / doble the single / double room la reserva (Latin America also: la reservación) the reservation la recepción the front desk la llave the key desayuno incluido breakfast included Verbs Spanish English viajar to travel tomar (Spain also: coger) to take (a bus, a train) salir (de) to leave (from) llegar (a) to arrive (in, at) perder (el tren) to miss (the train) reservar to book quedarse (en) to stay (at) hacer la maleta (Latin America also: empacar) to pack facturar (Spain) / documentar (Mexico) to check (a bag) Word partners Spanish English Tomé el tren de las ocho. I took the eight o'clock train. Perdimos el autobús y llegamos tarde. We missed the bus and arrived late. El vuelo salió con una hora de retraso. The flight left an hour late. Llegué a Madrid el lunes. I arrived in Madrid on Monday. Reservé una habitación doble. I booked a double room. Compré un billete de ida y vuelta. (Spain) I bought a round-trip ticket. Fuimos en avión. We went by plane. Volví a casa a pie. I walked back home. Nos quedamos en un hotel cerca de la playa. We stayed at a hotel near the beach. Good to know Transportation takes en (en tren, en avión, en coche), but \"on foot\" is a pie. The place you arrive in takes a: llegar a Lima, llegar al hotel. English says \"in\" or \"at\"; Spanish has only a. El billete also means a banknote; in Latin America a ticket is el boleto or el pasaje. Common mistakes ✗ Llegué en Madrid a las diez. → ✓ Llegué a Madrid a las diez. — the place you arrive in takes a. ✗ Fuimos por avión. → ✓ Fuimos en avión. — transportation takes en. ✗ Volví en pie. → ✓ Volví a pie. — \"on foot\" is a pie. ✗ una doble habitación → ✓ una habitación doble — doble and individual go after the noun. Practice Write the word with el or la. ticket (Spain) platform flight suitcase Translate into Spanish. I missed the train. We arrived in Lima on Friday. A round-trip ticket, please. (Spain) She went to work by bus. Answers el billete el andén el vuelo la maleta / la valija Perdí el tren. Llegamos a Lima el viernes. / El viernes llegamos a Lima. Un billete de ida y vuelta, por favor. Fue al trabajo en autobús. / Fue a trabajar en autobús. (en camión, en colectivo)","level":"A2","category":"Vocabulary","route":"#/a2/vocabulary-travel-transport"},{"id":"page:a2-verbs-imperfect","group":"pages","title":"Pretérito imperfecto: descriptions and habits in the past","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Cuando era niño, jugaba en la calle. The past tense for how things used to be, what people were like and what they did regularly.","text":"The imperfect (pretérito imperfecto) describes the past instead of telling events: how things were and what people used to do. English uses \"used to\", \"would\" or the simple past: Cuando era niño, jugaba en la calle. (When I was a child, I used to play in the street.) Spanish usually needs no extra verb: the ending does the job. And it is the most regular tense in Spanish. How it works Person hablar comer vivir yo hablaba comía vivía tú hablabas comías vivías él, ella, usted hablaba comía vivía nosotros, nosotras hablábamos comíamos vivíamos vosotros, vosotras (Spain) hablabais comíais vivíais ellos, ellas, ustedes hablaban comían vivían Yo and él / ella / usted share one form: hablaba. Add the subject when it is not clear. -ía always has a written accent. In the -ar forms only nosotros has one: hablábamos. Stem-changing verbs are regular here: quería, podía, tenía. Only three irregular verbs: Person ser ir ver yo era iba veía tú eras ibas veías él, ella, usted era iba veía nosotros, nosotras éramos íbamos veíamos vosotros, vosotras (Spain) erais ibais veíais ellos, ellas, ustedes eran iban veían Hay becomes había (there was / there were): Antes había un cine aquí. When to use it Habits in the past. Todos los veranos íbamos a la playa. (Every summer we went to the beach.) Descriptions of people, places and things. Mi abuela era muy alta y tenía el pelo blanco. (My grandmother was very tall and had white hair.) Age and time in the past. Tenía diez años. Eran las nueve de la noche. (I was ten. It was nine p.m.) Then and now. Antes vivía en el centro; ahora vivo en las afueras. (I used to live downtown; now I live in the suburbs.) Feelings and states. Estaba cansada y no quería salir. (I was tired and didn't want to go out.) Signal words: antes (before, in the past), de niño / de niña (as a child), cuando era pequeño (when I was little), siempre, todos los días, normalmente. Examples Spanish English De niña, vivía en un pueblo pequeño. As a child, I lived in a small village. Mi padre trabajaba en un banco. My father worked in a bank. Los domingos comíamos en casa de mis abuelos. On Sundays we used to eat at my grandparents' house. La casa tenía un jardín muy bonito. The house had a very nice garden. ¿Qué querías ser de pequeño? What did you want to be when you were little? Antes había muchas tiendas en esta calle. There used to be a lot of stores on this street. Era un día muy bonito. It was a very nice day. Good to know De pequeño (Spain) and de chico (much of Latin America) both mean \"as a child\", like de niño. Common mistakes ✗ Usaba jugar en la calle. → ✓ Jugaba en la calle. — \"used to\" is usually just the imperfect ending. ✗ Cuando fui niño, vivía en Cádiz. → ✓ Cuando era niño, vivía en Cádiz. — childhood is a background description: era. ✗ Antes comia mucha carne. → ✓ Antes comía mucha carne. — -ía always has a written accent. ✗ Estaba diez años. → ✓ Tenía diez años. — age uses tener, in the past too. Practice Write the imperfect. De niño, yo (jugar) en el parque. Mis abuelos (vivir) en el campo. Nosotros (ir) a la playa todos los veranos. Mi profesora (ser) muy simpática. Antes (haber) un mercado en la plaza. Translate into Spanish. When I was little, I didn't like vegetables. My brother used to work in a hotel. Answers jugaba vivían íbamos era había Cuando era pequeño (pequeña, niño, niña), no me gustaban las verduras. Mi hermano trabajaba en un hotel. / Antes mi hermano trabajaba en un hotel.","level":"A2","category":"Verbs: tenses and moods","route":"#/a2/verbs-imperfect"},{"id":"page:a2-verbs-preterite-vs-imperfect-basics","group":"pages","title":"Indefinido vs imperfecto: first contrast","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Llovía cuando salí de casa. The imperfect sets the scene, the preterite tells what happened.","text":"Spanish has two simple past tenses, and English has one. \"It rained\" can be llovió or llovía, and the choice depends on what you want to say. Think of a film: the imperfect (llovía) describes the scene in the background; the preterite (salí) is the action that moves the story on. Llovía cuando salí de casa. (It was raining when I left home.) How it works Imperfect (hablaba) Preterite (hablé) the scene: what things were like the events: what happened an action in progress, without an end a finished action, often with a start or an end a habit: what used to happen one time, or a counted number of times siempre, todos los días, normalmente, antes ayer, una vez, el lunes, de repente (suddenly), tres veces The basic pattern: an action in progress (imperfect) is interrupted by a new event (preterite). Cenábamos cuando llamó Ana. (We were having dinner when Ana called.) The imperfect can often be translated \"was / were + -ing\": Leía = I was reading. The same verb changes meaning with the tense: Fue un día largo (the day as a whole, now over) / Era un día de sol (description of the setting). When to use it Background + event. Hacía sol cuando llegamos a la playa. (It was sunny when we got to the beach.) Interrupted action. Dormía cuando sonó el teléfono. (I was sleeping when the phone rang.) Habit vs one time. Siempre comíamos paella los domingos, pero ese domingo comimos pizza. (We always had paella on Sundays, but that Sunday we had pizza.) Description vs action in a story. La casa era vieja y oscura. Abrí la puerta y entré. (The house was old and dark. I opened the door and went in.) A closed period counts as an event. Viví en Roma tres años. (I lived in Rome for three years.) But: Cuando vivía en Roma, iba mucho al cine. (When I lived in Rome, I often went to the movies.) Examples Spanish English Llovía cuando salí de casa. It was raining when I left home. Mientras yo cocinaba, Pablo puso la mesa. While I was cooking, Pablo set the table. Eran las once cuando terminó la película. It was eleven when the movie ended. Antes iba al gimnasio, pero el año pasado empecé a correr. I used to go to the gym, but last year I started running. Estaba muy cansada, así que me acosté temprano. I was very tired, so I went to bed early. Conocí a Laura cuando trabajaba en Bogotá. I met Laura when I was working in Bogotá. De repente, empezó a llover. Suddenly, it started to rain. Good to know Mientras (while) is usually followed by the imperfect: Mientras esperaba el autobús, leí las noticias. Cuando can go with both. Ask yourself: is it a background (imperfect) or an event (preterite)? Cuando era joven… / Cuando llegué… Common mistakes ✗ Cuando llegué a casa, mi madre cocinó. → ✓ Cuando llegué a casa, mi madre cocinaba. — she was in the middle of it: imperfect. ✗ De niño fui a la playa todos los veranos. → ✓ De niño iba a la playa todos los veranos. — a repeated habit takes the imperfect. ✗ Ayer veía una película muy buena. → ✓ Ayer vi una película muy buena. — one finished action yesterday: preterite. ✗ Estaba en Roma tres días. → ✓ Estuve en Roma tres días. — a closed period is an event: preterite. Practice Choose the imperfect or the preterite. Ayer (llover) todo el día. Cuando era pequeña, (tener) un perro. (ducharse, yo) cuando llegó el cartero. El sábado pasado (ir, nosotros) al cine. Antes Carlos (trabajar) los fines de semana. La habitación (ser) pequeña, pero (tener) mucha luz. Translate into Spanish. I was reading when you called. (to a friend) Answers llovió tenía Me duchaba fuimos trabajaba era, tenía Leía cuando llamaste. / Leía cuando me llamaste.","level":"A2","category":"Verbs: tenses and moods","route":"#/a2/verbs-preterite-vs-imperfect-basics"},{"id":"page:a2-verbs-present-perfect","group":"pages","title":"Pretérito perfecto; ya, todavía no, alguna vez","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"He comido, ¿has estado alguna vez…? The present perfect for experiences, for things not done yet and, in Spain, for today.","text":"The present perfect (pretérito perfecto) is built like English \"have done\": he comido (I have eaten). It looks at the past from now: experiences (¿Has estado alguna vez en México?), things not done yet (Todavía no he comido) and, in Spain, things that happened today. Unlike English, nothing goes between the two parts: Ya he comido (I have already eaten). How it works haber (present) + participle Person haber + participle yo he hablado / comido / vivido tú has hablado / comido / vivido él, ella, usted ha hablado / comido / vivido nosotros, nosotras hemos hablado / comido / vivido vosotros, vosotras (Spain) habéis hablado / comido / vivido ellos, ellas, ustedes han hablado / comido / vivido Participle: -ar → -ado (hablado), -er / -ir → -ido (comido, vivido). The participle never changes: Ella ha comido. Ellos han comido. Ya, no and reflexive pronouns go before haber: Ya he terminado. No he terminado. Me he levantado tarde. Irregular participles: Infinitive Participle Infinitive Participle hacer hecho ver visto decir dicho escribir escrito poner puesto abrir abierto volver vuelto romper roto When to use it Experiences in your life (no time given). He estado dos veces en Perú. (I've been to Peru twice.) ¿Has comido alguna vez pulpo? (Have you ever eaten octopus?) Not yet / already. Todavía no he visto la película. (I haven't seen the movie yet.) ¿Ya has hecho la cena? (Have you made dinner yet?) Never. Nunca he viajado en avión. = No he viajado nunca en avión. (I've never traveled by plane.) A time that is not over (Spain). Hoy he trabajado mucho. Esta semana hemos ido dos veces al cine. (Today I've worked a lot. This week we've been to the movies twice.) Signal words: ya (already, yet), todavía no / aún no (not yet), alguna vez (ever), nunca (never), hoy, esta mañana, esta semana, este año. Examples Spanish English ¿Has estado alguna vez en México? Have you ever been to Mexico? No, nunca he estado allí. No, I've never been there. Todavía no he comido. I haven't eaten yet. ¿Ya habéis terminado? (Spain) Have you finished already? Mi hermana ha vivido en tres países. My sister has lived in three countries. ¿Quién ha abierto la ventana? Who opened the window? Esta mañana me he levantado a las seis. This morning I got up at six. Good to know Spain vs Latin America. In Spain the present perfect is normal for today: Hoy he desayunado tarde. In most of Latin America people use the preterite for today and in many countries also for ya and todavía no: Hoy desayuné tarde. ¿Ya comiste? Todavía no comí. For experiences (¿Has estado alguna vez…?) the perfect is common everywhere. With a finished time (ayer, el año pasado, en 2019), use the preterite everywhere: Ayer comí paella, never ayer he comido. Common mistakes ✗ Ayer he visto a Marta. → ✓ Ayer vi a Marta. — ayer is a finished time: preterite. ✗ ¿Has hacido la cena? → ✓ ¿Has hecho la cena? — hacer has an irregular participle: hecho. ✗ He ya comido. → ✓ Ya he comido. — nothing goes between haber and the participle. ✗ Ella ha comida. → ✓ Ella ha comido. — the participle after haber does not change. Practice Write the present perfect. Yo nunca (estar) en Argentina. ¿Tú ya (hacer) la cena? Nosotros todavía no (ver) la nueva serie. Mis padres (volver) del viaje. ¿Quién (romper) el vaso? Translate into Spanish. Have you ever eaten paella? (to a friend) I haven't finished yet. She has written three books. Answers he estado has hecho hemos visto han vuelto ha roto ¿Has comido alguna vez paella? / ¿Has comido paella alguna vez? / ¿Alguna vez has comido paella? Todavía no he terminado. / No he terminado todavía. / Todavía no terminé. Ha escrito tres libros. / Ella ha escrito tres libros.","level":"A2","category":"Verbs: tenses and moods","route":"#/a2/verbs-present-perfect"},{"id":"page:a2-functions-describing","group":"pages","title":"Describe people, places and things","headings":["Key phrases","Dialogue","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Say what someone looks like and is like, describe a home or a town, and explain what an object is, now and in the past.","text":"Spanish describes with four verbs where English mostly uses \"to be\" and \"to have\": ser for what someone or something is like, estar for where it is and how it is now, tener for features, and llevar for what someone wears. Es un piso pequeño pero muy luminoso. (Spain: It's a small but very bright apartment.) To describe the past, use the imperfect: La casa era vieja y tenía un jardín. Key phrases Asking Spanish English ¿Cómo es tu nuevo jefe? What's your new boss like? ¿Cómo era tu barrio? What was your neighborhood like? ¿Cómo es tu piso? (Spain) / ¿Cómo es tu departamento? (Latin America) What's your apartment like? ¿De qué color es? What color is it? ¿De qué es? What is it made of? People Spanish English Es alto y delgado. / Es bajita. He's tall and slim. / She's a bit short. Tiene el pelo largo y rizado. She has long, curly hair. Tiene los ojos verdes. He has green eyes. Lleva gafas (Spain). / Usa lentes (Latin America). He wears glasses. Es muy simpática y un poco tímida. She's very nice and a bit shy. Parece muy tranquilo. He seems very calm. Places Spanish English Es pequeño pero muy luminoso. It's small but very bright. Está en el centro, cerca del metro. It's downtown, near the subway. Tiene dos dormitorios y una terraza. It has two bedrooms and a terrace. Es un barrio tranquilo / ruidoso. It's a quiet / noisy neighborhood. Things Spanish English Es de madera / de metal / de plástico. It's made of wood / metal / plastic. Es redondo / cuadrado. It's round / square. Sirve para abrir botellas. It's used for opening bottles. Dialogue Two friends in Mexico City talk about a new apartment. Sofía: ¿Qué tal el departamento nuevo? ¿Cómo es? (How's the new apartment? What's it like?) Diego: Es pequeño, pero muy luminoso. Tiene una terraza con plantas. (It's small, but very bright. It has a terrace with plants.) Sofía: ¡Qué bonito! ¿Y dónde está? (How nice! And where is it?) Diego: Está en Coyoacán, cerca del metro. Es un barrio muy tranquilo. (It's in Coyoacán, near the subway. It's a very quiet neighborhood.) Sofía: ¿Y los vecinos? (And the neighbors?) Diego: La vecina de al lado es una señora mayor muy simpática. Tiene el pelo blanco y siempre lleva un sombrero rojo. (The neighbor next door is a very nice older lady. She has white hair and always wears a red hat.) Sofía: ¿Y tu departamento anterior? (And your old apartment?) Diego: Era grande, pero muy ruidoso y oscuro. No me gustaba nada. (It was big, but very noisy and dark. I didn't like it at all.) Good to know Ser or estar with the same adjective: Es guapo (he is good-looking) / Está guapo hoy (he looks good today). Es aburrido (he is boring) / Está aburrido (he is bored). Bajito, gordito: the ending -ito makes a description softer and friendlier. Common mistakes ✗ Es pelo largo. → ✓ Tiene el pelo largo. — features take tener. ✗ Es una persona muy simpático. → ✓ Es una persona muy simpática. — persona is feminine, for men too. ✗ Está una chica muy simpática. → ✓ Es una chica muy simpática. — ser + a noun describes someone. Practice Complete with es, está, tiene or lleva. Mi hermano alto y delgado. Mi abuela el pelo blanco. El hotel cerca de la playa. Hoy Laura un vestido azul. Write the question. Ask what your friend's new boss is like. Ask what the table is made of. Translate into Spanish. My apartment is small but very bright. (Spain) When I was a child, my neighborhood was very quiet. Answers es tiene está lleva ¿Cómo es tu nuevo jefe? / ¿Cómo es tu jefe nuevo? (jefa: nueva) ¿De qué es la mesa? Mi piso es pequeño pero muy luminoso. Cuando era niño, mi barrio era muy tranquilo. / Cuando era niña, mi barrio era muy tranquilo.","level":"A2","category":"Communication","route":"#/a2/functions-describing"},{"id":"page:a2-vocabulary-free-time-weather","group":"pages","title":"Free time, sports and weather","headings":["Words","Word partners","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Hobbies, sports and going out, and how to talk about the weather with hace, llueve and está nublado.","text":"The weather works differently in Spanish. English says \"it is hot\"; Spanish says \"it makes heat\": hace calor. Rain and snow have their own verbs: llueve (it's raining), nieva (it's snowing). For hobbies, watch the verbs: you juegas a game or a sport, but you tocas an instrument. In the past: Hacía sol, así que fuimos a la playa. Words Weather Spanish English ¿Qué tiempo hace? What's the weather like? hace calor / hace frío it's hot / it's cold hace sol / hace viento it's sunny / it's windy hace buen tiempo / hace mal tiempo the weather is good / bad llueve (llover) it rains, it's raining nieva (nevar) it snows, it's snowing está nublado it's cloudy hay niebla it's foggy la lluvia / la nieve the rain / the snow la tormenta the storm la temperatura the temperature Estamos a 30 grados. / Hace 30 grados. It's 30 degrees. Sports Spanish English el deporte the sport jugar al fútbol / al tenis (Latin America also: jugar fútbol) to play soccer / tennis el baloncesto (Spain) / el básquetbol (Latin America) basketball nadar / la natación to swim / swimming correr to run, to go running montar en bicicleta (Spain) / andar en bicicleta (Latin America) to ride a bike ir al gimnasio to go to the gym hacer senderismo to go hiking el partido the game, the match el equipo the team Free time Spanish English el tiempo libre free time salir con amigos to go out with friends quedar con alguien (Spain) to meet up with someone ir al cine / al teatro / a un concierto to go to the movies / the theater / a concert ver una serie / una película to watch a series / a movie leer / escuchar música to read / to listen to music tocar la guitarra / el piano to play the guitar / the piano bailar / cantar to dance / to sing pasear to go for a walk ir de compras to go shopping cocinar to cook la afición (Spain) / el pasatiempo the hobby Word partners Spanish English En verano hace mucho calor. It's very hot in summer. Ayer llovió todo el día. It rained all day yesterday. Cuando salimos, hacía mucho viento. When we went out, it was very windy. Los sábados juego al tenis con mi hermano. On Saturdays I play tennis with my brother. De niña tocaba el piano. As a child I played the piano. ¿Has visto el partido? Have you seen the game? Me gusta pasear por el parque. I like going for a walk in the park. Mañana va a nevar en la montaña. It's going to snow in the mountains tomorrow. Good to know Hace + noun: mucho goes with it, not muy: hace mucho calor, hace mucho frío. But muy with an adjective: Está muy nublado. El tiempo means both \"the weather\" and \"time\": ¿Qué tiempo hace? / No tengo tiempo. People feel cold or hot with tener: Tengo frío. (I'm cold.) Common mistakes ✗ Es calor hoy. → ✓ Hace calor hoy. — weather takes hacer. ✗ Estoy caliente. → ✓ Tengo calor. — \"I'm hot\" is tengo calor; estoy caliente has a sexual meaning. ✗ Juego la guitarra. → ✓ Toco la guitarra. — instruments take tocar. ✗ Hace muy calor. → ✓ Hace mucho calor. — calor is a noun, so mucho. Practice Complete. En invierno mucho frío. Mi hermano el piano muy bien. Hoy no hace sol: está . Los domingos al fútbol con mis amigos. Translate into Spanish. It's very hot today. I'm cold. It snowed a lot last winter. When I was a child, I swam every day. Answers hace toca nublado juego Hoy hace mucho calor. / Hace mucho calor hoy. Tengo frío. El invierno pasado nevó mucho. / Nevó mucho el invierno pasado. Cuando era niño, nadaba todos los días. / Cuando era niña, nadaba todos los días.","level":"A2","category":"Vocabulary","route":"#/a2/vocabulary-free-time-weather"},{"id":"page:a2-grammar-direct-object-pronouns","group":"pages","title":"Direct object pronouns and personal a","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Lo veo, la compré, los conozco: the pronouns for \"it\", \"him\", \"her\", \"them\", where they go, and the a before people.","text":"A direct object pronoun replaces the thing or person that receives the action: \"I see her\". Spanish chooses lo or la by the gender of the noun, so a book is lo and a house is la. The big difference from English is the position: the pronoun goes before the conjugated verb. ¿El libro? Lo compré ayer. And when the object is a person, Spanish puts a in front of it: Conozco a tu hermano. How it works The pronouns English Spanish Example me me Ana me llama. you (a friend) te Te veo mañana. him, it (masculine), you (formal, a man) lo Lo compré ayer. her, it (feminine), you (formal, a woman) la La conozco. us nos Nos invitan. you all (Spain) os Os espero. them (masculine or mixed), you all (ustedes) los Los veo los domingos. them (feminine), you all (ustedes, only women) las Las tengo aquí. Where it goes Before the conjugated verb: Lo veo. No lo veo. Lo he visto. La compré. With an infinitive (ir a, poder, querer…) it goes before the first verb or is attached to the infinitive: Lo voy a comprar = Voy a comprarlo. Never in both places. No comes first: No la conozco. Personal a A person as the object takes a: Veo a María. Llamo a mis padres. ¿Conoces a Luis? Things and places take no a: Veo la tele. Conozco Lima. When to use it To avoid repeating a noun. —¿Dónde está mi teléfono? —No lo sé. No lo veo. (Where's my phone? I don't know. I don't see it.) Short answers. —¿Tienes las llaves? —Sí, las tengo. (Yes, I have them.) English can say just \"yes, I do\"; Spanish needs the pronoun. To talk about people. —¿Conoces a Carmen? —Sí, la conozco del trabajo. (Yes, I know her from work.) Lo for an idea. Lo sé. (I know.) No lo entiendo. (I don't understand it.) Examples Spanish English ¿La bufanda? La compré en el mercado. The scarf? I bought it at the market. Te llamo esta tarde. I'll call you this afternoon. —¿Has visto a Pablo? —No, no lo he visto. \"Have you seen Pablo?\" \"No, I haven't seen him.\" Mis abuelos viven lejos, pero los visito en verano. My grandparents live far away, but I visit them in summer. ¿Las fotos? Voy a enviarlas esta noche. The photos? I'm going to send them tonight. Busco a mi hermana. ¿La has visto? I'm looking for my sister. Have you seen her? Señora, la espero en la entrada. Ma'am, I'll wait for you at the entrance. Good to know Leísmo (Spain). In Spain many people say le instead of lo for a man: Le vi ayer (I saw him yesterday). The norm accepts it; this course uses lo: Lo vi ayer. The Spanish present often means \"I'll…\" in offers: Te llamo esta tarde (I'll call you this afternoon). After a, use the article as usual: Veo al profesor (a + el = al). Common mistakes ✗ Veo lo. → ✓ Lo veo. — the pronoun goes before the conjugated verb. ✗ Veo María. → ✓ Veo a María. — a person as the object needs a. ✗ —¿Tienes el libro? —Sí, tengo. → ✓ Sí, lo tengo. — Spanish needs the pronoun. ✗ Lo voy a comprarlo. → ✓ Lo voy a comprar. / Voy a comprarlo. — the pronoun goes in one place only. Practice Replace the words in bold with a pronoun. Compro el pan todos los días. Llamé a mis padres ayer. No conozco a tu novia. Hemos visto las fotos. Add a where it is needed. Visité mis tíos en Bogotá. Translate into Spanish. The shirt? I bought it yesterday. I'm going to call you tomorrow. (to a friend) Answers Lo compro todos los días. Los llamé ayer. No la conozco. Las hemos visto. a (Visité a mis tíos en Bogotá.) ¿La camisa? La compré ayer. Te voy a llamar mañana. / Voy a llamarte mañana.","level":"A2","category":"Grammar","route":"#/a2/grammar-direct-object-pronouns"},{"id":"page:a2-grammar-indirect-object-pronouns","group":"pages","title":"Indirect object pronouns","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Le doy el libro, ¿me dices la hora?, les escribo: the pronouns for \"to me\", \"to her\", \"to them\".","text":"An indirect object is the person who receives something or for whom something is done: \"I give her the book\", \"I write to them\". You already know these pronouns from gustar: me, te, le, nos, os, les. They go where lo and la go: before the conjugated verb. Unlike English, Spanish often keeps the pronoun even when the person is named: Le doy el libro a Ana. How it works The pronouns English Spanish Example (to) me me ¿Me dices la hora? (to) you (a friend) te Te escribo luego. (to) him, her, you (formal) le Le doy el libro. (to) us nos Nos explicaron el plan. (to) you all (Spain) os Os mando las fotos. (to) them, you all (ustedes) les Les escribo cada semana. Only le and les differ from the direct object pronouns, and they do not change for gender: le = to him or to her. Where it goes: exactly like lo and la. Before the conjugated verb: Le escribí ayer. No le he dicho nada. With an infinitive, before the first verb or attached: Te voy a dar un regalo = Voy a darte un regalo. Saying the person twice. With le and les, add a + the person to make clear who it is: Le doy el libro a Ana. Les escribo a mis abuelos. This is normal Spanish, not a repetition to avoid. When to use it Giving and sending: dar, regalar, mandar, enviar, prestar. Le regalé un libro a mi padre. (I gave my father a book.) Telling and asking: decir, contar, explicar, preguntar, escribir. ¿Me explicas el ejercicio? (Can you explain the exercise to me?) Small requests to a friend: ¿Me pasas la sal? (Can you pass me the salt?) Examples Spanish English Le he dicho la verdad a Lucía. I've told Lucía the truth. ¿Me prestas tu bicicleta? Can you lend me your bike? Mis padres nos mandaron un paquete. My parents sent us a package. Voy a escribirles un correo a mis profesores. I'm going to write an email to my teachers. El médico le explicó todo a mi madre. The doctor explained everything to my mother. ¿Qué le vas a regalar a tu hermano? What are you going to give your brother? Good to know Two pronouns together. The indirect one comes first: ¿Me lo repites? (Can you repeat it for me?) With le / les + lo / la, le changes to se: Se lo doy. This is a later topic; for now, learn ¿Me lo repites? as a phrase. Llamar takes a direct object: La llamo (I call her). Preguntar takes an indirect one: Le pregunto (I ask her). Common mistakes ✗ Doy le el libro. → ✓ Le doy el libro. — the pronoun goes before the conjugated verb. ✗ Lo doy un regalo a mi hermano. → ✓ Le doy un regalo a mi hermano. — \"to him\" is le; lo means \"him\" or \"it\". ✗ Escribí a ella un correo. → ✓ Le escribí un correo. — with a ella or a él you must add le. ✗ Le pregunté una pregunta. → ✓ Le hice una pregunta. — \"to ask a question\" is hacer una pregunta. Practice Complete with me, te, le, nos or les. Mañana es el cumpleaños de mi madre. Voy a regalar flores. ¿ dices tu número de teléfono? (to me) Mis abuelos viven en Chile y escribo cada mes. El profesor explicó la regla. (to us) Put the words in order. libro / le / un / regalé / a Marta Translate into Spanish. Can you lend me your pen? (to a friend) I told her the truth. Answers regalarle (Le voy a regalar flores.) Me les nos Le regalé un libro a Marta. / A Marta le regalé un libro. ¿Me prestas tu bolígrafo? / ¿Me prestas tu boli? (Spain) / ¿Me prestas tu pluma? (Mexico) Le dije la verdad. / Le dije la verdad a ella.","level":"A2","category":"Grammar","route":"#/a2/grammar-indirect-object-pronouns"},{"id":"page:a2-grammar-comparatives-superlatives","group":"pages","title":"Comparatives and superlatives","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Más alto que, tan caro como, el mejor, la más grande: compare people and things and say which one is the most.","text":"English has two ways to compare: \"taller\" and \"more expensive\". Spanish has one: más (more) or menos (less) before the adjective, and que (than) after it: más alto que, más caro que. Only a few words have their own form, like mejor (better). For \"the most\", add the article: el más alto. How it works Comparing two things Meaning Pattern Example more … than más + adjective + que Madrid es más grande que Sevilla. less … than menos + adjective + que El tren es menos cómodo que el coche. as … as tan + adjective + como Lucas es tan alto como su padre. as much / as many … as tanto, tanta, tantos, tantas + noun + como No tengo tanto tiempo como tú. The adjective agrees as usual: Estas botas son más caras. With verbs: Ana trabaja más que yo. Duermo tanto como tú. With a number, \"more than\" is más de: Hay más de veinte personas. After que and como use yo and tú: más alto que yo. Irregular forms Adjective Comparative Superlative bueno (good) mejor (better) el / la mejor (the best) malo (bad) peor (worse) el / la peor (the worst) grande (for age: old) mayor (older) el / la mayor (the oldest) pequeño (for age: young) menor (younger) el / la menor (the youngest) Bien and mal (well, badly) also become mejor and peor: Hoy me siento mejor. The most: superlative el, la, los, las + (noun) + más / menos + adjective (+ de) Es el edificio más alto de la ciudad. (It's the tallest building in the city.) Carmen es la más joven de la familia. When to use it Comparing two people or things. Este hotel es más barato que el otro. (This hotel is cheaper than the other one.) Saying two things are equal. El pescado es tan sano como la carne. (Fish is as healthy as meat.) Choosing the best or the worst. Es el mejor café del barrio. (It's the best coffee in the neighborhood.) Examples Spanish English Mi hermano es más alto que yo. My brother is taller than me. Este restaurante es menos caro que el de ayer. This restaurant is less expensive than yesterday's. Valeria habla inglés tan bien como su madre. Valeria speaks English as well as her mother. En mi clase hay más de treinta estudiantes. There are more than thirty students in my class. Este es el mejor libro que he leído. This is the best book I've read. Fue el peor día de mi vida. It was the worst day of my life. Good to know -ísimo means \"very, extremely\": caro → carísimo, bueno → buenísimo. \"In\" after a superlative is de: la ciudad más bonita del país. Common mistakes ✗ Este café es más bueno que el otro. → ✓ Este café es mejor que el otro. — \"better\" is mejor. ✗ Es más mejor. → ✓ Es mejor. — mejor already means \"more good\". ✗ Ana es más alta como yo. → ✓ Ana es más alta que yo. — after más comes que. ✗ Es tan caro que el otro. → ✓ Es tan caro como el otro. — after tan comes como. ✗ Tengo más que veinte años. → ✓ Tengo más de veinte años. — before a number, \"more than\" is más de. Practice Complete with que, como or de. Mi coche es más viejo el de mi padre. Pablo es tan simpático su hermana. Es el restaurante más caro la ciudad. Tengo más cien libros. Translate into Spanish. This movie is better than the other one. My sister is shorter than me. It's the best hotel in the city. Answers que como de de Esta película es mejor que la otra. Mi hermana es más baja que yo. / Mi hermana es menos alta que yo. Es el mejor hotel de la ciudad.","level":"A2","category":"Grammar","route":"#/a2/grammar-comparatives-superlatives"},{"id":"page:a2-grammar-indefinites-quantifiers","group":"pages","title":"Indefinites and quantifiers","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Algo, nada, alguien, nadie, algún, ningún, otro; mucho, poco, bastante, demasiado, and when they agree with the noun.","text":"Indefinites talk about things and people without naming them: algo (something), alguien (somebody). Each one has a negative partner: nada, nadie, ningún. You already know from A1 that Spanish uses a double negative: No veo a nadie (I don't see anybody, word for word \"I don't see nobody\"). Quantifiers say how much: mucho, poco, bastante, demasiado. Unlike English \"much\" and \"many\", they agree with the noun: mucho tiempo, muchas personas. How it works Indefinites and their negative partners Positive Negative Used for algo (something, anything) nada (nothing, not anything) things, no noun after it alguien (somebody, anybody) nadie (nobody, not anybody) people, no noun after it algún / alguna / algunos / algunas; alone: alguno (some, any) ningún / ninguna; alone: ninguno (no, not any) before a noun, or alone Alguno and ninguno lose the -o before a masculine noun: algún libro, ningún problema. Alone they keep it: ¿Tienes algún libro? —No, no tengo ninguno. Ningún / ninguna are almost always singular. A negative word after the verb needs no before the verb: No hay nada. No viene nadie. Before the verb it works alone: Nadie viene. Alguien and nadie as objects take the personal a: ¿Conoces a alguien aquí? No conozco a nadie. otro (another, other) Otro, otra, otros, otras agree with the noun and never take un / una: otro café (another coffee), otra vez (again). Quantifiers with a noun (agrees) with a verb or adjective (never changes) a lot, very mucho, mucha, muchos, muchas mucho (verb) / muy (adjective) a little, few poco, poca, pocos, pocas poco enough, quite bastante, bastantes bastante too much, too many demasiado, demasiada, demasiados, demasiadas demasiado With a noun they agree: muchas horas, poca gente, bastantes sillas, demasiada sal. With a verb they never change: Trabaja mucho. Come poco. Duermo bastante. Before an adjective or adverb, \"very\" is muy, not mucho: Es muy caro. Vive muy lejos. When to use it Asking about something or someone unknown. ¿Quieres algo? ¿Ha llamado alguien? (Do you want anything? Has anybody called?) Answering no. —¿Necesitas algo? —No, nada, gracias. (No, nothing, thanks.) Asking if there is any. ¿Hay algún banco por aquí? (Is there a bank around here?) Saying how much. Hoy tengo poco tiempo. Hay demasiados coches en el centro. (I have little time today. There are too many cars downtown.) Examples Spanish English ¿Hay alguien en casa? Is anybody home? No he comprado nada para la cena. I haven't bought anything for dinner. ¿Tienes alguna pregunta? Do you have any questions? No hay ningún problema. There's no problem at all. En la fiesta había mucha gente. There were a lot of people at the party. Mis hijos ven demasiada televisión. My children watch too much TV. Good to know La gente (people) is singular in Spanish: mucha gente, poca gente, and the verb is singular: La gente es muy simpática. Common mistakes ✗ Quiero un otro café. → ✓ Quiero otro café. — otro never takes un. ✗ ¿Tienes alguno libro? → ✓ ¿Tienes algún libro? — before a masculine noun, alguno loses the -o. ✗ No conozco a alguien aquí. → ✓ No conozco a nadie aquí. — after no, use the negative word nadie. ✗ Es mucho caro. → ✓ Es muy caro. — before an adjective, \"very\" is muy. ✗ Hay muchos gente. → ✓ Hay mucha gente. — gente is feminine and singular. Practice Complete with algo, nada, alguien or nadie. ¿Quieres de beber? No hay en la oficina; todos se han ido. Creo que hay en la puerta. Complete with the right form of mucho, poco or demasiado. En verano hay turistas en la playa. (mucho) Mi abuelo come muy carne. (poco) Hay sal en la sopa. No me gusta. (demasiado) Correct the mistake. No tengo ninguno problema. ¿Me das un otro vaso de agua? Answers algo nadie alguien muchos poca demasiada No tengo ningún problema. ¿Me das otro vaso de agua?","level":"A2","category":"Grammar","route":"#/a2/grammar-indefinites-quantifiers"},{"id":"page:a2-vocabulary-shopping-clothes","group":"pages","title":"Shopping, clothes and colors","headings":["Words","Word partners","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Clothes, shoes and colors, and what you say in a clothes shop: la talla, el probador, me queda bien.","text":"Colors are adjectives, so most of them agree with the noun: una camisa blanca, unos zapatos negros. To say how something fits, Spanish uses quedar like gustar: Me queda bien (it fits me well), Me quedan grandes (they're too big for me). And English \"clothes\" is plural, but Spanish la ropa is singular: La ropa es cara aquí. Words Clothes and shoes Spanish English la ropa clothes la camisa the shirt la camiseta / la playera (Mexico) / la remera (Argentina) the T-shirt el pantalón / los pantalones the pants los vaqueros (Spain) / los jeans (Latin America) the jeans la falda / la pollera (Argentina, Uruguay) the skirt el vestido the dress el jersey (Spain) / el suéter (Latin America) the sweater la chaqueta the jacket el abrigo the coat los zapatos the shoes las zapatillas (Spain, Argentina, Chile) / los tenis (Mexico, Colombia) the sneakers las botas the boots los calcetines the socks el gorro / la gorra the hat (warm) / the cap la bufanda the scarf el bolso (Spain) / la bolsa (Mexico) / la cartera (Argentina) the bag, the purse Colors Spanish English blanco, negro, rojo, amarillo white, black, red, yellow azul, verde, gris blue, green, gray marrón (Spain) / café (Mexico) brown rosa, naranja pink, orange morado purple azul claro / azul oscuro light blue / dark blue In the shop Spanish English la tienda de ropa the clothes shop la talla the size (clothes) el número the size (shoes) el probador the fitting room probarse to try on quedar bien / mal to fit well / badly, to suit quedar grande / pequeño to be too big / too small las rebajas (Spain) / las ofertas the sales pagar con tarjeta / en efectivo to pay by card / in cash llevar to wear; to take (a purchase) Word partners Spanish English ¿Tiene esta camisa en azul? Do you have this shirt in blue? ¿Tiene una talla más grande? Do you have a bigger size? ¿Puedo probármelo? Can I try it on? ¿Dónde están los probadores? Where are the fitting rooms? Uso la talla M. I wear a size M. Este vestido te queda muy bien. This dress really suits you. Los pantalones me quedan un poco grandes. The pants are a bit too big for me. Me lo llevo. I'll take it. Compré estos zapatos en las rebajas. I bought these shoes in the sales. Good to know Azul, verde, gris, marrón change only for the plural: una falda azul, unos zapatos azules. Rosa and naranja usually do not change at all: unas camisetas rosa. After probarse, the pronoun for the item agrees with it: ¿Puedo probármela? (the shirt), ¿Puedo probármelos? (the shoes). Llevar means \"to wear\": Hoy llevo un jersey rojo. It is also what you say when you buy something: Me lo llevo. Common mistakes ✗ Quiero comprar unas ropas. → ✓ Quiero comprar ropa. — ropa is singular and has no plural here. ✗ Me queda bien los zapatos. → ✓ Me quedan bien los zapatos. — like gustar: plural thing, quedan. ✗ Busco una camisa blanco. → ✓ Busco una camisa blanca. — the color agrees with camisa. ✗ ¿Tiene un número más grande de esta camisa? → ✓ ¿Tiene una talla más grande de esta camisa? — clothes have a talla; número is for shoes. Practice Complete. Hace frío. Voy a llevar el y la bufanda. (coat) Para ir al gimnasio me pongo . (sneakers) Estos pantalones me pequeños. —¿Qué usa? —La M. Write the color in the right form. Tengo dos camisetas . (white) Mi hermano lleva unos zapatos . (gray) Translate into Spanish. Do you have it in black? (to the shop assistant) This jacket is too big for me. Answers abrigo zapatillas / unas zapatillas / tenis / unos tenis quedan talla blancas grises ¿Lo tiene en negro? / ¿La tiene en negro? Esta chaqueta me queda grande. / Esta chaqueta me queda muy grande. / Esta chaqueta me queda demasiado grande.","level":"A2","category":"Vocabulary","route":"#/a2/vocabulary-shopping-clothes"},{"id":"page:a2-verbs-estar-gerund","group":"pages","title":"estar + gerundio: actions in progress","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Estoy leyendo, ¿qué estás haciendo?: say what is happening right now with estar and the gerund (-ando, -iendo).","text":"Estar + gerund (gerundio) works like English \"be + -ing\": Estoy leyendo (I'm reading). It describes an action in progress right now. But Spanish uses it less than English: the simple present can also describe what is happening (¿Qué haces? —Leo.), and it is never used for future plans. English \"I'm leaving tomorrow\" is Mañana salgo or Mañana voy a salir. How it works estar (present) + gerund Person estar + gerund yo estoy hablando / comiendo / escribiendo tú estás hablando / comiendo / escribiendo él, ella, usted está hablando / comiendo / escribiendo nosotros, nosotras estamos hablando / comiendo / escribiendo vosotros, vosotras (Spain) estáis hablando / comiendo / escribiendo ellos, ellas, ustedes están hablando / comiendo / escribiendo Gerund: -ar → -ando (hablando), -er / -ir → -iendo (comiendo, viviendo). The gerund never changes: Ella está comiendo. Ellos están comiendo. Irregular gerunds Pattern Infinitive → gerund vowel + -er / -ir → -yendo leer → leyendo, oír → oyendo, traer → trayendo irregular ir → yendo -ir verbs with e → i pedir → pidiendo, decir → diciendo, servir → sirviendo, venir → viniendo -ir verbs with o → u dormir → durmiendo, morir → muriendo Pronouns go before estar or are attached to the gerund, which then needs an accent: Me estoy duchando = Estoy duchándome. ¿Lo estás leyendo? = ¿Estás leyéndolo? When to use it Something happening right now. —¿Dónde está Lucía? —Está hablando por teléfono. (She's on the phone.) A temporary situation these days. Estoy leyendo un libro muy bueno. Este mes estamos trabajando mucho. (I'm reading a very good book. We're working a lot this month.) To stress that it is in progress when the simple present could mean a habit: Normalmente trabajo en casa, pero hoy estoy trabajando en la oficina. Signal words: ahora, ahora mismo (right now), en este momento, estos días, hoy. Examples Spanish English ¿Qué estás haciendo? What are you doing? Estoy preparando la cena. I'm making dinner. Los niños están durmiendo. The children are sleeping. No puedo hablar ahora, estoy conduciendo. (Spain; Latin America: manejando) I can't talk now, I'm driving. Está lloviendo mucho. It's raining a lot. Mi hermano está buscando trabajo. My brother is looking for a job. ¿Me estás escuchando? Are you listening to me? Estamos viendo una película. We're watching a movie. Good to know In the past, use the imperfect of estar: Estaba leyendo cuando me llamaste. (I was reading when you called.) Verbs like tener, saber, conocer, querer are not used in this form for states: Tengo un coche (I have a car), not estoy teniendo. Ir and venir rarely take it: Ya voy (I'm coming), ¿Vienes? (Are you coming?). Common mistakes ✗ Estoy yendo a Madrid mañana. → ✓ Mañana voy a Madrid. — for plans, use the present or ir a, not estar + gerund. ✗ Estoy leiendo. → ✓ Estoy leyendo. — between vowels, i becomes y. ✗ Soy trabajando. → ✓ Estoy trabajando. — the helper verb is estar, not ser. ✗ Nadando es bueno para la salud. → ✓ Nadar es bueno para la salud. — as a noun (\"swimming\"), Spanish uses the infinitive. ✗ Los niños están dormiendo. → ✓ Los niños están durmiendo. — dormir changes o → u in the gerund. Practice Write estar + gerund. Ahora mismo yo (escribir) un correo. ¿Qué (hacer) tú? Mis padres (dormir). Paula (leer) el periódico. (llover) desde esta mañana. Translate into Spanish. We're watching TV. I'm taking a shower. (two ways) I'm going to Lima tomorrow. Answers estoy escribiendo estás haciendo están durmiendo está leyendo Está lloviendo Estamos viendo la tele. / Estamos viendo la televisión. Me estoy duchando. / Estoy duchándome. Mañana voy a Lima. / Voy a Lima mañana. / Mañana voy a ir a Lima.","level":"A2","category":"Verbs: tenses and moods","route":"#/a2/verbs-estar-gerund"},{"id":"page:a2-verbs-obligation-ability","group":"pages","title":"Obligation, necessity and ability","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Tengo que, hay que, debo, necesito, puedo, sé nadar: say what you must, should, need to and can do.","text":"English uses small helper verbs: \"must\", \"have to\", \"should\", \"can\". Spanish uses ordinary verbs followed by an infinitive: Tengo que trabajar (I have to work), Puedo venir (I can come). Two points differ from English. \"Can\" has two verbs: poder (it is possible, I'm allowed) and saber (I know how): Sé nadar. And \"don't have to\" is not \"must not\": No tienes que venir means you are free not to come. How it works Meaning Form Example have to tener que + infinitive Tengo que estudiar. one has to, you have to (general) hay que + infinitive Hay que llegar temprano. must, should deber + infinitive Debes descansar. need to necesitar + infinitive Necesito dormir más. can, be able to, be allowed to poder + infinitive ¿Puedo pasar? can, know how to saber + infinitive Sé conducir. Tener and poder are irregular in the present (tengo, tienes…; puedo, puedes…). Saber has only one odd form, sé (I know): Person tener que poder saber yo tengo que puedo sé tú tienes que puedes sabes él, ella, usted tiene que puede sabe nosotros, nosotras tenemos que podemos sabemos vosotros, vosotras (Spain) tenéis que podéis sabéis ellos, ellas, ustedes tienen que pueden saben Hay que never changes and has no person. When to use it Personal obligation: tener que. Mañana tengo que trabajar. (I have to work tomorrow.) A general rule for everyone: hay que. En la biblioteca hay que hablar bajo. (In the library you have to speak quietly.) Advice or duty: deber. Debes beber más agua. (You should drink more water.) Need: necesitar. Necesito hablar contigo. (I need to talk to you.) Possibility and permission: poder. Hoy no puedo salir. ¿Puedo abrir la ventana? (I can't go out today. Can I open the window?) A skill you learned: saber. Mi hija ya sabe leer. (My daughter can already read.) Negatives: No tienes que venir (you don't have to come: it's not necessary). No debes fumar aquí (you must not smoke here: it's not allowed or not a good idea). Examples Spanish English Tengo que llamar a mi madre. I have to call my mother. Hay que comprar pan. We need to buy bread. No debes conducir tan rápido. You shouldn't drive so fast. ¿Puedes ayudarme? Can you help me? ¿Sabes nadar? Can you swim? Mañana no tienes que venir; es fiesta. You don't have to come tomorrow; it's a holiday. Ayer tuve que trabajar hasta las nueve. Yesterday I had to work until nine. Necesitamos salir antes de las seis. We need to leave before six. Good to know In the past: tuve que (I had to, once), tenía que (I had to, regularly or as background): De niño tenía que ayudar en casa. Conducir is Spain; most of Latin America says manejar: Sé manejar. Poder for a skill also exists, but for learned skills saber is the usual verb: Sé tocar la guitarra. Common mistakes ✗ Tengo estudiar. → ✓ Tengo que estudiar. — tener needs que before the infinitive. ✗ Debo que trabajar. → ✓ Debo trabajar. — deber takes the infinitive directly, without que. ✗ Hay que estudiamos más. → ✓ Hay que estudiar más. — after hay que comes the infinitive. ✗ No debes venir mañana. (meaning \"it's not necessary\") → ✓ No tienes que venir mañana. — \"don't have to\" is no tener que. ✗ Sé a nadar. → ✓ Sé nadar. — saber takes the infinitive directly. Practice Complete with the right form of tener que, poder or saber. Hoy no salir: estoy enferma. (yo, poder) Mi hermano hablar tres idiomas. (saber) Nosotros terminar el informe hoy. (tener que) ¿ conducir? (tú, saber) Choose hay que or tengo que. Para entrar en el museo comprar una entrada. Esta tarde ir al médico. Translate into Spanish. You don't have to pay. (to a friend) I can't swim. Answers puedo sabe tenemos que Sabes hay que tengo que No tienes que pagar. No sé nadar.","level":"A2","category":"Verbs: tenses and moods","route":"#/a2/verbs-obligation-ability"},{"id":"page:a2-verbs-future-simple","group":"pages","title":"Future simple: predictions and promises","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Mañana lloverá, te llamaré luego: the future tense for predictions and promises, its endings and its ten common irregular stems.","text":"English makes the future with a helper word: \"I will call\". Spanish adds an ending to the infinitive: llamaré (I will call). The endings are the same for all verbs, and a few common verbs have a short irregular stem: tener → tendré. In everyday speech, plans usually take ir a + infinitive (Voy a llamar a Ana); the future is typical for predictions and promises: Mañana lloverá. Te llamaré luego. How it works infinitive + ending (the same for -ar, -er, -ir) Person ending hablar comer vivir yo -é hablaré comeré viviré tú -ás hablarás comerás vivirás él, ella, usted -á hablará comerá vivirá nosotros, nosotras -emos hablaremos comeremos viviremos vosotros, vosotras (Spain) -éis hablaréis comeréis viviréis ellos, ellas, ustedes -án hablarán comerán vivirán Every form has a written accent except nosotros: hablaré, but hablaremos. Irregular stems (same endings) Infinitive Stem yo Infinitive Stem yo tener tendr- tendré hacer har- haré poner pondr- pondré decir dir- diré salir saldr- saldré poder podr- podré venir vendr- vendré saber sabr- sabré querer querr- querré haber (hay) habr- habrá (there will be) When to use it Predictions. Mañana lloverá en el norte. (It will rain in the north tomorrow.) Creo que ganará nuestro equipo. (I think our team will win.) Promises and decisions about the future. Te llamaré esta noche. (I'll call you tonight.) No lo haré más. (I won't do it again.) The more distant future, often in writing. El año que viene viviremos en Valencia. (Next year we'll live in Valencia.) After si + present. Si llueve, nos quedaremos en casa. (If it rains, we'll stay at home.) Signal words: mañana, pasado mañana (the day after tomorrow), la semana que viene, el año que viene / el próximo año, dentro de dos días (in two days), pronto (soon), algún día. Examples Spanish English Mañana hará sol. It will be sunny tomorrow. ¿Vendrás a mi fiesta? Will you come to my party? Te lo diré mañana. I'll tell you tomorrow. En el futuro habrá más coches eléctricos. In the future there will be more electric cars. Mis padres saldrán a las ocho. My parents will leave at eight. No podré ir a la reunión. I won't be able to go to the meeting. Algún día tendremos una casa en el campo. One day we'll have a house in the country. Si estudias, aprobarás el examen. If you study, you'll pass the exam. Good to know Ir a + infinitive or the future? Both are correct for most future ideas. Ir a is more common in conversation, especially for plans already decided: Esta tarde voy a ver a Marta. For an offer right now (\"I'll get it!\"), Spanish uses the present: ¡Ya voy! Yo te ayudo. Habrá is the future of hay: Mañana habrá una reunión. Common mistakes ✗ Haceré la cena. → ✓ Haré la cena. — hacer has the stem har-. ✗ Teneré tiempo mañana. → ✓ Tendré tiempo mañana. — tener has the stem tendr-. ✗ Si lloverá, no saldremos. → ✓ Si llueve, no saldremos. — after si, use the present. ✗ Mañana te llamare. → ✓ Mañana te llamaré. — the yo ending has an accent: -é. Practice Write the future. Mañana (llover) en Madrid. Te (llamar, yo) esta noche. ¿ (venir, tú) a la cena? Nosotros (hacer) una fiesta el sábado. El año que viene mis hijos (tener) diez años. Correct the mistake. Si tendré tiempo, iré al cine. Translate into Spanish. I'll tell you tomorrow. (to a friend) There will be a lot of people. Answers lloverá llamaré Vendrás haremos tendrán Si tengo tiempo, iré al cine. Te lo diré mañana. / Mañana te lo diré. Habrá mucha gente.","level":"A2","category":"Verbs: tenses and moods","route":"#/a2/verbs-future-simple"},{"id":"page:a2-verbs-imperative-affirmative","group":"pages","title":"Affirmative imperative: instructions and directions","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Gira a la derecha, toma, siga recto: tell a friend or a stranger what to do, with tú, usted and ustedes forms.","text":"The imperative (imperativo) gives instructions: \"turn\", \"come\", \"take it\". English has one form for everybody. Spanish has a form for tú (a friend), for usted (a stranger, formal) and for the plural: Gira a la derecha (to a friend), Gire a la derecha (formal). Pronouns are attached to the end: Llámame (call me), Dígame (tell me, the usual way to answer the phone in Spain). This page covers \"do\"; \"don't\" has other forms and comes later. How it works tú: the él / ella form of the present Infinitive Present (él) tú imperative hablar habla habla comer come come subir sube sube seguir (e → i) sigue sigue girar gira gira Eight irregular tú forms decir hacer ir poner salir ser tener venir di haz ve pon sal sé ten ven usted and ustedes: start from the yo form of the present, drop -o, swap the vowel Infinitive yo usted ustedes hablar hablo hable hablen comer como coma coman subir subo suba suban tener tengo tenga tengan seguir sigo siga sigan hacer hago haga hagan -ar verbs take -e / -en; -er and -ir verbs take -a / -an. Irregular: ir → vaya, ser → sea, saber → sepa, dar → dé. Spelling: cruzar → cruce, buscar → busque, llegar → llegue. vosotros (Spain): infinitive with -d instead of -r: hablad, comed, subid, venid. Pronouns are attached, and the verb often needs a written accent to keep its stress: llama → llámame, diga → dígame, siéntese (sit down, formal), pásame la sal. When to use it Directions. Sigue todo recto y toma la primera a la izquierda. (Go straight on and take the first left.) Instructions and recipes. Corta las cebollas y ponlas en la sartén. (Cut the onions and put them in the pan.) Offers and invitations. Pasa, pasa. Siéntate. (Come in, come in. Sit down.) Toma, un regalo. (Here, a present.) Directions and requests to strangers (usted). Perdone, ¿la estación? —Siga por esta calle. (Excuse me, the station? Keep going along this street.) Add por favor to sound polite. Between friends, a question in the present is often softer: ¿Me pasas la sal? Examples Spanish English Ven aquí un momento. Come here a moment. Haz café, por favor. Make some coffee, please. Llámame esta noche. Call me tonight. Pase, señora. Come in, ma'am. Gire a la derecha en el semáforo. Turn right at the traffic light. Abran sus libros en la página diez. Open your books to page ten. Dime la verdad. Tell me the truth. Cruce la plaza y siga recto. Cross the square and go straight on. Good to know Ve (go) and ven (come) are easy to mix up: Ve a la tienda (go to the shop), Ven a mi casa (come to my house). Sé (be) has an accent, like sé (I know): Sé bueno. (Be good.) Common mistakes ✗ Hace la cama. (to a friend) → ✓ Haz la cama. — the tú imperative of hacer is haz. ✗ Me llama mañana. (as an instruction) → ✓ Llámame mañana. — pronouns are attached to the imperative. ✗ Llamame. → ✓ Llámame. — with a pronoun attached, the stressed syllable needs an accent. ✗ Sigua todo recto. → ✓ Siga todo recto. — from yo sigo: siga. ✗ Viene aquí. (to a friend) → ✓ Ven aquí. — the tú imperative of venir is ven. Practice Write the tú imperative. (venir) a mi casa esta tarde. (girar) a la izquierda. (poner) la mesa, por favor. Write the usted imperative. (tomar) la segunda calle. (seguir) recto. (cruzar) el puente. Translate into Spanish. Call me tomorrow. (to a friend) Tell me, please. (formal) Answers Ven Gira Pon Tome Siga Cruce Llámame mañana. / Mañana llámame. Dígame, por favor.","level":"A2","category":"Verbs: tenses and moods","route":"#/a2/verbs-imperative-affirmative"},{"id":"page:a2-functions-directions","group":"pages","title":"Ask for and give directions","headings":["Key phrases","Dialogue","Culture note","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"¿Para ir a la estación?, sigue todo recto, gira a la izquierda: ask the way, understand the answer and give directions yourself.","text":"To ask the way, Spanish speakers often use a short question: ¿Para ir a la estación? (How do I get to the station?) The answer uses the imperative you have just learned: sigue / siga (go on), gira / gire (turn), toma / tome (take). Note that English says \"turn left\", but Spanish says gira a la izquierda. Key phrases Asking Spanish English Perdone (formal) / Perdona (informal), ¿para ir a la estación? Excuse me, how do I get to the station? ¿Dónde está el museo? Where is the museum? ¿Hay una farmacia por aquí? Is there a pharmacy around here? ¿Está lejos? / ¿Está cerca? Is it far? / Is it near? ¿Se puede ir andando? (Spain) / ¿Se puede ir caminando? Can you walk there? Giving directions Spanish English Siga (formal) / Sigue (informal) todo recto. Go straight on. Gire / Gira a la derecha. Turn right. Tome / Toma la primera calle a la izquierda. Take the first street on the left. Cruce / Cruza la plaza. Cross the square. Al final de la calle, gire a la izquierda. At the end of the street, turn left. Está en la esquina. It's on the corner. Está enfrente del banco. It's opposite the bank. Está a cinco minutos a pie. It's five minutes' walk away. Está a dos calles (Spain) / a dos cuadras (Latin America). It's two blocks away. Reacting Spanish English ¿Puede repetir, por favor? Could you repeat that, please? Lo siento, no soy de aquí. Sorry, I'm not from around here. Dialogue Tom, a visitor, asks Carmen the way in Valencia. Tom: Perdone, ¿hay una farmacia por aquí? (Excuse me, is there a pharmacy around here?) Carmen: Sí, hay una en la calle Colón. ¿Va andando? (Yes, there's one on Colón Street. Are you walking?) Tom: Sí. ¿Está lejos? (Yes. Is it far?) Carmen: No, está a cinco minutos. Siga todo recto hasta el semáforo. (No, it's five minutes away. Go straight on to the traffic light.) Tom: Hasta el semáforo… ¿y después? (To the traffic light… and then?) Carmen: Después gire a la derecha y tome la segunda calle a la izquierda. La farmacia está en la esquina, enfrente de un banco. (Then turn right and take the second street on the left. The pharmacy is on the corner, opposite a bank.) Tom: ¿Puede repetir lo último, por favor? (Could you repeat the last part, please?) Carmen: La segunda a la izquierda. Está en la esquina. (The second on the left. It's on the corner.) Tom: Muchas gracias. (Thank you very much.) Culture note Todo recto (Spain) and todo derecho (Latin America, also Spain) both mean \"straight on\". Be careful: derecho means \"straight\", but a la derecha means \"to the right\". In Latin America a block is a cuadra, and people often count in blocks: Camine tres cuadras. In Spain you count streets: Está a dos calles. Common mistakes ✗ Gira en la derecha. → ✓ Gira a la derecha. — directions take a: a la derecha, a la izquierda. ✗ ¿Dónde es la estación? → ✓ ¿Dónde está la estación? — location takes estar. ✗ ¿Hay la farmacia cerca? → ✓ ¿Hay una farmacia cerca? — hay takes un / una, not el / la. ✗ Toma la segunda calle en la izquierda. → ✓ Toma la segunda calle a la izquierda. — \"on the left\" is a la izquierda. Practice Complete. Perdone, ¿ ir al museo? Siga todo y gire a la izquierda. El banco está del supermercado. (opposite) ¿Hay un cajero aquí? (around) Give the directions to a stranger (usted). Turn right. Take the first street on the left. Translate into Spanish. Is it far? Sorry, I'm not from around here. Answers para recto / derecho enfrente por Gire a la derecha. Tome la primera calle a la izquierda. / Tome la primera a la izquierda. ¿Está lejos? Lo siento, no soy de aquí. / Perdone, no soy de aquí.","level":"A2","category":"Communication","route":"#/a2/functions-directions"},{"id":"page:a2-grammar-por-para-basics","group":"pages","title":"por and para: the basics","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Es para ti, gracias por todo, paso por el parque: the main uses of por and para, two Spanish words for English \"for\".","text":"English \"for\" has two Spanish partners, por and para, and they are not interchangeable. A simple picture helps: para looks ahead, to a goal, a person or a place you are heading for; por looks at the cause or the way: why something happens, which route you take, how you do it. Es para ti (it's for you), but Gracias por todo (thanks for everything). Learn them in phrases. How it works para: the goal Use Example purpose: para + infinitive (in order to) Estudio español para viajar. the person who gets something Este regalo es para ti. destination El tren para Sevilla sale a las diez. deadline (by) Necesito el informe para el lunes. opinion (in my view) Para mí, es demasiado caro. por: the cause and the way Use Example cause, reason (because of) Gracias por la ayuda. No salimos por la lluvia. through, along, around a place Paso por el parque. ¿Hay un banco por aquí? means (by) Te llamo por teléfono. price, exchange Compré la chaqueta por cuarenta euros. parts of the day por la mañana, por la tarde, por la noche Para + infinitive answers \"what for?\"; por + noun often answers \"why?\". When to use it Purpose. Ahorro dinero para comprar una casa. (I'm saving money to buy a house.) Who it is for. Estas flores son para mi madre. (These flowers are for my mother.) Thanks and reasons. Gracias por la invitación. Llegué tarde por el tráfico. (Thanks for the invitation. I arrived late because of the traffic.) Movement through a place. Me gusta pasear por el centro. (I like walking around downtown.) Means of communication. Hablamos por teléfono todos los domingos. (We talk on the phone every Sunday.) Price. Pagué veinte euros por la camiseta. (I paid twenty euros for the T-shirt.) Examples Spanish English ¿Este es el tren para Valencia? Is this the train to Valencia? Muchas gracias por tu ayuda. Thank you very much for your help. Te mando las fotos por correo electrónico. I'll send you the photos by email. No fuimos a la playa por el mal tiempo. We didn't go to the beach because of the bad weather. Para mí, esta es la mejor playa de la costa. For me, this is the best beach on the coast. Tengo que terminar el trabajo para el viernes. I have to finish the work by Friday. Compré estos zapatos por treinta euros. I bought these shoes for thirty euros. Good to know ¿Por qué? (why?) asks for the cause; ¿Para qué? (what for?) asks for the purpose: ¿Para qué estudias español? —Para viajar. For a length of time, Spanish usually needs no preposition: Estudié dos horas. (I studied for two hours.) In Latin America por dos horas is also common. Never para dos horas. Fixed phrases with por: por favor, por ejemplo, por fin (at last), por supuesto (of course). Common mistakes ✗ Gracias para el regalo. → ✓ Gracias por el regalo. — thanks always take por. ✗ Estudio español por viajar a México. → ✓ Estudio español para viajar a México. — \"in order to\" is para. ✗ Compré el libro para diez euros. → ✓ Compré el libro por diez euros. — the price you pay takes por. ✗ Hablo con mi madre para teléfono. → ✓ Hablo con mi madre por teléfono. — the means takes por. ✗ Viví en Lima para dos años. → ✓ Viví en Lima dos años. — a length of time needs no para. Practice Complete with por or para. Este café es ti. Gracias tu ayuda. Necesito dinero comprar los billetes. Me gusta caminar la playa. (along) Pagué quince euros la entrada. Tienes que hacer los ejercicios el jueves. Translate into Spanish. Thanks for everything. We didn't go out because of the rain. Answers para por para por por para Gracias por todo. / Muchas gracias por todo. No salimos por la lluvia.","level":"A2","category":"Grammar","route":"#/a2/grammar-por-para-basics"},{"id":"page:a2-grammar-saber-conocer","group":"pages","title":"Tricky verb pairs: saber vs conocer, pedir vs preguntar","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Sé cocinar, conozco Sevilla; pido un café, pregunto la hora: two English verbs that each need two Spanish verbs.","text":"English \"to know\" is two verbs in Spanish. Saber is for facts, information and skills: Sé su nombre. Sé cocinar. Conocer is for people, places and things you are familiar with: Conozco Sevilla. Conozco a tu hermano. English \"to ask\" is two verbs too: pedir is \"to ask for\" something (Pido un café), preguntar is \"to ask\" a question (Pregunto la hora). How it works The present Person saber conocer pedir preguntar yo sé conozco pido pregunto tú sabes conoces pides preguntas él, ella, usted sabe conoce pide pregunta nosotros, nosotras sabemos conocemos pedimos preguntamos vosotros, vosotras (Spain) sabéis conocéis pedís preguntáis ellos, ellas, ustedes saben conocen piden preguntan Pedir changes e → i, also in the preterite pidió, pidieron. Preguntar is regular. saber or conocer? saber conocer a fact: Sé su número. a person, with a: Conozco a Lucía. que, si, dónde, cuándo…: No sé dónde vive. a place: Conozco Lima muy bien. infinitive, a skill: Sé nadar. something you have tried: ¿Conoces este restaurante? pedir or preguntar? pedir preguntar ask for, order, request: Pido la cuenta. ask a question: Pregunto el precio. no preposition: pedir ayuda (ask for help) preguntar por + a person: ask about someone Both take an indirect object for the person: Le pido ayuda. Le pregunto la hora. When to use it A fact or information: saber. ¿Sabes a qué hora abre el museo? (Do you know what time the museum opens?) People and places you know: conocer. ¿Conoces a mi novio? No conozco Bogotá. (Do you know my boyfriend? I don't know Bogotá.) A skill: saber + infinitive. Mi hijo sabe tocar el piano. (My son can play the piano.) Meeting someone for the first time: conocer in the preterite. Conocí a Laura en el trabajo. (I met Laura at work.) Asking for or ordering something: pedir. Siempre pido la sopa del día. (I always order the soup of the day.) Asking a question: preguntar. No sé. Pregúntale a Juan. (I don't know. Ask Juan.) Examples Spanish English No sé dónde están mis llaves. I don't know where my keys are. ¿Conoces un buen restaurante por aquí? Do you know a good restaurant around here? Conocí a mi mejor amiga en el colegio. I met my best friend at school. Sé que es tarde, pero tengo que hablar contigo. I know it's late, but I have to talk to you. ¿Pedimos la cuenta? Shall we ask for the check? Los turistas siempre me preguntan dónde está la catedral. Tourists always ask me where the cathedral is. Ayer tu madre preguntó por ti. Your mother asked about you yesterday. ¿Sabes cocinar? Can you cook? Good to know \"To ask a question\" is hacer una pregunta: ¿Puedo hacerte una pregunta? (see ). In Spain, pedir cita means \"to make an appointment\": Tengo que pedir cita con el médico. Common mistakes ✗ Sé a tu hermano. → ✓ Conozco a tu hermano. — people take conocer. ✗ No conozco dónde vive. → ✓ No sé dónde vive. — information after dónde, qué, cuándo takes saber. ✗ Conozco nadar. → ✓ Sé nadar. — a skill is saber + infinitive. ✗ Pregunté un café. → ✓ Pedí un café. — ordering is pedir. ✗ Pedí por la cuenta. → ✓ Pedí la cuenta. — pedir already means \"ask for\". Practice Complete with the present of saber or conocer. ¿ (tú) a mi hermana? No (yo) a qué hora sale el tren. Mis padres Madrid muy bien. ¿ (tú) hablar francés? Complete with pedir or preguntar. En el restaurante siempre (yo) pescado. Voy a dónde está la estación. Translate into Spanish. I don't know his name. I met Carlos in Mexico. Answers Conoces sé conocen Sabes pido preguntar No sé su nombre. / No sé cómo se llama. Conocí a Carlos en México.","level":"A2","category":"Grammar","route":"#/a2/grammar-saber-conocer"},{"id":"page:a2-grammar-connectors-basic","group":"pages","title":"Basic connectors; también and tampoco","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Y, pero, porque, por eso, cuando, entonces, and how to say \"me too\" and \"me neither\" with yo también and yo tampoco.","text":"Connectors join ideas: y (and), pero (but), porque (because). Most work as in English, with three traps. Y and o change before some words: Ana e Isabel, siete u ocho. \"Because\" is porque, one word, but \"why?\" is ¿por qué?, two words with an accent. And \"me neither\" is yo tampoco: Spanish never says también no. How it works Connector Meaning Example y (e before i- / hi-) and pan y queso, padre e hijo o (u before o- / ho-) or ¿Té o café?, siete u ocho pero but Es caro, pero es muy bueno. porque because No voy porque estoy cansado. por eso that's why, so Estoy cansado; por eso no voy. así que so Llovía, así que nos quedamos en casa. cuando when Cuando llego a casa, ceno. entonces then, so Entonces, ¿vienes o no? porque or ¿por qué? Form Meaning Example ¿por qué? why? (a question) ¿Por qué no vienes? porque because (the answer) Porque tengo que trabajar. también and tampoco Agree with a positive sentence: también. —Soy de Chile. —Yo también. Agree with a negative sentence: tampoco. —No tengo perro. —Yo tampoco. Disagree: —Tengo hambre. —Yo no. / —No tengo hambre. —Yo sí. With gustar, encantar and doler, the person starts with a: A mí también. A mí tampoco. Tampoco before the verb replaces no: Yo tampoco voy = Yo no voy tampoco. When to use it Adding: y, además. Tengo un hermano y una hermana. (I have a brother and a sister.) Contrast: pero. Quería ir a la fiesta, pero no pude. (I wanted to go to the party, but I couldn't.) Cause: porque. No como carne porque soy vegetariana. (I don't eat meat because I'm a vegetarian.) Result: por eso, así que. Perdí el autobús, así que llegué tarde. (I missed the bus, so I arrived late.) Time: cuando. Cuando era niño, vivía en Quito. (When I was a child, I lived in Quito.) Agreeing: también, tampoco. —Mañana no trabajo. —Yo tampoco. (I'm not working tomorrow. Me neither.) Examples Spanish English El hotel estaba lejos del centro; por eso tomamos un taxi. The hotel was far from downtown; that's why we took a taxi. —Vivo en el centro. —¡Yo también! \"I live downtown.\" \"Me too!\" —No me gusta el fútbol. —A mí tampoco. \"I don't like soccer.\" \"Me neither.\" La casa es pequeña, pero tiene mucha luz. The house is small, but it has a lot of light. Mi hermano tampoco come carne. My brother doesn't eat meat either. Good to know Y becomes e only before the sound i: Ana e Isabel, but agua y hielo (hie- sounds like \"ye\"). Common mistakes ✗ Yo también no. → ✓ Yo tampoco. — to agree with a negative sentence, use tampoco. ✗ Me también. → ✓ Yo también. — \"me too\" is yo también (with gustar: a mí también). ✗ No fui por qué estaba enfermo. → ✓ No fui porque estaba enfermo. — \"because\" is one word without an accent. ✗ ¿Porque no vienes? → ✓ ¿Por qué no vienes? — \"why\" is two words with an accent. ✗ Ana y Isabel son hermanas. → ✓ Ana e Isabel son hermanas. — before i- or hi-, y becomes e. Practice Choose y or e, o or u. Hablo español inglés. ¿Vienen siete ocho personas? Complete with pero, porque or por eso. Me gusta mucho esta casa, es muy cara. No salgo esta noche mañana tengo un examen. Estaba muy cansado; me acosté temprano. Reply. —No me gusta el café. — (Me neither.) —Soy de Colombia. — (Me too.) Translate into Spanish. \"Why don't you come?\" \"Because I have to work.\" Answers e u pero porque por eso A mí tampoco. Yo también. —¿Por qué no vienes? —Porque tengo que trabajar.","level":"A2","category":"Grammar","route":"#/a2/grammar-connectors-basic"},{"id":"page:a2-functions-invitations-plans","group":"pages","title":"Suggest, invite, accept and decline","headings":["Key phrases","Dialogue","Culture note","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"¿Vamos al cine?, ¿te apetece…?, ¿quedamos a las ocho?: make plans with friends, say yes, or say no politely.","text":"To suggest a plan, Spanish often uses a simple question in the present: ¿Vamos al cine? (Shall we go to the movies?) \"Do you feel like…?\" is ¿Te apetece…? (Spain) or ¿Tienes ganas de…?; apetecer works like gustar: me apetece. To say no, people say sorry, give a reason with es que… and suggest another day. Key phrases Suggesting and inviting Spanish English ¿Quieres venir a cenar el sábado? Do you want to come for dinner on Saturday? ¿Te apetece ir al cine? (Spain) / ¿Tienes ganas de ir al cine? Do you feel like going to the movies? ¿Por qué no vamos a la playa? Why don't we go to the beach? ¿Quedamos el viernes? (Spain) / ¿Nos vemos el viernes? Shall we meet on Friday? Accepting Spanish English ¡Claro! / ¡De acuerdo! / ¡Vale! (Spain) Sure! / OK! ¡Buena idea! / ¡Genial! Good idea! / Great! Me encantaría. I'd love to. (a fixed phrase) Declining Spanish English Lo siento, no puedo. I'm sorry, I can't. Es que tengo que trabajar. The thing is, I have to work. Mejor otro día. / Mejor el domingo. Another day would be better. / Sunday would be better. Arranging Spanish English ¿A qué hora quedamos? (Spain) / ¿A qué hora nos vemos? What time shall we meet? ¿Dónde quedamos? (Spain) / ¿Dónde nos vemos? Where shall we meet? Dialogue Lucía calls her friend Marcos in Madrid. Lucía: Marcos, ¿te apetece ir al cine el viernes? (Marcos, do you feel like going to the movies on Friday?) Marcos: Lo siento, el viernes no puedo. Es que tengo una cena con mis padres. (Sorry, I can't on Friday. I have a dinner with my parents.) Lucía: ¡Qué pena! ¿Y el sábado? (What a shame! What about Saturday?) Marcos: El sábado sí. ¿Qué película quieres ver? (Saturday is fine. Which movie do you want to see?) Lucía: Una comedia española. (A Spanish comedy.) Marcos: Vale. ¿A qué hora quedamos? (OK. What time shall we meet?) Lucía: La sesión es a las ocho. ¿Quedamos a las siete y media en la puerta del cine? (The showing is at eight. Shall we meet at seven thirty at the entrance to the movie theater?) Marcos: Perfecto. Y después, ¿vamos a cenar algo? (Perfect. And afterwards, shall we get something to eat?) Lucía: ¡Buena idea! Hasta el sábado. (Good idea! See you on Saturday.) Culture note Quedar (to meet up) is everyday Spanish in Spain. In Latin America people usually say nos vemos. A showing is la sesión (Spain) or la función (Latin America). Te invito can also mean \"it's on me\": the person who invites often pays. Common mistakes ✗ Yo apetezco ir al cine. → ✓ Me apetece ir al cine. — apetecer works like gustar. ✗ ¿Te apetece a ir al cine? → ✓ ¿Te apetece ir al cine? — apetecer takes the infinitive directly. ✗ ¿Quedamos en las ocho? → ✓ ¿Quedamos a las ocho? — a clock time takes a. ✗ Gracias para la invitación. → ✓ Gracias por la invitación. — thanks take por. Practice What do you say? Invite a friend to go to the movies on Saturday. Accept an invitation. Say sorry, you can't, because you have to work. Ask a friend what time you are meeting. Complete. ¿Te ir a la playa mañana? (do you feel like) ¿ no vamos a tomar algo? (why) Translate into Spanish. Thanks for the invitation. It's on me. Answers ¿Quieres ir al cine el sábado? / ¿Vamos al cine el sábado? / ¿Te apetece ir al cine el sábado? / ¿Tienes ganas de ir al cine el sábado? / ¿Por qué no vamos al cine el sábado? ¡Claro! / ¡Vale! / ¡De acuerdo! / ¡Buena idea! / Me encantaría. Lo siento, no puedo. Es que tengo que trabajar. / Lo siento, no puedo porque tengo que trabajar. ¿A qué hora quedamos? / ¿A qué hora nos vemos? apetece Por qué Gracias por la invitación. / Muchas gracias por la invitación. Te invito. / Invito yo.","level":"A2","category":"Communication","route":"#/a2/functions-invitations-plans"},{"id":"page:a2-functions-tickets-bookings-problems","group":"pages","title":"Buy tickets, make bookings and report a simple problem","headings":["Key phrases","Dialogue","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Quería reservar una mesa para dos, ¿tienen habitaciones libres?, el aire no funciona: book, buy tickets and complain politely.","text":"To book something, start with Quería… (I'd like…), the polite form you know from ordering in a café: Quería reservar una mesa para dos. The number of people and the date take para: para cuatro, para el sábado. To report a problem, the key verb is funcionar: English \"it doesn't work\" is no funciona, not no trabaja. Key phrases Booking Spanish English Quería reservar una mesa para dos, por favor. I'd like to book a table for two, please. ¿Tienen habitaciones libres para esta noche? Do you have any rooms available for tonight? Tengo una reserva a nombre de Ana López. (Latin America also: una reservación) I have a reservation under the name Ana López. ¿Para cuántas personas? For how many people? ¿Está incluido el desayuno? Is breakfast included? Tickets Spanish English Dos entradas para la sesión de las ocho, por favor. (Spain) Two tickets for the eight o'clock showing, please. Un billete de ida y vuelta a Toledo. (Spain; Latin America: un boleto) A round-trip ticket to Toledo. ¿Hay descuento para estudiantes? Is there a student discount? Reporting a problem Spanish English El aire acondicionado no funciona. The air conditioning doesn't work. No hay agua caliente. There's no hot water. La habitación está sucia. The room is dirty. Falta una toalla. There's a towel missing. ¿Me puede cambiar de habitación? Can you give me a different room? Dialogue Andrés arrives at a hotel in Seville. Paula works at the front desk. Andrés: Buenas noches. Tengo una reserva a nombre de Andrés Molina. (Good evening. I have a reservation under the name Andrés Molina.) Paula: Sí, una habitación doble para tres noches. Es la 204. (Yes, a double room for three nights. It's room 204.) Andrés: (Later) Perdone, hay un problema con la habitación. El aire acondicionado no funciona y hace mucho calor. (Excuse me, there's a problem with the room. The air conditioning doesn't work and it's very hot.) Paula: Lo siento mucho. Ahora mismo viene alguien a revisarlo. (I'm very sorry. Someone is coming to check it right away.) Andrés: De acuerdo, gracias. Y también falta una toalla. (OK, thanks. And there's also a towel missing.) Paula: (Ten minutes later) Señor Molina, hoy no podemos arreglar el aire. ¿Le cambio de habitación? (Mr. Molina, we can't fix the air conditioning today. Shall I give you a different room?) Andrés: Sí, por favor. Muchas gracias. (Yes, please. Thank you very much.) Paula: Aquí tiene la llave de la 310, y una toalla. (Here's the key to 310, and a towel.) Good to know Tickets: la entrada for a show or a museum, el billete (Spain) or el boleto (Latin America) for transportation. Common mistakes ✗ El aire acondicionado no trabaja. → ✓ El aire acondicionado no funciona. — machines funcionan; people trabajan. ✗ Quería reservar una mesa por dos personas. → ✓ Quería reservar una mesa para dos personas. — the number of people takes para. ✗ La habitación es sucia. → ✓ La habitación está sucia. — a state at the moment takes estar. ✗ No hay caliente agua. → ✓ No hay agua caliente. — the adjective goes after the noun. Practice What do you say? Book a table for four for Saturday. At the hotel, say you have a reservation under the name García. Say that the shower doesn't work. Ask if breakfast is included. Complete. ¿Tienen habitaciones para esta noche? (available) ¿Hay para estudiantes? (a discount) Translate into Spanish. There's no hot water. Can you give me a different room? (formal) Answers Quería reservar una mesa para cuatro para el sábado. / … para cuatro personas para el sábado. / … para cuatro el sábado. Tengo una reserva a nombre de García. / Tengo una reservación a nombre de García. La ducha no funciona. (Mexico: la regadera) ¿Está incluido el desayuno? / ¿El desayuno está incluido? libres descuento No hay agua caliente. ¿Me puede cambiar de habitación? / ¿Puede cambiarme de habitación? / ¿Me puede dar otra habitación?","level":"A2","category":"Communication","route":"#/a2/functions-tickets-bookings-problems"},{"id":"page:a2-functions-health","group":"pages","title":"Talk about health at the doctor's or pharmacy","headings":["Key phrases","Dialogue","Culture note","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Me duele la cabeza, tengo fiebre, ¿tiene algo para la tos?: say what is wrong, answer the doctor and ask at the pharmacy.","text":"Pain works like gustar: Me duele la cabeza (my head hurts, word for word \"the head hurts me\"), and with a plural, Me duelen los pies. Spanish uses la, not mi, with body parts, because me already says whose head it is. Many symptoms take tener: Tengo fiebre. Tengo tos. At the doctor's, people use usted. Key phrases Saying what is wrong Spanish English Me siento mal. I don't feel well. Me duele la cabeza. I have a headache. Tengo dolor de garganta. I have a sore throat. Tengo fiebre. / Tengo tos. I have a fever. / I have a cough. Estoy resfriado. / Estoy resfriada. I have a cold. Me duele desde ayer. / Me duele desde hace dos días. It has hurt since yesterday. / It has hurt for two days. The doctor asks and says Spanish English ¿Qué le pasa? What's the matter? ¿Desde cuándo? Since when? ¿Es alérgico a algún medicamento? Are you allergic to any medicine? Tome una pastilla cada ocho horas. Take one pill every eight hours. Tiene que descansar y beber mucha agua. You need to rest and drink plenty of water. At the pharmacy and on the phone Spanish English ¿Tiene algo para la tos? Do you have anything for a cough? ¿Necesito receta? Do I need a prescription? Quería pedir cita con el médico. (Spain) / Quería hacer una cita con el doctor. (Latin America) I'd like to make an appointment with the doctor. Dialogue Isabel is at the doctor's in Guadalajara, Mexico. Doctor: Buenos días. ¿Qué le pasa? (Good morning. What's the matter?) Isabel: Me siento muy mal. Me duele mucho la garganta y tengo tos. (I feel really unwell. My throat really hurts and I have a cough.) Doctor: ¿Desde cuándo? (Since when?) Isabel: Desde hace tres días. Y anoche tuve fiebre. (For three days. And last night I had a fever.) Doctor: A ver… Abra la boca, por favor. Sí, tiene la garganta muy roja. ¿Es alérgica a algún medicamento? (Let's see… Open your mouth, please. Yes, your throat is very red. Are you allergic to any medicine?) Isabel: No, a ninguno. (No, none.) Doctor: Bien. Tome este jarabe tres veces al día y beba mucha agua. Tiene que descansar dos o tres días. (Good. Take this syrup three times a day and drink plenty of water. You need to rest for two or three days.) Isabel: De acuerdo. Muchas gracias, doctor. (OK. Thank you very much, doctor.) Culture note Desde + a point in time (desde ayer, desde el lunes) and desde hace + a length of time (desde hace dos días) both go with the present: Me duele desde ayer. English uses \"has hurt\". To wish someone well, say ¡Que te mejores! (to a friend) or ¡Que se mejore! (formal): \"Get well soon!\" Learn it as a fixed phrase. Common mistakes ✗ Me duele mi cabeza. → ✓ Me duele la cabeza. — body parts take el / la; me already says whose. ✗ Me duele los pies. → ✓ Me duelen los pies. — like gustar: a plural thing takes duelen. ✗ Estoy fiebre. → ✓ Tengo fiebre. — fever and cough take tener. ✗ ¿Tiene algo por la tos? → ✓ ¿Tiene algo para la tos? — a remedy \"for\" a problem takes para. Practice What do you say? Say that your head hurts. Ask at the pharmacy for something for a cough. Say that you have had a fever since yesterday. The doctor asks ¿Desde cuándo? Answer: for two days. Complete. Me las piernas. (doler) ¿Es usted alérgico a medicamento? (any) Translate into Spanish. I have a cold. Take one pill every eight hours. (formal) Answers Me duele la cabeza. / Tengo dolor de cabeza. ¿Tiene algo para la tos? Tengo fiebre desde ayer. / Desde ayer tengo fiebre. Desde hace dos días. duelen algún Estoy resfriado. / Estoy resfriada. / Tengo un resfriado. Tome una pastilla cada ocho horas.","level":"A2","category":"Communication","route":"#/a2/functions-health"},{"id":"page:a2-vocabulary-health-body","group":"pages","title":"Body, health and feelings","headings":["Words","Word partners","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Parts of the body, common illnesses and medicine, and feelings with estar: estoy resfriado, nervioso, contento.","text":"With parts of the body, Spanish uses el / la where English says \"my\": Me lavo las manos (I wash my hands). Feelings and states of health take estar: Estoy cansada. Estoy resfriado. And careful with English \"I have a cold\": tengo frío means \"I'm cold\". A cold is un resfriado. Words The body Spanish English el cuerpo the body la cabeza the head la cara the face los ojos / la nariz / la boca the eyes / the nose / the mouth los dientes the teeth la oreja / el oído the ear (outer) / the ear (inner), hearing el cuello the neck la garganta the throat el hombro the shoulder el pecho the chest la espalda the back el brazo the arm la mano the hand el dedo the finger, the toe el estómago (informal: la barriga; Latin America also: la panza) the stomach, the belly la pierna the leg la rodilla the knee el pie the foot Health Spanish English la salud health el dolor (de cabeza, de muelas) the pain, the ache (headache, toothache) la fiebre the fever la tos the cough el resfriado (Latin America also: el resfrío) the cold la gripe (Mexico, Colombia: la gripa) the flu la alergia the allergy el médico / la médica (Latin America also: el doctor / la doctora) the doctor el hospital the hospital la receta the prescription la pastilla the pill el jarabe the syrup estar enfermo / enferma to be sick, ill estar resfriado / resfriada to have a cold estar mareado / mareada to feel dizzy Feelings Spanish English contento / contenta happy, pleased triste sad nervioso / nerviosa nervous tranquilo / tranquila calm preocupado / preocupada worried cansado / cansada tired enfadado (Spain) / enojado (Latin America) angry tener miedo to be afraid Word partners Spanish English Me duele la espalda. My back hurts. Me lavo las manos antes de comer. I wash my hands before eating. Mi hijo tiene fiebre y hoy no va al colegio. My son has a fever and isn't going to school today. Tengo dolor de estómago. I have a stomachache. Me rompí el brazo el año pasado. I broke my arm last year. Estoy un poco resfriada. I have a bit of a cold. Estoy muy contento con mi nuevo trabajo. I'm very happy with my new job. Mi madre está preocupada por mí. My mother is worried about me. ¿Por qué estás enfadado? (Spain) / ¿Por qué estás enojado? (Latin America) Why are you angry? Good to know La mano is feminine although it ends in -o: la mano derecha (the right hand). Oreja is the part you can see; oído is inside: Me duele el oído (I have an earache). Ser or estar: Es nervioso (he is a nervous person) / Está nervioso (he is nervous now). For how you feel today, use estar. Common mistakes ✗ Tengo un frío. → ✓ Estoy resfriado. — \"a cold\" is un resfriado; tengo frío means \"I'm cold\". ✗ Me lavo mis manos. → ✓ Me lavo las manos. — with body parts, use el / la / los / las. ✗ el mano → ✓ la mano — mano is feminine. ✗ Estoy muy embarazada. (meaning \"embarrassed\") → ✓ Me da mucha vergüenza. — embarazada means \"pregnant\"; \"it embarrasses me\" is me da vergüenza. ✗ Soy cansado. → ✓ Estoy cansado. — feeling tired is a state: estar. Practice Write the word with el or la. hand back knee throat Write the adjective in the right form. Mi madre está muy porque mi hermano no ha llamado. (worried) Mis hijos están muy porque mañana empiezan las vacaciones. (happy) Translate into Spanish. I have a stomachache. She has a cold. Answers la mano la espalda la rodilla la garganta preocupada contentos / felices Me duele el estómago. / Tengo dolor de estómago. / Me duele la barriga. / Me duele la panza. Está resfriada. / Ella está resfriada. / Tiene un resfriado. / Tiene un resfrío.","level":"A2","category":"Vocabulary","route":"#/a2/vocabulary-health-body"},{"id":"summary:A1","group":"pages","title":"Level summary A1","headings":["A1 in a nutshell"],"summary":"","text":"A1 in a nutshell What you can do at this level You can introduce yourself and others, say where you are from and what you do, and ask the same questions back. You can talk about your family, your home and your town, your day and your plans, and you can order in a café, buy food and ask what things cost. You understand people who speak slowly and clearly, and you know how to ask them to repeat. Key ideas Two verbs for \"to be\". Ser says what something is (Soy profesora, Es de Chile); estar says where it is and how it is now (Estoy en casa, Estoy cansada). Endings show the person. Hablo, hablas, habla…: the pronoun is usually left out. Every noun is masculine or feminine, and articles and adjectives agree with it: la casa blanca, los coches rojos. Gustar works backwards: the thing you like is the subject. Me gusta el café. Me gustan los gatos. Ir a + infinitive is your first future: Voy a estudiar. The most common trap Translating \"to be\" word for word. Age, hunger and cold take tener (Tengo 30 años, tengo hambre), location takes estar, and a job takes ser without un / una (Es médico). How long it takes Reaching A1 from zero usually takes about 80–100 hours of guided study plus practice on your own. With 3–4 hours a week, that is roughly 5–7 months. Speaking a little every day helps more than one long session a week. These are typical ranges for adult learners; your pace depends on how much time you spend with the language. What next You are ready for A2 when you can talk for two or three minutes about yourself, your family and your day, and ask someone a few questions about theirs. At A2 you will learn to talk about the past (ayer comí, de niño vivía) and to describe things in more detail.","level":"A1","category":null,"route":"#/a1"},{"id":"summary:A2","group":"pages","title":"Level summary A2","headings":["A2 in a nutshell"],"summary":"","text":"A2 in a nutshell What you can do at this level You can tell what you did last weekend or on vacation, describe how things used to be when you were a child, and say what you have or haven't done yet. You can describe people, places and things, compare them, and talk about what is happening now and what will happen. You can get around: buy tickets, book a room or a table, ask the way and follow directions, see a doctor or a pharmacist, make plans with friends and report a simple problem politely. Key ideas Two simple past tenses. The preterite tells what happened (Ayer fui al cine); the imperfect describes the scene and habits (De niño iba mucho al cine). Together: Llovía cuando salí de casa. The present perfect looks at the past from now: experiences and things not done yet (¿Has estado alguna vez en México? Todavía no he comido). In Spain it is also normal for today. Object pronouns go before the verb: Lo compré ayer. Le doy el libro a Ana. With an infinitive or gerund they may also be attached: Voy a comprarlo. por and para both mean \"for\": para looks ahead to a goal (para ti, para viajar), por looks at the cause or the way (gracias por todo, por teléfono). Ordinary verbs + infinitive do the work of \"must\", \"can\" and \"will\": tengo que, hay que, puedo, sé nadar; and the future adds endings to the infinitive: llamaré, comeré (a few verbs have short stems: tendré, haré). The most common trap Choosing the past tense by the English verb. \"I was\" can be estuve, fui, estaba or era; ask whether it is an event (preterite) or a background or habit (imperfect), and use the preterite with a finished time like ayer or el año pasado, never the perfect. How long it takes Going from A1 to A2 usually takes about 100–150 more hours of guided study plus practice on your own. With 3–4 hours a week, that is roughly 7–10 months. Telling short stories about your own day out loud is the fastest way to make the past tenses automatic. These are typical ranges for adult learners; your pace depends on how much time you spend with the language. What next You are ready for B1 when you can tell a short story about a trip or your childhood in two or three minutes, mixing the preterite and the imperfect, and deal with a hotel, a doctor or a ticket office without switching to English. At B1 you will meet the subjunctive (quiero que vengas), the conditional (me gustaría) and the pluperfect (ya había salido), and learn to give opinions and argue a point.","level":"A2","category":null,"route":"#/a2"},{"id":"cheatsheet:A1","group":"pages","title":"Cheat sheet A1","headings":["Forms","Grammar","Phrases","Signal words","Watch out"],"summary":"The forms, rules, phrases and traps of A1 on one page.","text":"A1 cheat sheet Forms Form Pattern Example ser soy, eres, es, somos, sois, son Soy de Lima. Es médica. estar estoy, estás, está, estamos, estáis, están Estoy en casa. Está cansado. tener tengo, tienes, tiene, tenemos, tenéis, tienen Tengo dos hermanos. -ar / -er / -ir -o, -as/-es, -a/-e, -amos/-emos/-imos, -áis/-éis/-ís, -an/-en hablo, comes, vivimos stem changes e→ie, o→ue, e→i (not nosotros, vosotros) quiero, puedo, pido yo in -go hago, salgo, pongo, tengo, traigo Salgo a las ocho. ir (a) voy, vas, va, vamos, vais, van + a + infinitive Voy a estudiar. reflexive me, te, se, nos, os, se + verb Me levanto a las siete. Grammar Articles: el, la, los, las / un, una, unos, unas; a + el = al, de + el = del. No article with a job: Es profesor. Agreement: el chico alto, la chica alta, los chicos altos; colors after the noun: un coche rojo. ser / estar: what it is (ser) vs where it is and how it is now (estar). Events: La fiesta es en mi casa. hay / está: Hay un banco (something new) / El banco está allí (a known thing). gustar: me / te / le / nos / os / les + gusta (one thing, an action) / gustan (plural). Questions and no: ¿Dónde vives? No vivo aquí. No tengo nada. Question words have accents. Possessives / demonstratives: mi, tu, su, nuestro; este, ese, aquel. Phrases Spanish English Hola, me llamo… Mucho gusto. Hi, my name is… Nice to meet you. ¿De dónde eres? —Soy de… Where are you from? —I'm from… ¿Cuántos años tienes? —Tengo… años. How old are you? —I'm… ¿A qué te dedicas? —Soy… What do you do? —I'm a… ¿Qué hora es? —Son las tres y media. What time is it? —It's half past three. ¿A qué hora abre? What time does it open? ¿Hay un banco por aquí? Is there a bank around here? Quería un café, por favor. I'd like a coffee, please. ¿Cuánto cuesta? / ¿Cuánto es? How much is it? La cuenta, por favor. The check, please. ¿Puede repetir, por favor? Can you repeat that, please? Más despacio, por favor. Slower, please. ¿Cómo se dice … en español? How do you say … in Spanish? Me gusta… / No me gusta… I like… / I don't like… Signal words Spanish English siempre / a veces / nunca always / sometimes / never todos los días / los lunes every day / on Mondays por la mañana / tarde / noche in the morning / afternoon / at night a las ocho at eight hoy / mañana / esta noche today / tomorrow / tonight el fin de semana on the weekend en julio / en verano in July / in summer aquí / ahí / allí (Latin America also acá / allá) here / there / over there Watch out ✗ Soy 25 años. → ✓ Tengo 25 años. ✗ Soy cansado. → ✓ Estoy cansado. ✗ Yo gusto el café. → ✓ Me gusta el café. ✗ la casa blanco → ✓ la casa blanca ✗ en el lunes → ✓ el lunes","level":"A1","category":"Cheat sheet","route":"#/a1/cheatsheet"},{"id":"cheatsheet:A2","group":"pages","title":"Cheat sheet A2","headings":["Forms","Grammar","Phrases","Signal words","Watch out"],"summary":"The past tenses, pronouns, por and para, phrases and traps of A2 on one page.","text":"A2 cheat sheet Forms Form Pattern Example preterite -ar -é, -aste, -ó, -amos, -asteis, -aron Ayer hablé con Ana. preterite -er / -ir -í, -iste, -ió, -imos, -isteis, -ieron Comimos en casa. irregular preterite fui (ser / ir), tuve, estuve, hice / hizo, pude, puse, vine, dije El sábado fui a la playa. imperfect -aba, -abas… / -ía, -ías…; era, iba, veía De niño jugaba en la calle. present perfect he, has, ha, hemos, habéis, han + -ado / -ido Todavía no he comido. estar + gerund estoy… + -ando / -iendo (leyendo, durmiendo) Estoy preparando la cena. future infinitive + -é, -ás, -á, -emos, -éis, -án; tendré, haré, diré Mañana lloverá. imperative tú: habla, come (di, haz, ve, pon, sal, ten, ven) · usted: hable, coma Gira a la derecha. Grammar Preterite or imperfect? Event, one time, closed period → preterite. Scene, habit, age, time → imperfect: Dormía cuando sonó el teléfono. Object pronouns before the verb: lo, la, los, las (it, him, her, them), le, les (to him / her / you / them). Personal a before a person: Veo a María. Compare: más / menos … que, tan … como, el más alto de; mejor, peor; más de + number. por / para: para = purpose, recipient, destination, deadline; por = cause, thanks, through a place, means, price. saber / conocer: facts and skills (sé nadar) / people and places (conozco a Luis, conozco Lima). pedir / preguntar: ask for / ask a question. Phrases Spanish English ¿Qué tal el fin de semana? How was your weekend? Lo pasé muy bien. (Latin America also: La pasé muy bien.) I had a great time. ¿Has estado alguna vez en…? Have you ever been to…? ¿Te apetece ir al cine? (Spain) / ¿Tienes ganas de…? Do you feel like going to the movies? Lo siento, no puedo. Es que… Sorry, I can't. The thing is… Perdone, ¿para ir a la estación? Excuse me, how do I get to the station? Siga todo recto (Latin America: derecho) y gire a la derecha. Go straight ahead and turn right. Quería reservar una mesa para dos. I'd like to book a table for two. El aire acondicionado no funciona. The air conditioning doesn't work. Me duele la cabeza. / Tengo fiebre. I have a headache. / I have a fever. ¿Tiene algo para la tos? Do you have anything for a cough? Signal words Spanish English ayer, anoche, el año pasado, hace dos años yesterday, last night, last year, two years ago antes, de niño, siempre, todos los veranos before, as a child, always, every summer mientras while ya, todavía no, alguna vez, nunca already, not yet, ever, never hoy, esta mañana, esta semana (Spain: perfect) today, this morning, this week ahora mismo, estos días right now, these days mañana, la semana que viene, pronto, algún día tomorrow, next week, soon, one day Watch out ✗ Ayer he visto a Marta. → ✓ Ayer vi a Marta. ✗ Cuando fui niño, vivía en Cádiz. → ✓ Cuando era niño, vivía en Cádiz. ✗ Gracias para el regalo. → ✓ Gracias por el regalo. ✗ Lo voy a comprarlo. → ✓ Lo voy a comprar. / Voy a comprarlo. ✗ Yo también no. → ✓ Yo tampoco.","level":"A2","category":"Cheat sheet","route":"#/a2/cheatsheet"},{"id":"review","group":"exercises","title":"Flashcards and mistakes","headings":[],"summary":"One review queue: deck cards, My mistakes and weak spots from exercises.","text":"flashcards review decks mistakes weak spots","level":null,"category":"Flashcards","route":"#/review"},{"id":"review:words","group":"exercises","title":"My words","headings":[],"summary":"Words and phrases you selected in texts, with their sentence and meaning.","text":"my words personal vocabulary flashcards","level":null,"category":"Flashcards","route":"#/review/words"},{"id":"deck:vocab-a1-numbers-days-months","group":"exercises","title":"Numbers, days and months (flashcards)","headings":[],"summary":"Numbers 0–100, the days of the week and the months, for the topic a1-vocabulary-numbers-days-months.","text":"flashcards deck Numbers, days and months A1","level":null,"category":"Flashcards","route":"#/review/decks/deck/vocab-a1-numbers-days-months"},{"id":"deck:vocab-a1-family-people","group":"exercises","title":"Family and people (flashcards)","headings":[],"summary":"Family, describing people, nationalities and jobs, for the topic a1-vocabulary-family-people.","text":"flashcards deck Family and people A1","level":null,"category":"Flashcards","route":"#/review/decks/deck/vocab-a1-family-people"},{"id":"deck:vocab-a1-home-city","group":"exercises","title":"Home and town (flashcards)","headings":[],"summary":"Rooms, furniture and places in town, for the topic a1-vocabulary-home-city.","text":"flashcards deck Home and town A1","level":null,"category":"Flashcards","route":"#/review/decks/deck/vocab-a1-home-city"},{"id":"deck:vocab-a1-food-shopping","group":"exercises","title":"Food, drink and shopping (flashcards)","headings":[],"summary":"Food, drink, shops, amounts and prices, for the topic a1-vocabulary-food-shopping.","text":"flashcards deck Food, drink and shopping A1","level":null,"category":"Flashcards","route":"#/review/decks/deck/vocab-a1-food-shopping"},{"id":"deck:ser-estar","group":"exercises","title":"ser or estar (flashcards)","headings":[],"summary":"Choose and conjugate ser or estar in everyday sentences (A1–B1). The A1 part covers identity, origin, jobs, location, states and events; the A2 part adds the past tenses, estar + gerund and adjectives whose meaning changes with the verb.","text":"flashcards deck ser or estar A1","level":null,"category":"Flashcards","route":"#/review/decks/deck/ser-estar"},{"id":"deck:verbs-stem-changing","group":"exercises","title":"Stem-changing verbs (flashcards)","headings":[],"summary":"e→ie, o→ue, e→i and u→ue in the present (A1–A2); the A2 part adds -ir verbs in the preterite and the gerund, and seguir in the imperative.","text":"flashcards deck Stem-changing verbs A1","level":null,"category":"Flashcards","route":"#/review/decks/deck/verbs-stem-changing"},{"id":"deck:vocab-a2-travel-transport","group":"exercises","title":"Travel and transportation (flashcards)","headings":[],"summary":"Trains, buses, planes, tickets and hotel rooms, for the topic a2-vocabulary-travel-transport.","text":"flashcards deck Travel and transportation A2","level":null,"category":"Flashcards","route":"#/review/decks/deck/vocab-a2-travel-transport"},{"id":"deck:vocab-a2-free-time-weather","group":"exercises","title":"Free time and weather (flashcards)","headings":[],"summary":"Weather, sports and hobbies, for the topic a2-vocabulary-free-time-weather.","text":"flashcards deck Free time and weather A2","level":null,"category":"Flashcards","route":"#/review/decks/deck/vocab-a2-free-time-weather"},{"id":"deck:vocab-a2-shopping-clothes","group":"exercises","title":"Shopping and clothes (flashcards)","headings":[],"summary":"Clothes, colors and the words of a clothes shop, for the topic a2-vocabulary-shopping-clothes.","text":"flashcards deck Shopping and clothes A2","level":null,"category":"Flashcards","route":"#/review/decks/deck/vocab-a2-shopping-clothes"},{"id":"deck:vocab-a2-health-body","group":"exercises","title":"Body and health (flashcards)","headings":[],"summary":"Parts of the body, illnesses, medicine and feelings, for the topic a2-vocabulary-health-body.","text":"flashcards deck Body and health A2","level":null,"category":"Flashcards","route":"#/review/decks/deck/vocab-a2-health-body"},{"id":"deck:verbs-preterite-irregular","group":"exercises","title":"Irregular preterites (flashcards)","headings":[],"summary":"The irregular pretérito indefinido in everyday sentences: fui, tuve, estuve, hice, dije, pidió (A2).","text":"flashcards deck Irregular preterites A2","level":null,"category":"Flashcards","route":"#/review/decks/deck/verbs-preterite-irregular"},{"id":"deck:verbs-like-gustar","group":"exercises","title":"Verbs like gustar (flashcards)","headings":[],"summary":"gustar, encantar, doler, quedar, apetecer (Spain) and faltar, where the thing is the subject (A2–B1). This A2 part covers the present and the past.","text":"flashcards deck Verbs like gustar A2","level":null,"category":"Flashcards","route":"#/review/decks/deck/verbs-like-gustar"},{"id":"deck:por-para","group":"exercises","title":"por or para (flashcards)","headings":[],"summary":"Choose por or para in everyday sentences (A2–B1). This A2 part covers purpose, people, places, deadlines, thanks, causes, means and prices.","text":"flashcards deck por or para A2","level":null,"category":"Flashcards","route":"#/review/decks/deck/por-para"},{"id":"deck:verbs-core-100","group":"exercises","title":"100 core verbs (flashcards)","headings":[],"summary":"The 100 most useful verbs (A1–A2): English to infinitive, and the present tense in sentences.","text":"flashcards deck 100 core verbs A1","level":null,"category":"Flashcards","route":"#/review/decks/deck/verbs-core-100"},{"id":"set:a1-verbs-gustar-1","group":"exercises","title":"gustar: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: choose the form, fill the gaps, build sentences and translate.","text":"Topic: gustar and similar verbs","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-verbs-gustar-1"},{"id":"set:a1-verbs-gustar-2","group":"exercises","title":"gustar: write it yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: type the missing word, rewrite sentences, write what you hear and build a question.","text":"Topic: gustar and similar verbs","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-verbs-gustar-2"},{"id":"set:a1-verbs-gustar-3","group":"exercises","title":"gustar: agree, disagree and say how much","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: listen and write, rewrite, find the mistake, match reactions and fill a table with encantar.","text":"Topic: gustar and similar verbs","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-verbs-gustar-3"},{"id":"set:a1-grammar-alphabet-pronunciation-1","group":"exercises","title":"Sounds and spelling: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: stress, special letters, accents and question marks.","text":"Topic: Alphabet, pronunciation and stress","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-grammar-alphabet-pronunciation-1"},{"id":"set:a1-grammar-subject-pronouns-1","group":"exercises","title":"Subject pronouns: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: the right pronoun, tú or usted, and when to leave the pronoun out.","text":"Topic: Subject pronouns; tú vs usted","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-grammar-subject-pronouns-1"},{"id":"set:a1-verbs-ser-1","group":"exercises","title":"ser: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: the forms of ser, origin, jobs and descriptions.","text":"Topic: ser: identity, origin and profession","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-verbs-ser-1"},{"id":"set:a1-grammar-gender-number-1","group":"exercises","title":"Gender and plurals: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: el or la, plural endings and the usual exceptions.","text":"Topic: Gender and number of nouns","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-grammar-gender-number-1"},{"id":"set:a1-grammar-articles-1","group":"exercises","title":"Articles: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: el, la, un, una, al and del, and when to use no article.","text":"Topic: Definite and indefinite articles; al and del","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-grammar-articles-1"},{"id":"set:a1-grammar-adjective-agreement-1","group":"exercises","title":"Adjectives: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: agreement, position, nationalities and buen / gran.","text":"Topic: Adjective agreement and position","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-grammar-adjective-agreement-1"},{"id":"set:a1-verbs-tener-1","group":"exercises","title":"tener: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: the forms of tener, age, family and tener hambre / frío.","text":"Topic: tener: possession, age and common expressions","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-verbs-tener-1"},{"id":"set:a1-verbs-present-regular-1","group":"exercises","title":"Present tense: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: the endings of -ar, -er and -ir verbs, questions and negatives.","text":"Topic: Present tense of regular -ar, -er, -ir verbs","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-verbs-present-regular-1"},{"id":"set:a1-grammar-questions-negation-1","group":"exercises","title":"Questions and negation: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: question words, accents, no and nada / nadie / nunca.","text":"Topic: Question words and negation","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-grammar-questions-negation-1"},{"id":"set:a1-verbs-estar-hay-1","group":"exercises","title":"estar and hay: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: the forms of estar, location, feelings, and hay vs está.","text":"Topic: estar for location and states; hay vs está","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-verbs-estar-hay-1"},{"id":"set:a1-verbs-ser-vs-estar-basics-1","group":"exercises","title":"ser or estar: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: identity, origin, location, feelings, events and adjectives that change meaning.","text":"Topic: ser vs estar: the basics","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-verbs-ser-vs-estar-basics-1"},{"id":"set:a1-verbs-present-irregular-1","group":"exercises","title":"Irregular verbs: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: stem changes, yo forms in -go and ir.","text":"Topic: Common irregular and stem-changing verbs in the present","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-verbs-present-irregular-1"},{"id":"set:a1-verbs-reflexive-routine-1","group":"exercises","title":"Daily routine: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: reflexive pronouns, stem changes and your day.","text":"Topic: Reflexive verbs for the daily routine","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-verbs-reflexive-routine-1"},{"id":"set:a1-grammar-possessives-demonstratives-1","group":"exercises","title":"My, your, this, that: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: possessives, demonstratives and esto / eso.","text":"Topic: Possessives and demonstratives","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-grammar-possessives-demonstratives-1"},{"id":"set:a1-grammar-prepositions-place-time-1","group":"exercises","title":"Prepositions: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: en, a, de, por, con, para, and days without a preposition.","text":"Topic: Basic prepositions of place and time","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-grammar-prepositions-place-time-1"},{"id":"set:a1-verbs-ir-a-infinitive-1","group":"exercises","title":"ir a + infinitive: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: plans with ir a, questions, negatives and let's.","text":"Topic: ir a + infinitive: plans","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-verbs-ir-a-infinitive-1"},{"id":"set:a2-verbs-preterite-regular-1","group":"exercises","title":"Regular preterite: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: endings, accents, spelling changes and time words.","text":"Topic: Pretérito indefinido: regular verbs","level":"A2","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a2-verbs-preterite-regular-1"},{"id":"set:a2-verbs-preterite-irregular-1","group":"exercises","title":"Irregular preterite: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: new stems, ser and ir, stems in -j and -ir verbs with a stem change.","text":"Topic: Pretérito indefinido: common irregular verbs","level":"A2","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a2-verbs-preterite-irregular-1"},{"id":"set:a2-verbs-imperfect-1","group":"exercises","title":"Imperfect: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: endings and accents, ser, ir and había, habits and descriptions.","text":"Topic: Pretérito imperfecto: descriptions and habits in the past","level":"A2","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a2-verbs-imperfect-1"},{"id":"set:a2-verbs-preterite-vs-imperfect-basics-1","group":"exercises","title":"Indefinido or imperfecto: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: scene or event, habit or one time, interrupted actions.","text":"Topic: Indefinido vs imperfecto: first contrast","level":"A2","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a2-verbs-preterite-vs-imperfect-basics-1"},{"id":"set:a2-verbs-present-perfect-1","group":"exercises","title":"Present perfect: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: haber, participles, ya, todavía no and nunca.","text":"Topic: Pretérito perfecto; ya, todavía no, alguna vez","level":"A2","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a2-verbs-present-perfect-1"},{"id":"set:a2-grammar-direct-object-pronouns-1","group":"exercises","title":"Direct object pronouns: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: lo, la, los, las, where they go, and the personal a.","text":"Topic: Direct object pronouns and personal a","level":"A2","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a2-grammar-direct-object-pronouns-1"},{"id":"set:a2-grammar-indirect-object-pronouns-1","group":"exercises","title":"Indirect object pronouns: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: me, te, le, nos, os, les, where they go, and the person twice.","text":"Topic: Indirect object pronouns","level":"A2","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a2-grammar-indirect-object-pronouns-1"},{"id":"set:a2-grammar-comparatives-superlatives-1","group":"exercises","title":"Comparatives and superlatives: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: más … que, tan … como, mejor, peor, mayor and the superlative.","text":"Topic: Comparatives and superlatives","level":"A2","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a2-grammar-comparatives-superlatives-1"},{"id":"set:a2-grammar-indefinites-quantifiers-1","group":"exercises","title":"Indefinites and quantifiers: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: algo, nada, alguien, nadie, algún, ningún, otro, mucho and muy.","text":"Topic: Indefinites and quantifiers","level":"A2","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a2-grammar-indefinites-quantifiers-1"},{"id":"set:a2-verbs-estar-gerund-1","group":"exercises","title":"estar + gerundio: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: estar, gerunds, irregular forms and pronouns.","text":"Topic: estar + gerundio: actions in progress","level":"A2","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a2-verbs-estar-gerund-1"},{"id":"set:a2-verbs-obligation-ability-1","group":"exercises","title":"Obligation and ability: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: tener que, hay que, deber, necesitar, poder and saber.","text":"Topic: Obligation, necessity and ability","level":"A2","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a2-verbs-obligation-ability-1"},{"id":"set:a2-verbs-future-simple-1","group":"exercises","title":"Future simple: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: endings, irregular stems, si + present and habrá.","text":"Topic: Future simple: predictions and promises","level":"A2","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a2-verbs-future-simple-1"},{"id":"set:a2-verbs-imperative-affirmative-1","group":"exercises","title":"Affirmative imperative: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: tú, usted and ustedes forms, irregular verbs and pronouns.","text":"Topic: Affirmative imperative: instructions and directions","level":"A2","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a2-verbs-imperative-affirmative-1"},{"id":"set:a2-grammar-por-para-basics-1","group":"exercises","title":"por and para: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: purpose, people, thanks, price, means and route.","text":"Topic: por and para: the basics","level":"A2","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a2-grammar-por-para-basics-1"},{"id":"set:a2-grammar-saber-conocer-1","group":"exercises","title":"saber, conocer, pedir, preguntar: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: knowing facts, people and places; asking for things and asking questions.","text":"Topic: Tricky verb pairs: saber vs conocer, pedir vs preguntar","level":"A2","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a2-grammar-saber-conocer-1"},{"id":"set:a2-grammar-connectors-basic-1","group":"exercises","title":"Connectors: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: y / e, pero, porque, por eso, así que, también and tampoco.","text":"Topic: Basic connectors; también and tampoco","level":"A2","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a2-grammar-connectors-basic-1"},{"id":"mix:mix-a1-u1","group":"exercises","title":"Unit review: Sounds and first words","headings":[],"summary":"Sounds and spelling, subject pronouns and ser, mixed.","text":"Mixed set. Topics: Alphabet, pronunciation and stress, Subject pronouns; tú vs usted, ser: identity, origin and profession","level":null,"category":"Mixed set","route":"#/practice/mix/mix-a1-u1"},{"id":"mix:mix-a1-u2","group":"exercises","title":"Unit review: People and things","headings":[],"summary":"Gender and plurals, articles, adjectives and tener, mixed.","text":"Mixed set. Topics: Gender and number of nouns, Definite and indefinite articles; al and del, Adjective agreement and position, tener: possession, age and common expressions","level":null,"category":"Mixed set","route":"#/practice/mix/mix-a1-u2"},{"id":"mix:mix-a1-u3","group":"exercises","title":"Unit review: Everyday life","headings":[],"summary":"Present tense, questions and negation, estar and hay, ser or estar, mixed.","text":"Mixed set. Topics: Present tense of regular -ar, -er, -ir verbs, Question words and negation, estar for location and states; hay vs está, ser vs estar: the basics","level":null,"category":"Mixed set","route":"#/practice/mix/mix-a1-u3"},{"id":"mix:mix-a1-u4","group":"exercises","title":"Unit review: Likes and routines","headings":[],"summary":"gustar, irregular verbs and the daily routine, mixed.","text":"Mixed set. Topics: gustar and similar verbs, Common irregular and stem-changing verbs in the present, Reflexive verbs for the daily routine","level":null,"category":"Mixed set","route":"#/practice/mix/mix-a1-u4"},{"id":"mix:mix-a1-u5","group":"exercises","title":"Unit review: Out and about","headings":[],"summary":"Possessives and demonstratives, prepositions and ir a + infinitive, mixed.","text":"Mixed set. Topics: Possessives and demonstratives, Basic prepositions of place and time, ir a + infinitive: plans","level":null,"category":"Mixed set","route":"#/practice/mix/mix-a1-u5"},{"id":"mix:mix-a2-u1","group":"exercises","title":"Unit review: Talking about the past","headings":[],"summary":"Regular and irregular verbs in the pretérito indefinido, mixed.","text":"Mixed set. Topics: Pretérito indefinido: regular verbs, Pretérito indefinido: common irregular verbs","level":null,"category":"Mixed set","route":"#/practice/mix/mix-a2-u1"},{"id":"mix:mix-a2-u2","group":"exercises","title":"Unit review: How things used to be","headings":[],"summary":"Imperfect, indefinido or imperfecto, and the present perfect, mixed.","text":"Mixed set. Topics: Pretérito imperfecto: descriptions and habits in the past, Indefinido vs imperfecto: first contrast, Pretérito perfecto; ya, todavía no, alguna vez","level":null,"category":"Mixed set","route":"#/practice/mix/mix-a2-u2"},{"id":"mix:mix-a2-u3","group":"exercises","title":"Unit review: Objects and comparisons","headings":[],"summary":"Direct and indirect object pronouns, comparisons, indefinites and quantifiers, mixed.","text":"Mixed set. Topics: Direct object pronouns and personal a, Indirect object pronouns, Comparatives and superlatives, Indefinites and quantifiers","level":null,"category":"Mixed set","route":"#/practice/mix/mix-a2-u3"},{"id":"mix:mix-a2-u4","group":"exercises","title":"Unit review: Now and next","headings":[],"summary":"estar + gerundio, obligation and ability, the future and the affirmative imperative, mixed.","text":"Mixed set. Topics: estar + gerundio: actions in progress, Obligation, necessity and ability, Future simple: predictions and promises, Affirmative imperative: instructions and directions","level":null,"category":"Mixed set","route":"#/practice/mix/mix-a2-u4"},{"id":"mix:mix-a2-u5","group":"exercises","title":"Unit review: Getting things done","headings":[],"summary":"por and para, saber / conocer and pedir / preguntar, and basic connectors with también and tampoco, mixed.","text":"Mixed set. Topics: por and para: the basics, Tricky verb pairs: saber vs conocer, pedir vs preguntar, Basic connectors; también and tampoco","level":null,"category":"Mixed set","route":"#/practice/mix/mix-a2-u5"},{"id":"mix:mix-a2-past-tenses","group":"exercises","title":"Mixed: the three past tenses","headings":[],"summary":"Pretérito indefinido, imperfecto and perfecto together: what happened, what it was like and what you have done.","text":"Mixed set. Topics: Pretérito indefinido: regular verbs, Pretérito indefinido: common irregular verbs, Pretérito imperfecto: descriptions and habits in the past, Indefinido vs imperfecto: first contrast, Pretérito perfecto; ya, todavía no, alguna vez","level":null,"category":"Mixed set","route":"#/practice/mix/mix-a2-past-tenses"},{"id":"mix:mix-a2-pronouns-pairs","group":"exercises","title":"Mixed: pronouns and tricky pairs","headings":[],"summary":"lo, la, le and les, por or para, saber or conocer, pedir or preguntar, mixed.","text":"Mixed set. Topics: Direct object pronouns and personal a, Indirect object pronouns, por and para: the basics, Tricky verb pairs: saber vs conocer, pedir vs preguntar","level":null,"category":"Mixed set","route":"#/practice/mix/mix-a2-pronouns-pairs"},{"id":"lab:presente","group":"lab","title":"Presente","headings":["Present"],"summary":"What is true now, what you do regularly, and general facts.","text":"True now Vivo en un apartamento pequeño cerca del centro. I live in a small apartment near downtown. Habits and routines Los sábados juego al tenis con mi hermana. On Saturdays I play tennis with my sister. General facts El sol sale por el este. The sun rises in the east. Happening at the moment ¿Qué haces? —Leo una novela. What are you doing? —I'm reading a novel. Fixed plans and timetables Mañana trabajo hasta las ocho. Tomorrow I'm working until eight. siempre normalmente todos los días los lunes a veces nunca ahora English says “I'm reading” for an action going on now. Spanish can just use the present: Leo una novela. Estoy leyendo is also very common, but you do not need it every time. The ending already shows the person, so the subject pronoun is usually left out: Vivo en Lima. Using yo in every sentence sounds unnatural; keep it for emphasis or contrast. Stem changes do not happen in the nosotros and vosotros forms: queremos, podemos, not *quieremos, *puedemos.","level":"A1","category":"Lab card","route":"#/lab/presente"},{"id":"lab:preterito-indefinido","group":"lab","title":"Pretérito indefinido","headings":["Preterite"],"summary":"Finished actions at a definite time in the past.","text":"One finished action Ayer comí con Lucía. Yesterday I had lunch with Lucía. A chain of actions Llegué a casa, cené y me acosté. I got home, had dinner and went to bed. A closed period of time Viví tres años en Lima. I lived in Lima for three years. ayer anoche el lunes la semana pasada en 2019 hace dos años The English simple past covers both one finished event and a habit: “I ate there yesterday” / “As a kid I ate there every Sunday”. Spanish separates them: comí (once, finished) and comía (habit or background). Present yo and preterite él differ only by the accent: hablo (I speak) / habló (he or she spoke). The preterite yo form is hablé.","level":"A2","category":"Lab card","route":"#/lab/preterito-indefinido"},{"id":"lab:preterito-imperfecto","group":"lab","title":"Pretérito imperfecto","headings":["Imperfect"],"summary":"Descriptions, background and habits in the past.","text":"Habits in the past De niña vivía en un pueblo e iba a la escuela a pie. As a girl I lived in a village and walked to school. Descriptions La casa era vieja y tenía un jardín enorme. The house was old and had a huge garden. Background to an event Hacía frío y llovía cuando llegamos. It was cold and raining when we arrived. Time and how someone felt Eran las once y yo tenía mucho sueño. It was eleven o'clock and I was very sleepy. antes de niño / de niña siempre todos los veranos normalmente mientras cuando era joven English “used to play” and “was playing” are both often jugaba in Spanish. The imperfecto covers habits and actions in progress. The imperfecto does not tell you that an action finished. For one finished event at a definite time, use the indefinido: Ayer vi a Pablo. Ayer veía a Pablo sounds unfinished, as if the story goes on. Watch the accents: hablábamos (only the nosotros form of -ar verbs), but every -er and -ir form has í: comía, vivíamos.","level":"A2","category":"Lab card","route":"#/lab/preterito-imperfecto"},{"id":"lab:ir-a-infinitivo","group":"lab","title":"ir a + infinitivo","headings":["going to + infinitive"],"summary":"Plans, intentions and things that are clearly about to happen.","text":"Plans Este verano vamos a viajar a Perú. This summer we're going to travel to Peru. Intentions El próximo año voy a estudiar más. Next year I'm going to study more. About to happen (you can see it) ¡Cuidado, te vas a caer! Careful, you're going to fall! Let's… (vamos a) Bueno, vamos a empezar. OK, let's start. mañana esta noche este fin de semana el próximo año la semana que viene pronto Do not leave out a: Voy a comer, not *Voy comer. After a comes the infinitive, never a conjugated verb: Vamos a salir, not *Vamos a salimos. English “I'm going to the movies” has no second verb. In Spanish that is ir a + a place: Voy al cine.","level":"A1","category":"Lab card","route":"#/lab/ir-a-infinitivo"},{"id":"lab:preterito-perfecto","group":"lab","title":"Pretérito perfecto","headings":["Present perfect"],"summary":"The past seen from now: experiences, things done or not done yet and, in Spain, today.","text":"Experiences (no time given) ¿Has estado alguna vez en México? Have you ever been to Mexico? Already and not yet Todavía no he comido. I haven't eaten yet. Never Nunca he viajado en avión. I've never traveled by plane. A time that is not over (Spain) Hoy he trabajado mucho. I've worked a lot today. ya todavía no aún no alguna vez nunca hoy esta mañana esta semana este año With a finished time (ayer, el año pasado, en 2019) use the preterite everywhere: Ayer vi a Marta, never ayer he visto. Nothing goes between haber and the participle: Ya he comido (not he ya comido), No lo he visto. The participle after haber never changes: Ella ha comido, ellos han comido. Spain vs Latin America: in most of Latin America people use the preterite for today and often for ya and todavía no: Hoy desayuné tarde. ¿Ya comiste?","level":"A2","category":"Lab card","route":"#/lab/preterito-perfecto"},{"id":"lab:estar-gerundio","group":"lab","title":"estar + gerundio","headings":["be + -ing (in progress)"],"summary":"An action in progress right now, or a temporary situation these days.","text":"Right now Está hablando por teléfono. She's on the phone. These days Este mes estamos trabajando mucho. We're working a lot this month. Today, unlike usual Normalmente trabajo en casa, pero hoy estoy trabajando en la oficina. I usually work at home, but today I'm working at the office. ahora ahora mismo en este momento estos días hoy Never for future plans: English “I'm leaving tomorrow” is Mañana salgo or Mañana voy a salir. The helper is estar, never ser: Estoy trabajando. Verbs for states usually do not take it: Tengo un coche (not estoy teniendo un coche), Sé la respuesta (not estoy sabiendo). As a noun (“swimming is good”) Spanish uses the infinitive: Nadar es bueno.","level":"A2","category":"Lab card","route":"#/lab/estar-gerundio"},{"id":"lab:futuro-simple","group":"lab","title":"Futuro simple","headings":["Future"],"summary":"Predictions, promises and the more distant future.","text":"Predictions Mañana lloverá en el norte. It will rain in the north tomorrow. Promises Te llamaré esta noche. I'll call you tonight. The more distant future El año que viene viviremos en Valencia. Next year we'll live in Valencia. After si + present Si llueve, nos quedaremos en casa. If it rains, we'll stay at home. mañana pasado mañana la semana que viene el año que viene el próximo año dentro de dos días pronto algún día After si, use the present, not the future: Si llueve, no saldremos (not si lloverá). Every form has a written accent except nosotros: hablaré, but hablaremos. Irregular stems are short: haré (not haceré), tendré (not teneré), diré (not deciré). For an offer right now (“I'll get it!”), Spanish uses the present: ¡Ya voy! Yo te ayudo.","level":"A2","category":"Lab card","route":"#/lab/futuro-simple"},{"id":"lab:imperativo","group":"lab","title":"Imperativo","headings":["Imperative"],"summary":"Instructions, directions, offers and requests: “do this”.","text":"Directions Sigue todo recto y toma la primera a la izquierda. Go straight ahead and take the first left. (Todo recto is Spain; Latin America: sigue derecho.) Instructions and recipes Corta las cebollas y ponlas en la sartén. Cut the onions and put them in the pan. Offers and invitations Pasa, pasa. Siéntate. Come in, come in. Sit down. To a stranger (usted) Siga por esta calle. Keep going along this street. por favor perdone perdona primero después al final de la calle The tú form looks like the present: habla, come. Context and tone show it is an instruction. Ve (go) and ven (come) are easy to mix up: Ve a la tienda. Ven a mi casa. Pronouns are attached, and the stressed syllable often needs an accent: llámame, not me llama or llamame. This card is about “do”; “don't” has other forms and comes later.","level":"A2","category":"Lab card","route":"#/lab/imperativo"},{"id":"lab:ser-estar","group":"lab","title":"ser vs estar","headings":["what someone or something is","where it is and how it is now"],"summary":"Two verbs for “to be”: ser says what something is, estar says where it is and how it is right now.","text":"Identity and jobs Who or what someone is, including their job. Origin Where someone or something comes from. What it is like Lasting characteristics: personality, looks, size, color. Events Where or when an event happens: a party, a class, a concert. Time and dates The time, the day and the date. Location Where a person or a thing is, even when it never moves. (Where an event happens takes ser.) Feelings and states How someone feels or what state something is in at the moment. bien and mal Asking and saying how someone is. *Soy cansado → Estoy cansado. Feelings and states take estar. *Está médico → Es médico. Jobs take ser. *La farmacia es en esta calle → La farmacia está en esta calle. Location takes estar. *Soy 20 años → Tengo 20 años. Age takes tener, not ser or estar.","level":"A1","category":"Contrast card","route":"#/lab/contrast/ser-estar"},{"id":"lab:indefinido-imperfecto","group":"lab","title":"indefinido vs imperfecto","headings":["what happened","the scene and habits"],"summary":"Two simple past tenses: the indefinido tells what happened, the imperfecto describes the scene and habits.","text":"One finished action An event at a definite time. A chain of events Actions one after the other that move the story on. A closed period A period with a clear end, counted as one event. The interruption The new event that cuts into an action in progress. Background What things were like, the weather, the time, how someone felt. Action in progress Something going on, with no end in view (“was doing”). Habits What used to happen again and again. *De niño fui a la playa todos los veranos → De niño iba a la playa todos los veranos. A habit takes the imperfecto. *Cuando llegué, mi madre cocinó (meaning she was in the middle of it) → Cuando llegué, mi madre cocinaba. Cocinó would mean she started cooking after I arrived. *Ayer veía una película muy buena → Ayer vi una película muy buena. One finished action yesterday. *Estaba en Roma tres días → Estuve en Roma tres días. A closed period is an event.","level":"A2","category":"Contrast card","route":"#/lab/contrast/indefinido-imperfecto"},{"id":"lab:perfecto-indefinido","group":"lab","title":"perfecto vs indefinido","headings":["the past seen from now","at a finished time"],"summary":"Two ways to say what happened: the perfecto looks at the past from now, the indefinido places it at a finished time.","text":"Experiences Something in your life up to now; when is not said. Already, not yet With ya, todavía no, aún no. A time that is not over (Spain) Hoy, esta mañana, esta semana, este año. A finished time Ayer, anoche, el año pasado, en 2019, hace dos años: everywhere. Today in Latin America In most of Latin America people also use it for today and often for ya and todavía no. *Ayer he visto a Marta → Ayer vi a Marta. Ayer is a finished time. *He ya comido → Ya he comido. Nothing goes between haber and the participle. *¿Has hacido la cena? → ¿Has hecho la cena? Hacer has the participle hecho. Hoy desayuné tarde is not a mistake: it is the normal Latin American form.","level":"A2","category":"Contrast card","route":"#/lab/contrast/perfecto-indefinido"},{"id":"lab:por-para","group":"lab","title":"para vs por","headings":["the goal","the cause and the way"],"summary":"Two words for English “for”: para looks ahead to a goal, por looks at the cause or the way.","text":"Purpose para + infinitive: in order to. Who gets it The person something is for. Destination Where you are heading. Deadline By a time or a day. Opinion para mí: in my view. Cause, thanks Because of; gracias + what you thank someone for takes por. Through a place Through, along, around. Means By phone, by email. Price What you pay for something. *Gracias para el regalo → Gracias por el regalo. Thanks take por. *Estudio español por viajar → Estudio español para viajar. “In order to” is para. *Compré el libro para diez euros → Compré el libro por diez euros. The price takes por. *Viví en Lima para dos años → Viví en Lima dos años. A length of time needs no para.","level":"A2","category":"Contrast card","route":"#/lab/contrast/por-para"},{"id":"lab:saber-conocer","group":"lab","title":"saber vs conocer","headings":["facts, information, skills","people, places and things you are familiar with"],"summary":"Two verbs for English “to know”: saber for facts and skills, conocer for people and places.","text":"A fact Information you have. + que, si, dónde, cuándo… Information in a clause. + infinitive A skill you learned. A person With personal a. A place A town, a country, a restaurant. Meeting someone In the preterite: met for the first time. *Sé Madrid / *Sé a tu hermano → Conozco Madrid / Conozco a tu hermano. People and places take conocer. *No conozco dónde vive → No sé dónde vive. Information after dónde takes saber. *Conozco nadar → Sé nadar. A skill is saber + infinitive. Yo forms: sé (with an accent) and conozco.","level":"A2","category":"Contrast card","route":"#/lab/contrast/saber-conocer"},{"id":"lab-tree:ser-or-estar","group":"lab","title":"ser or estar?","headings":[],"summary":"Think of the sentence you want to say with “to be”. A few questions lead to the right verb, and sometimes it is neither of the two.","text":"What does “to be” tell us in your sentence? Where a person or a thing is estar Where an event happens: a party, a class, a concert ser Who someone is, their job, or where they are from ser The time, the day or the date ser How old someone is tener What someone is doing right now (“is doing”) estar + gerund What a thing is made of ser What someone or something is like, or how they are  Is it what they are like in general, or how they are at the moment? What they are like in general: personality, looks, size, color ser How they feel or how they are right now: tired, glad, ill, busy estar Just “How are you?” or “I'm fine” estar","level":"A1","category":"Decision tree","route":"#/lab/pick/ser-or-estar"},{"id":"lab-tree:pick-past-tense","group":"lab","title":"Pick the past tense","headings":[],"summary":"You want to say something about the past. A few questions lead to the indefinido, the imperfecto or the perfecto.","text":"Do you say when it happened? Yes, a finished time: ayer, anoche, el año pasado, en 2019, hace dos años  Yes, a time that is not over yet: hoy, esta mañana, esta semana perfecto (Spain) / indefinido (most of Latin America) No, it is about my life up to now: ever, never, already, not yet perfecto (much of Latin America: also indefinido with ya / todavía no) No, it is a description or a habit: as a child, before, always  Within that finished time, is it an event or the scene? An event: something happened, once or a counted number of times indefinido A closed period: three years, all day, two weeks indefinido The scene: the weather, the time, how someone felt, what was going on imperfecto What are you describing? What used to happen again and again imperfecto What someone or something was like, age or the time imperfecto An action in progress when something else happened imperfecto + indefinido","level":"A2","category":"Decision tree","route":"#/lab/pick/pick-past-tense"}]};
